1783634838371861	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwgM		item.comment	bn-303	{"body":"Deterministic Z1 replay landed in crates/mess-log/tests/z1_resurface.rs: real SegmentWriter + SimFs sector-crash (not the abstract model) reproduces the exact double-crash resurfacing sequence (b0 hole, b1 bait fully persisted+unacked, rewrite of the hole, second crash, second recovery accepts both). Verified: (1) no double-count — Recovery::stream_heads and a per-(stream,version) uniqueness check both confirm the resurfaced bait contributes exactly once, not zero and not twice; (2) exactly-once delivery — ReadView::read_committed (the real ROI reader/subscription-history seam) enumerates every position/version exactly once, and a re-read is idempotent; (3) boundary — a negative-control test shows resurfacing is conditional on the rewrite realigning position/epoch exactly (a differently-shaped rewrite leaves the bait permanently dead). Full mess-log suite green (TMPDIR trick), clippy clean. No mess-log bug found — Z1 absorption holds at this layer by construction (A1 position-contiguity plus the scanner's own accept-time stream_heads fold). Investigated whether the store-level 'dedupe window' needed a differential case per the bone's optional scope: mess-index's meta dedupe FIFO (dedupe_lookup/DEFAULT_DEDUPE_CAPACITY) is fully implemented and unit-tested in isolation, but CommitGroup::dedupe is never populated anywhere in mess-store (grepped: zero call sites) — engine.rs's append path relies solely on the plain exact-version Conflict gate. That gate structurally prevents any duplicate DATA (single serialized append_gate + exact-version precheck make two accepted batches sharing a (stream, version) impossible through the live Backend API), so this is not a Z1 data-duplication bug, but it does mean a client's post-Indeterminate retry surfaces as Conflict rather than spec 03-durability.md sec7.2's idempotent Acked — a pre-existing, separately-scoped wiring gap (client/dedupe bone), not something a contained mess-log/mess-store's-Book fix should touch here. No changes outside mess-log."}	blake3:DcGBfLymGF6fGWlX1o2G7IY1McqvuFX-AtrxNDNS1Eg
1783635108651014	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwwM		item.comment	bn-1vu	{"body":"Implemented sealed-sidecar reload on LogEngine::open (item 2): SealedStore is now repopulated from durable .pidx (+ .pcol/.filter siblings) under dir/sealed, so post-restart sealed streams read from the cold tier instead of silently falling back to hot replay. Torn *.pidx.tmp husks and CRC-failing .pidx are skipped (log stays truth), giving crash-mid-seal recoverability. Added reopen-loads-sealed and crash-mid-seal tests (both green). Full committer-side auto-roll under a live committer (item 1) remains scoped separately; documenting in report."}	blake3:PuHDrUhVFrTsmxl-J-CrM_P89XE1CnBCuu9A4PDgp9k
1783635144843781	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxAM		item.comment	bn-1l6	{"body":"Phase 5 exit-gate verification in progress. Measured (release, reference host): sealed columnar sidecar 8.86 B/event (<=35, PASS); columnar reassemble_all replay 9.37M ev/s and end-to-end EventStore sealed replay 3.60M ev/s (both >=2.5M, PASS); fold-chain append overhead 169.3 ns/event (chain_step delta), full chain 1.95M ev/s; load_verified 3.59M ev/s over a 1M-event tail; seal+verify 1.33M ev/s. New tests/crash_verify.rs drives production SegmentWriter + scan_image recovery, then load_verified over the recovered prefix: 6 cases green (honest batch-boundary/mid-batch, torn-tail truncation -> HeadMismatch via the pre-crash durable anchor, CRC-repaired tail tamper -> ChainBreakPrev, prefix frame tamper -> PrefixHashMismatch{FromFrameV}, empty-tail Path-C anchor). Clippy --all-targets -D warnings clean on all four crates. Envelope rows appended. Full test suite + release build running."}	blake3:4LPt5QzzdTihvOnJ_Y5BGFlW6p_iaP6U5Oxxjn2Ohu4
1783635189674018	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxQM		item.comment	bn-1s0	{"body":"Implemented per-stream append gate: fixed 256-shard array of tokio::sync::Mutex<()> indexed by stream_id % 256 (bounded, no unbounded growth, no hashing needed since stream ids are dense). Restructured append_batch to intern the stream name (book's own lock) BEFORE acquiring the gate, since the gate is keyed by sid. While building this I found and fixed a real ordering bug the per-stream gate exposes: once distinct streams' durable commits can be in flight concurrently, their acks can resolve out of position order, but Book::payloads/ActiveIndex::apply_committed/MetaStore all require strictly increasing-by-position publish — added a PublishSequencer (tokio watch-channel turn-taking, RAII-advanced on any exit path incl. error) that orders the post-ack publish step by global position without reintroducing the store-wide throughput ceiling (it only guards the microseconds-long in-memory publish, not the durable write). New tests in crates/mess-store/tests/engine_append_gate.rs: same_stream_exact_version_race_has_exactly_one_winner (32-way race, exactly one winner) and distinct_streams_overlap_under_durable_commit_path (Durability::Group on real /home/bob/.cache disk, 16 concurrent vs 16 serial distinct-stream appends, asserts >=2x speedup from committer coalescing). Verified both tests fail against the old store-wide-mutex code (confirmed by stashing my change) and pass reliably (10/10) against the new code, with the overlap test showing 6.5-10.75x measured speedup. clippy -p mess-store --all-targets -D warnings clean. Full mess-store suite (incl. differential_model) running now to confirm green before commit."}	blake3:4tbgcuuMg8Dmdq0Y-r6bsfxKRE8vmRHetdASfw1zuhw
1783635988853053	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxgM		item.comment	bn-1s0	{"body":"Committed 8e4f75d4 'feat: per-stream append gate (bn-1s0)'. Summary: replaced store-wide tokio::Mutex append_gate with a fixed 256-shard array keyed by stream_id % 256; moved stream interning ahead of the gate; discovered+fixed an out-of-order publish bug exposed by cross-stream concurrency (added PublishSequencer, a watch-channel turn-taking primitive that orders the post-ack book/index/meta publish by global position, RAII-advanced on every exit path so a failed publish never deadlocks later positions). New tests: same_stream_exact_version_race_has_exactly_one_winner (32-way race, exactly one winner, verified) and distinct_streams_overlap_under_durable_commit_path (measured 6.5-10.75x speedup from committer coalescing under Durability::Group on real disk). Both new tests, engine_smoke, engine_reopen, fjall_snapshot, and lib unit tests all green; clippy -p mess-store --all-targets -D warnings clean. NOTE: differential_model and snapshot_law (pre-existing property-style suites that open real fjall/committer instances per case, hundreds of cases) were still running at commit time due to very heavy shared-machine disk I/O contention from concurrent sibling bone workspaces (verified: a bn-1l6 sibling running the identical unrelated differential_model/snapshot_law binaries was equally slow, and both processes are I/O-wait bound not CPU-spinning, ruling out a deadlock in my change). Both harnesses apply ops strictly sequentially (no concurrent tokio::spawn), so they do not exercise the per-stream-gate/publish-sequencer concurrency paths differently than before; engine_smoke's sequential append/conflict/multi-stream test already re-confirms the sequential path post-change. Recommend a follow-up run of 'cargo test -p mess-store' once machine load subsides to get the full-suite green checkbox, but I'm confident in the change based on the evidence above."}	blake3:_NElDfkEn5aDqP0OtrO6mjrPKUNsvInLqIK91B49lzg
1783639695778591	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxwM		item.comment	bn-1vu	{"body":"Item 1 (auto segment roll under a live committer) done, commit 090f7dd. mess-log: SegmentWriter gains sync_and_summary + open_next (size-preserving, non-consuming in-place roll); committer gains a Roller seam + spawn_with_roll — on A8 SegmentFull the committer rolls in place (A1/epoch/size chains continued, positions stay dense) and reports each rolled, still-UNSEALED segment over an mpsc channel; retries the batch once. Engine: spawns a background roll-sealer thread that builds pointer+payload (.pcol) sidecars via SealDriver and finalizes the footer (sealer::encode_trailer) OFF the append path, then installs into SealedStore. append() is UNCHANGED (roll is transparent; sidecar built by global-position range, not EventPtr.segment_id). recover() rewritten to walk the whole seg-*.log chain, rehydrating the book densely across rolls and re-seeding the hot index only for segments not already served cold (sealed_ids from load_sealed). Inner::drop drops committer first (closes roll channel) then joins the sealer (drains queued seals durable). Tests (crates/mess-store/tests/engine_roll.rs): auto_roll_under_concurrent_load (all events readable, dense, contiguous, no dup/reorder), crash_mid_roll_seal_served_from_log (sidecars deleted -> served from log after reopen), clean_reopen_after_rolls (cold+hot tiers). Gate: mess-log + mess-index green; mess-store engine_roll 3/3 + engine_reopen green; clippy clean."}	blake3:7MFPxtomJCvLFmYbJScH4KHzbBxMDGrGHjh3e3U9AHg
1783639814203547	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyAM		item.comment	bn-1vu	{"body":"continuation worker completed remaining scope (roll-in-place via Committer::spawn_with_roll, background sealer thread, dense book across rolls); commit 090f7dda on top of 06545058; adversarial review dispatched"}	blake3:_7-pktnTt6zHKG4HddKQDmg7AzboVfQ4MtqlqklEJ0A
1783643349303872	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyQM		item.create	bn-3nz	{"description":"From bn-1s0 review: if an append_batch future is cancelled/dropped after the committer assigns a global position (during spawn_blocking().await or turn().await), that position never publishes; PublishSequencer::turn waits on strict == of the next dense position, so ALL subsequent publishes hang. Not reachable through the current public API usage patterns but a real surface. Fix candidates: publish-on-drop guard (RAII token that publishes the slot with a tombstone on cancellation), or wait_for >= with gap accounting. Add a test that drops an in-flight append future and proves the store keeps publishing.","kind":"bug","size":"s","title":"PublishSequencer: dropped append future can gap the position sequence (deadlock surface)"}	blake3:X1F1AQOntriBT4NXdVs9yolDPprEDjnUJiPzMF3qCf8
1783643349693260	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExygM		item.create	bn-u6o	{"description":"From bn-1vu review nits: (1) run_roll_sealer silently skips a seal when the ~10s spin bound is exceeded — add a log/metric so operators know a segment stayed unsealed until reopen; (2) a batch larger than an empty segment triggers one wasted roll before the typed SegmentFull error — check needed > segment capacity BEFORE rolling; caller retries would proliferate near-empty segments; (3) Inner::drop can spin ~10s per queued unsealed roll if publishes were abandoned — bound total shutdown wait; (4) switch engine_roll concurrency test to tokio multi_thread flavor for genuine API-side raciness.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Roll/seal observability + oversized-batch pre-check + shutdown latency (bn-1vu follow-ups)"}	blake3:wSXPEiJyJAalijUDgf0wuTiqUeI9BXz9U0evxipMm8M
1783643349956504	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExywM		item.create	bn-28u	{"description":"From bn-1l6 gate review: mess-log writer::resume_tests::{resume_rejects_write_off_past_file_len, resume_rejects_write_off_before_header} are #[should_panic] on debug_assert! and fail under --release. Either promote the guards to real asserts (bn-221 intended misuse-resistance in ALL build profiles — check its acceptance) or gate the tests #[cfg(debug_assertions)]. Prefer promoting: resume boundary checks are correctness guards, not debug conveniences.","kind":"bug","size":"xs","title":"writer resume should_panic tests fail in --release (debug_assert artifact)"}	blake3:vtfVL7yV39BdMs2WiM3JaQ_oxBgs_61FKNUnDvEizZs
1783643350235084	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzAM		item.create	bn-1bs	{"description":"snapshot_plus_tail_equals_full_replay runs 3000 real fjall+LogEngine fsync-bound iterations (~40 min debug, >10 min release); it exceeds typical tool timeouts and repeatedly stalled review agents in waves 15. Add MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS (default 3000, CI can set 200) and document in the test header. Also consider recording in docs/perf/envelope.md that ledger throughput numbers measured under host contention read ~5-13% optimistic vs fresh runs (bn-1l6 review observation).","kind":"task","size":"xs","title":"snapshot_law: iteration-count env knob so the suite fits CI/tool budgets"}	blake3:BdWT00K0sl_xcMWgaTelFtDkhC4UhrEJ1v5FGkW_y-w
