1783704450004009	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExywQ		item.create	bn-3ga	{"description":"Filed bn-1m4d in ~/src/maw (2026-07-10): session-2 field report after building all of mess v1 under maw. 5 items: postsync compile-check hook (4 semantic-rebase incidents), structured-conflict header syntax middle path, merge-path JSON discoverability/completeness, destroy-cwd output ordering, maw ws clean verb. Check #maw for maw-dev's triage; close when items are triaged into bones or declined with rationale.","kind":"task","title":"[tracking] maw bn-1m4d: field report 2 (postsync hook, header syntax, merge JSON, ws clean)"}	blake3:Wr9WrrNZeJsj3Fl12Op-RIq7WIiPd2PiWi_mzj_9gSo
1783705785220298	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzAQ		item.create	bn-1pa	{"description":"docs/perf/experiments-d10.md experiment 2 ended REFUTED-as-measured but confounded: the re-run host had load ~8/24 threads and 22/30 GiB swap in use (our own agent fleet), and the ORIGINAL round-4 harness re-run on the same contended host showed the identical collapse — so neither round 4's 2.3-4.4x nor the confounded ~1.0x is the settled figure for this device.\n\nScope:\n1. Re-run 'TMPDIR=$HOME/.cache/mess-bench-scratch cargo run --release -- bench' from spikes/odsync_probe/ on a QUIET host (load < 1, no swap pressure, no concurrent agents/benches — check uptime + free before starting and record them in the report). Also re-run the round-4 perf_group_commit harness as the cross-check, same session.\n2. Append the numbers + final verdict to docs/perf/experiments-d10.md (supersede the confounded table, keep it for history).\n3. Decision gate (D10): if coalesced O_DSYNC reproduces >= ~2x at group-commit sizes, file the adoption bone — wire spikes/odsync_probe/src/probe.rs (ProbeConfig/run_probe/Capability, incl. the lying-device bandwidth trap) into the committer's durability path behind the double opt-in (probe verdict AND explicit config), with the FUA-semantics caveat documented. If it does not reproduce, mark the D10 ledger entry DECLINED for this device class and close.\n\nNote: do NOT run this while any other workload is active; the measurement is the entire point.","kind":"task","labels":["perf"],"size":"s","title":"D10 follow-up: O_DSYNC quiet-host re-run + adoption decision"}	blake3:hZdo6hp-50iCFEOqXhg5oEpF0VaXwT1r6c1Tg8hXX2o
1783706432308177	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzQQ		item.create	bn-3eh	{"description":"Build examples/social into a real lightweight twitter clone: posting, deleting, following, unfollowing, liking, unliking, timelines, with an actual server-rendered HTML frontend. Two purposes, equally weighted: (1) SHOWCASE the mess v1 DX (store.command(id, cmd).await?, #[derive(Event/Aggregate)], GWT testkit, projections, doctor/verify on a real store); (2) DOGFOOD: every worker must log every API awkwardness, missing capability, unclear error, or doc gap they hit — these become mess bones. Start from clear domain objects and work outward (domain -> read models -> HTTP/HTML -> seed/demo -> rough-edges triage). The existing examples/social (single Post aggregate, moderation demo) is the seed; rework it. Design constraints: keep deps light (axum + one templating choice; no JS framework — plain forms or htmx), no auth (an 'act as user' picker is fine for a demo), everything rebuildable from the log (kill the read models, replay, identical pages).","kind":"goal","size":"xl","title":"social: lightweight twitter clone on mess (showcase + dogfood)"}	blake3:89EcyTCFmXkArnwyzPdHnA1gU8cwmOdQ6sGbAj201Hk
1783706453733051	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzgQ		item.create	bn-154	{"description":"The foundation; everything else builds on this. Rework examples/social/src into modules (domain/user.rs, domain/post.rs, contracts.rs), preserving the existing crate's teaching style (doc comments that explain the mess API as they use it, like examples/bank).\n\nDOMAIN (events + aggregates + commands via mess-core/mess-derive, per-stream invariants enforced in Decide):\n- User aggregate (stream user-<id>): UserEvent { Registered { handle, display_name }, DisplayNameChanged, Followed { target }, Unfollowed { target } }. Commands: RegisterUser (reject double-register, invalid handle), SetDisplayName, Follow (reject self-follow, double-follow, not-registered), Unfollow (reject not-following). The follow SET lives in the follower's own aggregate — document why (single-stream invariant enforcement).\n- Post aggregate (stream post-<id>): PostEvent { Posted { author, body }, Deleted { by }, Liked { user }, Unliked { user } }. Commands: CreatePost (reject empty/oversized body >500 chars, double-create), DeletePost (ONLY the author; reject already-deleted), Like (reject double-like, liking deleted, self-like allowed? DECIDE and document), Unlike (reject not-liked). Keep/absorb the existing moderation events (HiddenByPoster/HiddenByModerator) if it stays coherent — your call, justify.\n- Wire naming/versioning per #[event(name, version)] conventions from examples/bank.\n\nCONTRACTS (contracts.rs — the seam that lets read-models and HTTP build in parallel):\n- DTO structs: PostView { id, author_handle, author_display, body, likes, liked_by_me, created_seq }, ProfileView { handle, display_name, post_count, follower_count, following_count, followed_by_me }, TimelinePage { entries, next_cursor }.\n- trait ReadModels (async or sync — justify): home_timeline(user, cursor, limit), user_posts(handle, cursor, limit), firehose(cursor, limit), profile(handle, viewer), post(id, viewer). Plus a trait WriteOps thin wrapper mapping HTTP actions to store.command calls (or document why HTTP should call the store directly).\n- An in-memory FAKE ReadModels impl for frontend tests.\n\nTESTS: GWT (mess-testkit, like tests/gwt.rs) for EVERY invariant above — each command's accept + every rejection reason. Store-backed roundtrip test (examples/social.rs style) exercising command() against a real EventStore for at least user+post happy paths.\n\nDOGFOOD DUTY: log every rough edge in openQuestions — e.g. cross-aggregate reads in Decide (Follow can't check the target exists — is that fine? document the event-sourcing answer), error ergonomics of typed rejections, derive limitations.\n\nGATES: cargo test -p social (workspace member name — check Cargo.toml), clippy --all-targets -D warnings clean for the crate.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-3eh","size":"m","title":"social: domain model — User + Post aggregates, commands, invariants, read-model contracts"}	blake3:RB1gb46wELDIrwQ2lI8utBqWJcXg6nTBBKDYPjfv0uk
1783706476393266	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzwQ		item.create	bn-2xl	{"description":"Implement the bn-154 ReadModels contract as real projections over the mess store. This bone is the subscriptions/read-path dogfood: it WILL hit rough edges — logging them is half the deliverable.\n\nSCOPE:\n- A projector that consumes the store's event feed (investigate what exists: LogEngine/Backend read_global paging, mess-log subscription.rs D11 catch-up->live runtime — determine what mess-store actually EXPOSES to applications; if there is no store-level subscription/global-read API suitable for an app projection, build the projection on what exists (engine read_global via a small adapter) and FILE THE GAP as a top-priority openQuestion with a proposed store-level API sketch).\n- In-memory read models (HashMaps behind RwLock or similar; no extra DB deps): per-user home timeline (posts by followed users + own posts, newest-first, cursor-paged), user posts, firehose, profile counts (posts/followers/following), like counts + liked_by(viewer). Deleted posts drop out of timelines but keep a tombstone view for direct links (your call, justify).\n- REBUILDABLE: constructor takes the store, replays from position 0 to build state, then follows live (catch-up -> live handoff). A test proves: build models, write more events, rebuild fresh from scratch -> identical query results (the event-sourcing showcase moment).\n- Follow/unfollow retroactivity: DECIDE and document — does following someone show their OLD posts in your home timeline (rebuild says yes naturally) and does the live path match the rebuild path? The rebuild==live equivalence test must cover a follow-after-post case (this is a classic projection bug class).\n- Ordering/consistency: read-your-own-writes for the acting user (after POST /post, the redirect to home must show the new post) — document how (await projector catch-up to the append's position; expose a barrier like projector.wait_for(position)).\n\nTESTS: unit tests per model; the rebuild==live equivalence property test (seeded random event sequences: registers, follows, posts, likes, deletes, unfollows -> both paths -> identical); the wait_for barrier test.\n\nGATES: cargo test for the crate green, clippy -D warnings clean. DOGFOOD DUTY: subscription API gaps, paging ergonomics, position/cursor type confusion, anything that made this harder than it should be.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-3eh","size":"m","title":"social: read models — projections over the store (timelines, profiles, like counts), rebuildable"}	blake3:Cxdd4hlp7lc1wsy2kawbgRsK__riLfMWcR_ybMHEE-E
1783706497703619	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0AQ		item.create	bn-13l	{"description":"The visible face: a small axum server rendering real HTML against the bn-154 contracts (ReadModels trait + WriteOps/store commands). Built and tested against the in-memory FAKE from bn-154 so it can proceed in parallel with bn-2xl; a thin main.rs wires the real store + projections at the end.\n\nSCOPE:\n- Routes: GET / (home timeline for the acting user, paged), GET /firehose, GET /u/:handle (profile + posts + follow/unfollow button), GET /p/:id (single post), POST /post (form: body), POST /p/:id/delete, POST /p/:id/like, POST /p/:id/unlike, POST /u/:handle/follow, POST /u/:handle/unfollow, GET+POST /whoami (the act-as-user picker: a cookie holding the acting user id; register-on-first-use form; NO real auth, say so on the page).\n- HTML: server-rendered, ONE lightweight approach (maud or askama or hand-rolled — justify; keep the dep tree small). Clean minimal CSS (single static stylesheet, no framework). Forms + redirect-after-POST; htmx progressive enhancement optional and only if it stays tiny.\n- Every command rejection (double-like, delete-not-yours, self-follow...) renders as a friendly inline message, NOT a 500 — map the typed domain rejections to user-facing text (this exercises mess's typed-error DX; log friction).\n- Concurrency correctness surfaced honestly: version-conflict retries are the store's job (command() retry policy) — document what the handler does on exhausted retries.\n- Read-your-own-writes: after any POST, redirect must show the effect (use the bn-2xl wait_for barrier via the contracts trait — add it to the trait if bn-154 missed it, coordinating via bone comment).\n\nTESTS: axum handler tests against the FAKE (each route: happy path + one rejection rendering); an HTML smoke test asserting the timeline page contains posted bodies in order. No browser automation needed.\n\nGATES: crate tests green, clippy -D warnings. DOGFOOD DUTY: log every place the store/domain API made the web layer awkward.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-3eh","size":"m","title":"social: HTTP server + HTML frontend (axum, server-rendered, htmx-optional)"}	blake3:rrK1AyrNtsP5HkCvryctfKUm5A7KhBVOnB9fTBgp14M
1783706516351202	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0QQ		item.create	bn-1mw	{"description":"Make the demo lively and self-explanatory. SCOPE: (1) a seed subcommand (or bin) generating a deterministic demo corpus: ~50 users with handles/display names, Zipf-distributed follow graph, ~500 posts with varied bodies/timestamps-in-content, likes (Zipf on posts), some deletes and unfollows — all through real store.command calls (never raw appends), seeded RNG for reproducibility; (2) README.md rewrite for examples/social: what it shows, how to run (seed -> serve -> open browser), the domain model diagram in ASCII, the rebuild-from-log party trick (delete read models, restart, same pages), and an OPS TOUR: run mess doctor / mess inspect / mess verify --full / mess backup+restore against the demo store and paste real output (this is the showcase-of-the-CLI moment; log any CLI awkwardness on a real app store as dogfood); (3) a justfile or scripts entry to run the whole demo in one command. GATES: seed runs green from scratch in <60s, README instructions verified verbatim, clippy clean.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-3eh","size":"s","title":"social: seed corpus, demo script, README + ops-tour"}	blake3:waZfwA_XClsJSltAb4C870UKJyelTe3L3VTiLNt2HGQ
1783706516704493	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0gQ		item.create	bn-j9u	{"description":"The dogfood payoff, owned by the lead after bn-154/bn-2xl/bn-13l/seed land: collect every openQuestion + bone comment logged by the workers (API gaps, DX friction, unclear errors, doc gaps, missing store-level subscription API, etc.), deduplicate, and file each as a properly-scoped mess bone with the concrete social-example incident as evidence. Acceptance: a summary comment on bn-3eh listing every finding -> bone-id (or explicit decline with rationale); at least the store-level projection-API question gets a design-quality bone if bn-2xl confirms the gap.","kind":"task","labels":["social"],"parent":"bn-3eh","size":"s","title":"social: rough-edges triage — consolidate dogfood findings into mess bones"}	blake3:hzONrcwwXy6FOtry3hwJ4hDsuKrGor1DvA8tNxIHcok
