1783794679572306	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpQY		item.comment	bn-28g	{"body":"Spike B COMPLETE — verdict NARROW (commit 80db810e in workspace, do-not-merge). Gates: Process >=85%-of-bare FAIL (best 77.9% b0dth 250Bx100; spec-shaped tokio B0 21-37%; 94% only at 1-writer hot shape) | Group >=95%-of-engine PASS (b0 97-105%, b0d 97-103% medians, identical fsync counts) | p99 <=+10% PASS for B0Direct (better than engine everywhere in Process) | zero spawn_blocking PASS | zero condvar PASS | bounded queue PASS. All 16 correctness tests green incl. differential oracle vs LogEngine and scanner-recovery==shadow-heads. Key findings: (1) flat kernel beats current engine on EVERY cell (Process 1.2-2.5x, up to 3.5x matched-topology); (2) owner must OWN the writer — separate committer thread costs -35% (B0Direct is the design, per design.md 6.3 literal text); (3) B1 pipelining is a durable-mode LOSS (-6% to -18% at 64w) — H2a reconfirmed, drop it; (4) remaining gap to bare is producer-side thread-wake hops + record API, NOT validation (owner ~10% CPU; rr1024==perwriter); (5) owner gather needed the committer's D7 early-close (target width + in-flight gate + 200us grace) or it convoy-splits 2x fsyncs. Recommend re-basing the Process gate (current engine itself is 18-32% of bare at matched 4-writer workloads — C4's 63-84% was a different workload shape) and a follow-up producer-combining probe before freezing design 6.1's dedicated-thread wording. REPORT: spikes/flat_combined_append/REPORT.md"}	blake3:M6OiFOV_kJGIWy66f9XTPWAZqz8NcTSBdaJ0ntPxKoo
1783794760535167	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpgY		item.move	bn-28g	{"state":"done"}	blake3:p_JGpvgEwQIDqvITh2VgXPemQ9gaeTwCEhrW7S_-avs
1783794776975160	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpwY		item.comment	bn-3fn	{"body":"Spikes A+B complete and merged (50c465d9, 558cf11d). A (bn-hzc): direct chunked-page head tables WIN — A4 AtomicU128 cell 46x fjall / 2.6x HashMap under writer, 16.02 B/stream, 33-43M applies/s; keep-HashMap rejected; A2 seqlock is the portable fallback. B (bn-28g): NARROW — the literal 85%-of-bare Process kill gate FAILED (best 77.9%) but the premise behind the gate was wrong: the CURRENT engine measures 18-32% of bare at matched 4-writer workloads (the envelope's 63-84% figures came from unmatched single-stream rows), so the gate priced the async API (tokio wakes, ~69% of cycles producer-side) not the orchestration. Flat kernel beats current engine on every measured point (Process 1.2-3.5x, Group parity 97-105%, p99 better) and passes review-D1's durable gate. Design consequences for the pack: (1) owner must BE the committer — fronting the existing committer thread costs -35%; (2) B1 pipelining is dead, H2a reconfirmed with durable-mode losses; (3) re-base the Spike B/J gate as ratio-vs-current-engine + absolute floor, or fix producer topology first (producer-combining probe / owned-record API are the two remaining measured cost terms). Spike 0 (bn-u1k) should absorb the matched-baseline matrix B already produced. Next per graph: Spike C (bn-2ib, now unblocked)."}	blake3:yR2TbXkNEQRdQEBU_j4zegvErluBV4IvBhNOC9KZbr8
