1783897978375259	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx7gY		item.create	bn-1qa	{"description":"bn-3of (SealPack) added a field to the public SealInput struct and verified with 'cargo test -p mess-index -p mess-store', which is what the bone asked for. Two call sites outside that scope (crates/mess-bench/src/workloads/sealed_pointer.rs, crates/mess-cli/src/rebuild.rs) broke the workspace build on merge; the lead caught it via editor diagnostics post-merge and hotfixed on trunk. Fix: (1) update AGENTS.md / worker-loop guidance so any change to a public type requires 'cargo check --workspace --all-targets' before commit; (2) consider a pre-merge hook in the lead merge workflow that runs it. Cheap, prevents a class of merge breakage.","kind":"task","labels":["process"],"size":"xs","title":"spike/worker verification must run cargo check --workspace --all-targets, not just -p <touched crates>"}	blake3:iVXvDB8-eQqU5Cj1eH4JYAgx1HvVapewjV3_LJJD5qs
1783898255325483	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx7wY		item.comment	bn-2gu	{"body":"Spike J start. Plan: (1) composed_decision harness = baseline_matrix harness + Spike B FlatEngine ported to TODAY's main (one-line API drift: AppendOutcome::Acked gained segment_id/offset). Matrix: bare / current / sealpack / flat-b0d / flat-b0dth over 1/10/100/1000 x 24B/250B x 1,4w x Process/Group, interleaved, quiet-guarded, 3 reps. (2) FlatEngine-vs-main rematch is the headline. (3) carry-forwards: bn-3pz Group convoy split (confirm at 4/16/32/64w + cheap bisect using the frozen baseline_matrix 'point' binary at f0ab89e9^/9d4ec27d^/9f45f273^ worktrees); Spike F stride-8 <=7-header global-seek scan measured against the real v3 segment via scanner::peek_batch_total_len. (4) reopen/RSS 2M+10M current vs sealpack. Note up front: the matrix's segment_size is >=256MB by construction, so NO seal occurs in the append matrix -> sealpack is expected to be a no-op there; it will be measured where it bites (seal/reopen/cold read)."}	blake3:50-1IfvLRS5ngm6WjVPeVuefIgCICMHS-OBJso5sBuM
1783906292916512	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8AY		item.comment	bn-2gu	{"body":"MATRIX DONE (480 runs, 32 cells x 5 engines x 3 reps, ABBA-interleaved, load1 recorded per run: mean 4.26-4.28 for ALL five engines, min 2.6 max 7.3 — the interleave provably gave every engine the same ambient). HEADLINES. (1) FlatEngine REMATCH vs today's main: b0d (B0Direct, tokio producers, borrowed records — the drop-in shape) beats the current engine in EVERY one of the 32 cells. Process 4w vs current: 374%/312% (batch 1), 190%/190% (batch 10), 137%/137% (batch 100), 121%/123% (batch 1000). vs BARE: 64%/59% (b1), 42%/63% (b10), 33%/54% (b100), 107%/108% (b1000). The literal >=85%-of-bare gate still FAILS below batch 1000 (Spike B's finding stands; the gate prices the async API). Group: b0d 94.6-182% of current at 4 writers; the only b0d losses are at 1 WRITER (89.6-96.7%) = the D7 200us grace with no convoy to form (Spike B s7 predicted exactly this; it is a tunable). (2) bn-3pz CONFIRMED AND MISATTRIBUTED — bisect (frozen baseline_matrix 'point' binary rebuilt at each commit, Group 250B x1000 x4w): HEAD log 106/200/190 fsyncs vs bare 100-101; pre-C (43e8bd2a) log 200/200/200; pre-34o (a3d40ff1 = Spike B's OWN gen1 base) log 200/200/200. The convoy split PREDATES bn-34o, bn-2cj AND bn-2ib — it is not a regression, it is a structural property of the composed producer path (producers enter the committer's in-flight gate late, after registry/index/tokio work, so early-close fires on a half-formed convoy). It gets WORSE with concurrency, not better (batch 10: 98.6% of bare at 1w -> 55.4% at 64w; refutes the 'more writers fill the window' hypothesis). b0d hits bare's barrier count EXACTLY at every writer count (50/50 at 64w) — Spike B's rebuilt early-close is the fix. (3) sealpack is a no-op on the append path (96-104% of current; proven, not assumed: the matrix's >=256MiB segments never roll) but costs REOPEN RSS: +18% at 2M (125.2 vs 105.6 MiB) and +36% at 10M (373 vs 275 MiB) — a NEW finding Spike I did not measure. (4) Spike F stride-8 global assumption REFUTED as specified: headers/seek 4.5 (<=8, structural claim holds) but via pread it costs 2.4-2.7us p50 = 10-17x SLOWER than the incumbent in-memory global_range (140-240ns). mmap'd it reaches p50 parity (210-250ns) with a fault-exposed tail. Cold: 75-162us. Recommend stride-1 (16 B/batch, no scan) if the microblock global side is adopted at all. Writing REPORT + decision now."}	blake3:mOCNl13imI2Yz_Gn2TfB5wypcdHeSkEh7yfWit9VWo8
1783909720235987	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8QY		item.create	bn-31n	{"description":"Hit during Spike J (bn-2gu) on a full 'cargo test --workspace --release' at ambient load ~5-7 on the 3900X: crates/mess-store/tests/engine_publish_cancel.rs:254 panicked with 'append must be dropped in flight'. Re-ran the test alone 5/5 green under lighter load. NOT caused by bn-2gu (its only non-spike diff is an env toggle in the owb_bench EXAMPLE).\n\nRoot cause is structural, not a real engine bug: the test does poll_once_then_drop(engine.append_batch(..)) and then ASSERTS the future was still in flight after that single poll. Whether the append completes within its first poll is a scheduling race — under favourable scheduling the whole append resolves on poll 1, the drop is a no-op, and the assertion fires. The property the test really pins (a dropped in-flight append still publishes, and holds the per-stream gate until it does, so a racing same-version retry cannot double-write stream version 1) is correct and worth keeping; only the SETUP is racy.\n\nSuggested fix: make the in-flight-ness deterministic instead of asserted — gate the commit/publish behind a test seam the test releases explicitly, so the append is provably parked when dropped. Failing that, retry the setup (bounded) until poll_once_then_drop reports true, and only then run the assertion body.\n\nSame CLASS as bn-2rk (host-load-sensitive timing assertion in a mess-store test) but a DIFFERENT test — bn-2rk is engine_append_gate::distinct_streams_overlap_under_durable_commit_path (a 2x coalescing-speedup threshold). Filed separately so neither hides the other.","kind":"task","labels":["dx","flaky"],"size":"s","title":"flaky: engine_publish_cancel cancel_then_same_stream_retry_never_double_writes_version — 'append must be dropped in flight' under host load"}	blake3:PiCXjB9bolg-AwaZHNlke53ASobien_MfBZnaru9uwo
1783909954826972	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8gY		item.comment	bn-2gu	{"body":"FINAL DECISION — committed a94e83f4 in the bn-2gu workspace (no push, no merge). REPORT: spikes/composed_decision/REPORT.md.\n\nVERDICT: **PROCEED, NARROWED.** (a) YES, integrate the flat owner — it is the strongest result in the program. (b) NO, do not retire Fjall yet — exactly one thing blocks it. (c) NO to v4/migration now; the v3-compatible subset IS the right stopping point. (d) order below.\n\nPER-COMPONENT: ADOPT B (flat owner, highest priority), A, G, D, H, and F's STREAM side. REJECT F's stride-8 GLOBAL design point (use stride-1). HOLD I at default-OFF. STOP E/v4/M9.\n\nGATE TABLE (per batch size, review S7): (1) append >=85% bare — FAIL below batch 1000 for BOTH engines (b0d 33-64%, engine 17-39%); PASS at 1000 (b0d 107-108%). The gate prices the async API, not the orchestration — re-base it (REPORT s9.1). (2) new-name = one barrier — FAIL for the engine (a second, serialized Fjall SyncAll worth +953us/new-stream at Group/1w that commit.fsync cannot see); PASS for b0d. (3) head/snapshot — CARRIED from A/F, not re-measurable without a composed engine. (4) Book removed + bounded RSS — PASS, and it is the program's biggest realised win: 10M reopen 5.91s->3.76s, peak RSS 1872->268 MiB (14.3% of Book). (5) checkpoint open — CARRIED (D: 4.90%). (6) dedupe — CARRIED (G). (7) replay/storage — PASS. (8) suites — 649 passed / 1 failed: a host-load flake in engine_publish_cancel (asserts an append was still in flight after ONE poll; 5/5 green alone). Filed bn-31n; NOT caused by this spike. Plus: p99 <= engine — PASS everywhere; Group >=95% of engine — PASS at 4w, FAIL at 1w (89.6-96.7%, the D7 200us grace with no convoy to form; bounded + tunable).\n\nFLATENGINE REMATCH (the headline): b0d beats the current engine in ALL 32 cells. Process 4w vs engine: 3.74x/3.12x (b1), 1.90x (b10), 1.37x (b100), 1.21-1.23x (b1000). vs bare: 64%/59%, 42%/63%, 33%/54%, 107%/108%. Group 4w: 94.6-181.8% of engine, matching bare's barrier count exactly. p99 better in every Process cell (29 vs 230us at b1). This is STRONGER than Spike B gen1 (148-238%) — removing the Book did not eat the flat owner's lead, it widened it. Differential oracle vs TODAY's LogEngine green.\n\nCARRY-FORWARDS: bn-3pz CONFIRMED but MISATTRIBUTED — bisect (frozen baseline_matrix probe rebuilt at each commit) shows the ~2x barrier split present at pre-C (43e8bd2a) AND pre-34o (a3d40ff1 = Spike B's OWN base). Not a bn-34o/bn-2cj/bn-2ib regression: a pre-existing composed-producer-path defect that gets WORSE with concurrency (98.6% of bare at 1w -> 55.4% at 64w), refuting 'more writers fill the window'. Spike B's rebuilt D7 early-close fixes it (b0d = bare's barrier count at every writer count) => adopting the owner fixes bn-3pz for free; if it slips, hoist the in-flight gate to the top of LogEngine::append_batch. Spike F stride-8 REFUTED as specified: <=7-header claim HOLDS (mean 4.5) but each header is a pread syscall — 2.4-2.7us p50, 10-17x SLOWER than the incumbent in-memory global_range (140-240ns); mmap gets p50 parity with a fault tail; cold 75-162us. Use stride-1.\n\nNEW FINDING: SealPack costs +18%/+36% REOPEN PEAK RSS (2M/10M) — Spike I measured opens/p99/seal wall but never reopen RSS. It erodes the very gate (bounded RSS) that C won. Fix before seal_pack defaults ON.\n\nWHAT STILL DEPENDS ON FJALL: 6 of 8 keyspaces (stream_heads, snapshot_heads, checkpoints, dedupe, dedupe_order, hw) are derived caches with spike-proven replacements (A/D/G). stream_names + type_names are the durable source of truth for the name<->id bijection and are NOT rebuildable from the log (mess-index/src/meta/mod.rs says so itself). That is the ONLY blocker — and it is also the one UNMEASURED term in the composed projection, because the FlatEngine persists no names at all (its new-name numbers are a FLOOR, not a result).\n\nIMPLEMENTATION ORDER: 1) log-derived $registry (sole Fjall blocker + sole unmeasured cost + what gate 2 needs; acceptance test = REPORT s4.3's new-name table). 2) flat single owner B0Direct — owner OWNS the writer and the barrier; do NOT front the committer (-35%); do NOT build B1 (H2a twice); tune the 1-writer 200us grace. 3) A + G (retires 6 keyspaces). 4) F stream side with stride-1 globals. 5) D. 6) H (+ the free foldhash win). 7) SealPack RSS fix + bn-11g, THEN default it on. 8) v4/M9: not now. DEFERRED with a measured prize: owned-record/interned-type append API + producer combining — worth +30-45 points of bare and the only route to the original 85% gate."}	blake3:3ZMCGuADtKFQVFBiRWfQIMUXoJrY4yAGGwuh2qSGPpc
