1783910017008440	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9AY		item.move	bn-3fn	{"reason":"auto-completed: all children of bn-3fn are done","state":"done"}	blake3:jSvvEZUaBTtaqE5KQWQkeMbavFJ3HLVzOG80_iNOBFg
1783910033481530	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9QY		item.comment	bn-3pz	{"body":"Spike J (bn-2gu) VERDICT: CONFIRMED but MISATTRIBUTED — this is NOT a bn-34o/bn-2cj/bn-2ib regression. A three-commit bisect found the ~2x barrier convoy split already present at pre-C AND at pre-34o (a3d40ff1 — which is Spike B's own base commit). It is a PRE-EXISTING composed-producer-path defect that worsens with concurrency (98.6% of bare at 1 writer -> 55.4% at 64 writers), refuting the 'more writers fill the window' theory. Spike B's rebuilt D7 early-close (target width + in-flight producer gate + 200us grace) fixes it — so ADOPTING THE FLAT OWNER FIXES THIS FOR FREE. Recommend: do not fix separately; close this out as part of the flat-owner integration bone. Retitle/rescope accordingly."}	blake3:f3qvKFpbvAFhBaKpazpM0uIM8L19WK_j8ntFQ6-MlEY
1783910035027030	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9gY		item.create	bn-dbz	{"description":"Found by Spike J (spikes/composed_decision/REPORT.md) — NOT measured by Spike I, which gated on open syscalls, cold p99, and seal wall time but never reopen peak RSS. Enabling EngineOptions::seal_pack costs +18% reopen peak RSS at 2M events and +36% at 10M, eroding exactly the bounded-RSS gate that Spike C (bn-2ib) won by removing the Book. Likely cause: the pack is materialized whole into RAM at open (from_pack takes Vec<u8>) whereas the legacy trio loaded only .pidx eagerly and left .pcol payload blocks to bounded-cache preads. Fix direction: read the pack's directory + small sections eagerly, but leave PAYLOAD_COLUMNS (and other large sections) on the pread/bounded-cache path rather than resident. Must be fixed before seal_pack can default ON (alongside bn-11g footer identity binding).","kind":"bug","labels":["perf","asterism"],"size":"s","title":"SealPack: +18%/+36% reopen peak RSS (2M/10M) — erodes the Spike C bounded-RSS win; blocks seal_pack default-ON"}	blake3:p7Zbz8fGqUc-HByUR9GBXvmqFuGtOTvsn4JVfB0FvDk
1783910035061898	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9wY		item.link	bn-3of	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-11g"}	blake3:iEqDYsKWob2DZJaiNfZwjokobkZPYbp8S5BdJRbW3Z8
1783910051807670	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-AY		item.create	bn-agy	{"description":"Follow-on program to the Asterism spike program (bn-3fn, all 12 spikes complete). Spike J's decision (spikes/composed_decision/REPORT.md): PROCEED NARROWED.\n\nDECISIONS: (a) integrate the flat owner — YES, highest priority (beats current engine in all 32 measured cells, 1.2-3.7x, and fixes bn-3pz convoy-split for free). (b) retire Fjall — NOT YET: 6 of 8 keyspaces are derived caches with proven replacements, but stream_names + type_names are the durable source of truth for the name<->id bijection and are NOT rebuildable from the log. That is the SOLE blocker, and it is also the one unmeasured term in the composed projection (the FlatEngine prototype persists no names at all, so its new-name numbers are a floor, not a result). (c) v4/migration — STOP for now; the v3-compatible subset is the right stopping point. The v4 format is proven admissible (bn-9mw) and sits merged behind an off-by-default flag, ready when a reason to use it appears.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION ORDER (from Spike J): 1) log-derived $registry (unblocks everything; spec docs/spec/04-registry.md + the existing unused RegistryState impl in crates/mess-store/src/registry/); 2) flat owner / B0Direct (owner MUST be the committer — fronting the existing committer costs -35%); 3) dense heads (A) + epoch dedupe (G); 4) microblocks (F) STREAM side only with stride-1 global (stride-8 REFUTED by Spike J: each header is a pread, 2.4-2.7us p50 = 10-17x slower than the incumbent's in-memory seek); 5) segment effects + checkpoints (D); 6) bitrank sealed directory (H); 7) SealPack RSS fix (bn-dbz) + footer identity binding (bn-11g) before seal_pack defaults ON; 8) v4: not now.\n\nKNOWN CEILING: the literal >=85%-of-bare gate fails below batch 1000 for BOTH engines — it prices the async API, not the orchestration. Spike J bounded the remaining prize: dropping the tokio hop + record copy takes the flat owner from 53.8% -> 83.4% of bare. An owned-record/interned-type append API is the next lever after the above.","kind":"goal","title":"Asterism implementation: retire Fjall via a log-derived registry, then land the state kernel"}	blake3:8w21M2_AifFBMnvOYm8Tu4FLEk23zBJ62ev58tfHOIE
1783910075463642	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-QY		item.create	bn-2di	{"description":"THE blocker for retiring Fjall, per Spike J. Today crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs interns names in the Book and persists them to the Fjall stream_names/type_names tables, which are the DURABLE SOURCE OF TRUTH — v3 log frames carry only numeric ids, so deleting those tables makes every event uninterpretable. Everything else in MetaStore (heads, snapshot heads, checkpoints, dedupe, high-water) is a derived cache with a proven replacement from the spike program.\n\nAssets that already exist: (1) docs/spec/04-registry.md is NORMATIVE for the event-sourced registry design; (2) crates/mess-store/src/registry/{mod,state,codec}.rs already implements Registry<B> + a pure RegistryState replay machine with writer-assigned dense ids — fully tested but referenced ONLY by tests, never wired into the engine; (3) the v4 control-prelude capsule (bn-9mw, merged, flag-off) can carry StreamRegisteredV1/EventTypeRegisteredV1 atomically with the first event that uses the id, which is the eventual clean answer.\n\nScope decision to make first: land the registry on v3 (names as $registry system-stream event batches consuming global positions, per research/06 M4's option 1) — which keeps v4 out of the critical path per Spike J's decision (c) — OR gate it on flipping v4 on. Recommend v3 first: it is reversible, needs no format change, and unblocks Fjall retirement without committing to the migration.\n\nMust include: canonical registry import/migration for existing stores (research/06 M4 — export every (id, name) pair from Fjall, verify the bijection and dense-id continuity, prove every id referenced by existing v3 log bytes resolves); reuse RegistryState as the single fold implementation (review V6/S4 — do NOT write a third registry representation, and do not weaken AlreadyRegistered to satisfy effect idempotence); a mess doctor command that rebuilds and diffs the registry against Fjall; and the shadow-compare period before Fjall's name tables are dropped.","kind":"task","labels":["engine","asterism"],"parent":"bn-agy","size":"l","title":"Step 1: log-derived $registry — make stream/type names rebuildable from the log (the sole Fjall blocker)"}	blake3:GEq0rniXF0ByRbotp3OCiVRHqykhXZnM-ZkkJhCStRc
1783910075643578	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-gY		item.create	bn-2su	{"description":"Spike J: adopt, highest priority after the registry. The FlatEngine prototype (spikes/flat_combined_append/) beats today's main engine in ALL 32 measured cells: Process 4w 3.74x/3.12x at batch 1, 1.90x at 10, 1.37x at 100, 1.21-1.23x at 1000; Group 4w 94.6-182% matching bare's barrier count exactly; p99 better in every Process cell; differential oracle vs the real LogEngine green. Removing the Book (bn-2ib) WIDENED the flat owner's lead vs Spike B's gen1 measurement.\n\nCRITICAL DESIGN CONSTRAINT (measured, Spike B): the owner MUST BE the committer — it performs the write and the barrier itself. Fronting the existing committer thread with an owner costs -35%. The committer's D7 early-close (target width + in-flight producer gate + 200us grace) must be rebuilt inside the owner or the group convoy splits into ~2x fsyncs (this is exactly the pre-existing defect bn-3pz, which this integration FIXES FOR FREE).\n\nDo NOT implement B1 (pipelined next-group validation): measured a no-win in Process and a 5-18% LOSS in durable mode at 64 writers, reconfirming perf_group_commit H2a twice. Keeping it out also keeps a nondeterministic barrier cut out of the fault model.\n\nReplaces: the 256-shard AppendGate, both per-append spawn_blocking tasks, the PublishSequencer condvar, and the post-commit Fjall head write. Preserve: cancellation semantics (a committed capsule always publishes), dense global positions, exactly-one-winner on same-stream Exact(v) races, and every crash/torn/SIGKILL harness unchanged.","kind":"task","labels":["engine","asterism"],"parent":"bn-agy","size":"xl","title":"Step 2: integrate the flat-combined owner (B0Direct) — replace gates/spawn_blocking/PublishSequencer"}	blake3:DiBjDWjpdTyMBvg8h6XIiVkwjV2lUxiTPsMx_2xltYc
1783910096212336	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-wY		item.link	bn-2su	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-2di"}	blake3:yhVSaDLra05Q3FqbbAwpKw4aQRTVUw-tEZx7iQV4t20
1783910096239188	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_AY		item.comment	bn-3fn	{"body":"=== ASTERISM SPIKE PROGRAM COMPLETE: 12/12 spikes done, ZERO kill criteria tripped ===\n\nSpike J (bn-2gu) FINAL DECISION: PROCEED, NARROWED.\n(a) Integrate the flat owner: YES — highest priority. FlatEngine beats today's main engine in ALL 32 cells (1.2-3.7x Process, Group at parity-to-182%, p99 better everywhere). Removing the Book WIDENED its lead vs gen1.\n(b) Retire Fjall: NOT YET — stream_names/type_names are the durable name<->id source of truth and are not rebuildable from the log. Sole blocker. 6 of 8 keyspaces have proven replacements.\n(c) v4/migration: STOP for now — the v3-compatible subset is the right stopping point. v4 is proven admissible and merged behind an off-by-default flag, available when a reason appears.\n\nCARRY-FORWARDS RESOLVED: bn-3pz CONFIRMED but MISATTRIBUTED — the Group convoy-split is pre-existing (present at Spike B's own base commit a3d40ff1), worsens with concurrency (98.6% of bare at 1w -> 55.4% at 64w), and is FIXED FOR FREE by adopting the flat owner. Spike F's stride-8 global assumption REFUTED — the <=7-header claim holds (mean 4.5) but each header is a pread (2.4-2.7us p50, 10-17x slower than the incumbent's in-memory seek); use stride-1, adopt F's stream side only.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (unasked-for, both filed): SealPack costs +18%/+36% reopen peak RSS at 2M/10M — Spike I never measured reopen RSS, and this erodes the exact bounded-RSS gate Spike C won (bn-dbz; blocks seal_pack default-ON alongside bn-11g). And Gate 2 fails today: a second serialized Fjall SyncAll worth +953us/new-stream that the commit.fsync counter cannot see.\n\nKNOWN CEILING: the literal >=85%-of-bare gate fails below batch 1000 for BOTH engines — it prices the async API, not the orchestration (Spike B called this correctly). Spike J bounded the prize: dropping the tokio hop + record copy takes the flat owner from 53.8% -> 83.4% of bare. That is the next lever after the implementation program.\n\nImplementation program: bn-agy (goal), steps bn-2di (log-derived registry) -> bn-2su (flat owner) -> A/G -> F(stream, stride-1) -> D -> H -> SealPack fixes -> v4 not now."}	blake3:SMd9rfwuOpkiDQtxwQUvYqWvsEg6ZjX25hXaC7UG9Co
1783956211254549	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_QY		item.move	bn-2di	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:GKzS3a787xKDy8DoK-MJHItsiUzVXTlvlbaQcHgqJVA
1783956211261507	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_gY		item.assign	bn-2di	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-dev"}	blake3:-Fb5pfO-rjInLyDWP2XWSvwd5Jkj_jXvSZzmSwyGLAg
1783956697154011	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx_wY		item.comment	bn-2di	{"body":"START. Design settled after reading spec 04, registry/{codec,state}.rs, engine.rs append/recover, committer.rs.\n\nPLAN (v3, per lead's call):\n1. mess-log: add `Appender::append_ordered(Vec<AppendRequest>)` — sends N CommitReqs back-to-back into the gather channel under ONE gate.enter/leave, awaits N acks. Channel FIFO => file order; under Group, gather's try_recv drain puts them in ONE group => ONE fsync covers registration + first use. This is what kills the phantom second SyncAll.\n2. $registry = stream_id 0, event_type_id 0, codec_id 0 (spec REG1/REG5). Reuse RegistryState + codec verbatim as the single fold. Book gets reserved id 0 both namespaces.\n3. append_batch: new stream/type names -> RegistryRecord batch on stream 0, submitted immediately BEFORE the domain batch via append_ordered.\n4. recover: fold $registry from the log (targeted pread of stream-0 batches only, located via the scan's accepted list + sealed sidecar stream index — no full payload scan, Spike C's header-only open preserved). Legacy stores ($registry empty + Fjall names present) keep opening off Fjall until migrated.\n5. read_global must not leak system events to users: filter stream 0, add a frontier-reporting page method so the subscription cursor still makes progress (its dense-run assert is load-bearing).\n6. `mess migrate registry` + doctor diff check. Shadow Fjall name writes stay ON by default."}	blake3:F5yMVXpOXPtTsafBqXWDq7X0Lsiaq7-Ch35eQyZbZps
1783959023464804	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExgAc		item.comment	bn-2di	{"body":"REGISTRY-IN-LOG GREEN (commit 85b816c1).\n\n- mess-log `Appender::submit_ordered`: N batches pushed back-to-back under one gate span. Synchronous by design so the engine can push while holding the Book lock.\n- THE ORDERING INSIGHT: the $registry batch is pushed under the SAME Book mutex that publishes the minted id. Any thread that can *see* the id necessarily took the lock after it was released, hence after the registration was already in the committer's FIFO. Recovery accepts a contiguous prefix => no crash can keep a reference and lose its registration. Ordering is structural; no barrier needed. (Without this, two concurrent appends on different streams race: B mints type 7, A sees it interned, A's batch beats B's registration into the file, crash truncates between => unrecoverable store. Found it while writing the append path.)\n- Consequence: the name barrier is GONE. engine_name_durability now asserts ZERO meta flushes (persist AND persist_buffered) for a new name in Process/Os/Group. bn-150 + bn-2cj + bn-34o + Spike J's phantom ~953us SyncAll all collapse to nothing.\n- recover implements spec 04 §7.1 properly: scan defers ALL name resolution -> fold $registry -> resolve. Sealed segments are NOT rescanned (stream-0 pointer index gives the batches directly; targeted pread each). Spike C header-only open preserved.\n- $registry consumes global positions but is never delivered: read_global skips stream 0; new read_global_page reports the scan frontier so subscription cursors advance across the hole (the dense-run assert was load-bearing).\n- differential_model green on the engine after teaching the oracle to mirror the interner (registrations consume positions -> last_global shifts; deterministic, so the model just counts them).\n\nNEXT: migration traps + full suite + perf."}	blake3:gOrK5_aLPjKrUKhUd9FDY68R_nurGZWi6FdWCXvehac
1783963678758066	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExgQc		item.comment	bn-2di	{"body":"SCOPE CUT (Bob, 2026-07-13): nobody is using mess — early development, NO existing stores. All migration work cancelled: no migrate command, no Fjall name-table export/verification, no research/06 s16 data-loss traps, no shadow period. Instead: the log-derived $registry is the ONLY registry from a store's first event; stop writing Fjall stream_names/type_names entirely (delete the keyspaces, not flag them off); delete persist_new_names/name-SyncAll outright so Spike J's phantom +953us/new-stream fsync becomes structurally impossible; prove a store works with Fjall name tables NEVER created. Correctness bar unchanged (crash durability, full suite, workspace-wide check)."}	blake3:mOisz9hwJDZVWnzjrhg6pwjS9LKxI2I-KhhSGS5W-i4
1783963678820864	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExggc		item.comment	bn-agy	{"body":"SCOPE CUT (Bob, 2026-07-13): no existing stores / early development. The entire migration program (research/06 M0-M9, the phased cutover, shadow compares, rollback matrix, mess migrate commands) is MOOT. Fjall retirement is now a straight deletion, not a migration: land the log-derived registry (bn-2di), then delete the name keyspaces, then replace the remaining 6 derived-cache keyspaces with the spike-proven structures (dense heads A, epoch dedupe G, snapshot slots) and drop the Fjall dependency. This also removes most of the risk that motivated Spike J's 'hold v4' caution — v4 can be reconsidered on its own merits later (it is merged, proven, flag-off) rather than being gated on migration danger."}	blake3:ENgKAm_5672WDtC66UC1SPrzyKozaesB-iV2PfIsIcM
1783963679237773	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExgwc		item.create	bn-3m3	{"description":"Bob confirmed 2026-07-13 that mess has no users and no existing stores, so research/06-migration-plan.md (M0-M9 phased cutover, shadow compares, dedupe/snapshot import, rollback matrix, mess migrate CLI) describes work that will never be done. Add a header note marking it superseded/moot, and cross-reference from design.md s20 and the risk register (R3 'current authoritative name mappings lost during migration' drops out entirely; R2/R6 shrink). Keep the document — its analysis of WHY names were the sole non-rebuildable authority is the reasoning that produced bn-2di, and the trap list is good design commentary even if never executed.","kind":"bug","labels":["docs","asterism"],"size":"xs","title":"notes/mess-asterism: research/06 migration plan is moot (no existing stores) — annotate, don't delete"}	blake3:xDQ6Y1bs2OlXF48AHj8LuRH0pGAN3Y09GItIL3bkA7A
1783973488285347	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhAc		item.create	bn-2nd	{"description":"A load-sensitive perf-envelope assertion in crates/mess-log/tests/fold_chain_overhead.rs:70 that fails whenever the full workspace suite runs it alongside other crates' tests, and passes cleanly in isolation (verified 3/3 at 0.26s on a quiet machine, well inside envelope). Now observed in three separate programs of work (Spike E bn-9mw, Spike J bn-2gu, and bn-2di), each time costing a reviewer time to prove it is not a regression — it is actively eroding the signal value of a full-suite run. Fix: either gate the timing assertion behind a 'quiet machine' env/feature (like snapshot_law's MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS convention), widen the envelope to be robust under load, or convert it to a benchmark with a floor in mess-bench rather than a test. Related: bn-31n (engine_publish_cancel, same class).","kind":"bug","labels":["test"],"size":"xs","title":"flaky: fold_chain_overhead::chain_append_overhead_is_in_envelope fails under parallel test load (3rd occurrence)"}	blake3:qOkAA2kRDnSxb7mVEKkRv7RvKWVyqSPDN6vG7I0WlEI
1783974014590814	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhQc		item.comment	bn-2di	{"body":"ADVERSARIAL REVIEW: **REJECT** — two permanent-corruption paths reproduced from the PUBLIC API with no crash, no disk-full, no fault injection. The bone's headline claim ('the ordering is structural, and structure needs no fsync') is FALSE as implemented: the registration is ordered ahead of first use only if it actually reaches the committer, and there is no rollback when it doesn't — while the id has already been published to every other thread via the Book.\n\nF1 CRITICAL (repro'd): Book::intern_stream/intern_type mint ids + alloc_registry_versions bumps stream-0 INSIDE the Book lock, then submit_ordered(reqs)? propagates any pre-flight AppendError straight out with NO rollback. Trigger: append a >64MiB batch to a new stream -> EncodeError::BatchTooLarge (an ordinary documented user error). Ids are now minted; log is empty. Next append to that stream sees sid_new==false, emits ZERO $registry records, commits domain events referencing ids that exist nowhere. Reopen: 'no interned name for stream_id 1' -> store UNOPENABLE FOREVER with an acked event in it. Same program on main: REOPEN OK.\n\nF2 CRITICAL: cargo test -p mess-store --test registry = 10 passed / 12 FAILED (22/22 on main). The new append_batch guard rejects $registry as a system stream, but TestBackend=Tmp<LogEngine> means spec-04's Registry<B> writer path drives the real engine — so register_category/register_stream/register_event_type/register_dict/alias_* are all now non-functional. RegistryState is still the single FOLD, but the WRITER is forked: the engine emits registry records by bypassing the Backend seam. tests/registry.rs was never run.\n\nF3 HIGH (repro'd): finish_recovery's REG21 check validates referenced_streams but NOT event_type_ids (they live in subframe headers the metadata-only scan skips). Same trigger with an existing stream + new type: store OPENS CLEANLY, then read_stream fails 'no interned name for event_type_id 2' — one dangling type id makes the entire stream, including its valid events, permanently unreadable, with no warning at open. Arguably worse than F1's loud refusal.\n\nF4 HIGH (traced): commit_group writes each CommitReq independently — submit_ordered gives file ORDER, not ATOMIC FAILURE. Registry batch can be rejected (SegmentFull->StoreFull) while the domain batch in the same group is accepted and published. F1's brick reached via disk-full instead of user error.\n\nF5 MEDIUM: engine.rs:3495 publish_batch(...)? for the registry batch returns early WITHOUT publishing the already-Acked domain batch at higher positions — reintroduces the exact bn-3nz committed-but-never-published stall that commit 57dc5f5e exists to fix, one level up.\n\nF6 LOW: stale module doc still documents deleted fjall name persistence + broken intra-doc links to the deleted persist_new_names.\n\nWHAT HELD (attacked, survived): Spike C intact — sealed opens stay header-only, $registry batches located via the sealed per-stream pointer index (one pread per registration batch, zero when a segment has no stream-0 batch), no full payload scan reintroduced. Segment roll between the two batches is SAFE (roll_segment fsyncs the old segment before opening the new one, even under Process). Crash/torn seams genuinely safe — the contiguous-valid-prefix rule does make 'keep the reference, lose the registration' impossible FOR CRASHES; the hole is partial COMMIT failure, not crash. Fjall name path really gone (zero production callers of MetaStore::persist — phantom 953us fsync structurally unreachable). $registry containment correct (stream 0 filtered from read_global hot+sealed tiers). AlreadyRegistered strictness intact. Hot path clean."}	blake3:o3W-ivr59_KE4SX3oVMk71ixZOqSHka_dkaD-qiFOnA
1783980067185704	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhgc		item.comment	bn-2di	{"body":"REVIEW FIXES LANDED (workspace commit 08d624f7) — F1-F6 all addressed, plus four suites the review missed.\n\nROOT CAUSE, fixed at the root: no id may exist before its $registry record can.\n\nF1 (CRITICAL) — STAGED-COMMIT, not rollback. append_batch's Book-locked section now (1) resolves-or-PLANS every id in locals (hwm+1, the same allocation rule Registry<B> uses), (2) pushes the ordered unit, (3) mints only after submit_ordered returned Ok. Every pre-commit failure (BatchTooLarge on >64MiB, Closed, StorePoisoned) leaves the Book byte-for-byte untouched, so there is nothing to undo — the bad state is unrepresentable, not merely repaired. Staging over manual undo, as the bone preferred: it needs no second interner (the fold IS the interner now) and it strengthens the ordering claim rather than weakening it — the mint still happens under the lock that pushed, so a thread that can SEE an id necessarily pushed after the registration. Bonus: a CONFLICTING or EMPTY append now mints nothing at all (it used to burn an id + a global position on a registration nobody referenced).\n\nF4 (HIGH) — CommitReq carries a UnitTag{id,last}; the first non-Acked outcome in a unit makes commit_group SKIP every later batch of it (unwritten, no position consumed) with the new AppendError::UnitAborted. failed_units lives in committer_loop, because under Process/Os a unit spans ADJACENT GROUPS, not one. Regression-tested two ways from the DURABLE BYTES (recovery scanner, not the acks): SegmentFull, and an injected ENOSPC at the roll's preallocation — the reviewer's exact disk-full trace, and it did reproduce.\n\n+ the cross-unit hole the review did not name: a rejected $registry batch could still be overtaken by ANOTHER THREAD's batch referencing the id (that thread saw the id in the Book and pushed behind the registration; a full store can reject the tiny registration and accept a smaller batch behind it). Closed by a pending-registration PARK: an appender that would REFERENCE an id whose registration is still in flight waits for its outcome instead of pushing. Costs one is_empty() check on the hot path (both maps are empty unless a brand-new name is in flight right now); the minting appender never waits on itself; same-stream contenders are already serialised by the append gate. If a pushed registration does NOT land, the Book is poisoned (registry_lost) and every further append is refused — reopen (which rebuilds the registry from the log alone, consistent by construction) is the only exit.\n\nF5 (MEDIUM) — the $registry publish's error is now REMEMBERED, not returned: the already-Acked domain batch at higher positions is still published, then the error surfaces. The bn-3nz stall cannot come back through that seam.\n\nF3 (HIGH) — the open-time check now covers event_type_ids. Type ids come from the 28-byte SUBFRAME HEADERS of the batches the open already reads: recover_segment read the whole segment image for its CRC anyway and just dropped the buffer, so keeping it costs ZERO extra I/O and decodes ZERO payloads (the recover_payload_decodes()==0 gates still pass). Sidecar-trusted sealed segments are still not read at all — Spike C intact. Two things make that COMPLETE rather than partial: (a) ids are dense (Book::from_registry proves it), so a single max decides the whole namespace; (b) a sealed segment cannot carry a dangling id — its registrations sit at LOWER global positions, inside the accepted contiguous prefix, by construction. NB: a 'header-only walk' of a sidecar-skipped sealed segment is not physically possible (subframe headers are interleaved with payloads — you would have to pread the whole segment), so reading them WOULD have been the Spike C regression the bone forbids.\n\nF2 (CRITICAL) — route (a) in substance: RegistryState is now the single WRITER as well as the single fold. The Book no longer has its own name maps at all — it holds the folded RegistryState plus an Arc<str> projection for the read path. $registry is not write-LOCKED, it is TYPE-CHECKED: append_batch('$registry', ..) takes only RegistryEventV1 records that decode as codec_id 0 and fold cleanly (REG13: validated against a scratch clone, so a rule-violating record NEVER reaches the append-only log), and it mints through the same hwm+1 allocator the engine uses. Engine mints and Registry<B> writes are one code path, one fold, one set of REG-rules; AlreadyRegistered untouched. tests/registry.rs 22/22 (was 10/12). Known limit (spec D9, single writer): mixing a live Registry<LogEngine> writer with engine mints staleness its cached head — inherent to D9, documented.\n\nF6 — module doc rewritten (the fjall name persistence prose and both broken intra-doc links are gone).\n\nWHAT THE REVIEW MISSED — four suites were ALREADY RED on the workspace tip (verified by stashing the diff and running them at 57dc5f5e): engine_smoke::append_read_head_conflict, subscription (4), fjall_snapshot::subscribe_over_fjall_snapshot_backend_sees_live_commits, engine_seal_pack (4). One cause: their oracles assume 'global position == index of the Nth user event', which stopped being true the moment $registry records started consuming positions. Fixed — they now take positions from the acks or count the registrations explicitly. differential_model's oracle also had to stop registering a stream on the conflict/empty path (F1 changed that behaviour).\n\nBAR: tests/registry.rs 22/22. New suites: mess-store/tests/engine_registry_integrity.rs (5 — the reviewer's exact F1 repro end-to-end + reopen + readback; F3's dangling-type-id open refusal built from a hand-written log, since the engine can no longer PRODUCE one; plus a fully-registered control) and mess-log/tests/ordered_unit.rs (4). Full nextest workspace green, cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean, just fmt. Re-verified what the review said held: sealed opens still header-only (zero payload decodes), $registry still filtered from read_global in hot+sealed tiers, MetaStore::persist still has zero production callers, hot-path appends still emit zero $registry records, and new-stream append latency is still at the hot floor (Process p50 27.2us vs 21.3us hot floor; Group 12.2ms vs 11.8ms).\n\nNOT pushed, NOT merged — back to adversarial review."}	blake3:q1xLadQUtSGloEvDtmvrkEByi7HpM8ZAzSrizaVDjUY
1783981795476875	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhwc		item.move	bn-2di	{"state":"done"}	blake3:or_Qdf2IoHcBr6LqpR3bE6FJnQassiov-ZrKmTxxtPE
1783981816209916	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiAc		item.comment	bn-agy	{"body":"STEP 1 COMPLETE — merged at 05af6861. **Fjall now holds NOTHING authoritative.** Stream/type names live in the log as $registry (stream 0) batches, folded through spec-04's existing RegistryState on recovery; Fjall's stream_names/type_names keyspaces, persist_new_names, and the name barrier are DELETED. MetaStore::persist has zero callers on the append path — Spike J's phantom ~953us/new-stream SyncAll is now structurally unreachable, not merely unused. Under Group durability a new name costs ZERO extra barriers (registration + first-use event land in the same commit group; one fdatasync covers both).\n\nEvery remaining Fjall keyspace — stream_heads, snapshot_heads, checkpoints, dedupe, dedupe_order, hw — is a DERIVED CACHE with a spike-proven replacement. Deleting Fjall is now mechanical, not dangerous.\n\nAdversarial review earned its keep: the first pass REJECTED with reproduced PERMANENT STORE CORRUPTION from the public API (no crash, no fault injection) — an oversized (>64MiB) batch to a new stream minted ids into the Book, failed, and never rolled back; the next append saw them as registered, emitted no $registry record, and committed events referencing ids that existed nowhere. Store unopenable forever with an acked event in it. Plus a silent variant (dangling event_type_id -> store opens clean, then the whole stream is unreadable), 12/22 spec-04 registry tests broken by the new system-stream guard, non-atomic ordered-unit failure in commit_group, and a stranded-domain-batch publish path.\n\nThe redesign is STRUCTURAL: ids are STAGED, not minted-then-undone — plan_and_submit resolves-or-plans ids into locals and commits them to the Book only after submit_ordered has put the record irrevocably in the FIFO. An id cannot exist before its $registry record can. Ordered units now fail atomically in the committer (UnitTag/failed_units, proven by ENOSPC fault injection from the durable bytes). The open-time check now covers event_type_ids too — via subframe HEADERS only, so Spike C's header-only sealed open is intact (verified: zero new I/O). Registry<B> routes through the Backend seam again, so RegistryState is the single WRITER as well as the single FOLD. registry 22/22.\n\nNEXT: Step 2 — flat-combined owner (bn-2su)."}	blake3:9hZuB0jJfOweHwzG_tDRbnD-4ca_XLxHG2-97AMU6PA
1783981816244343	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiQc		item.move	bn-2su	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:BxXZC80KpnoBE50Ugsuljfl48zb0ee_ggykAl8llUc8
1783981816254868	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExigc		item.assign	bn-2su	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-dev"}	blake3:6gw_E-ujSonHqu3ahal6Ia0HOB1kITrke1lEQ2v1Tpo
1783981930178930	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiwc		item.create	bn-25c	{"description":"Consequence of bn-2di (log-derived registry on v3). $registry (stream 0) batches are real v3 event batches, so they CONSUME global positions. The engine filters stream 0 out of read_global in both hot and sealed tiers, so users never SEE registry events — but the global_position values on the user events they do see now have GAPS where registrations landed. Global positions are no longer dense over user events.\n\nThis was an explicit, anticipated design choice, not an accident: research/06 M4 offered it as option 1, and design.md s21.7 says 'keep registry as explicit $registry event batches and accept the extra global positions rather than weakening recovery.' The alternative — v4 control-prelude capsules, which by design do NOT consume domain positions (design s5.2) — is merged and proven (bn-9mw) but held per Spike J.\n\nWhy it matters: it already broke four test suites whose oracles assumed 'global position == index of the Nth user event' (engine_smoke, subscription, fjall_snapshot, engine_seal_pack — all fixed in bn-2di). Any external consumer, cursor, or projection that assumes a dense user-event position sequence will break the same way.\n\nDo: (1) document this loudly in docs/spec (01-log-format and/or a registry section) and in the engine's public API docs for read_global/subscriptions/cursors — state plainly that global positions are dense over ALL log events, not over user events; (2) note it in the Asterism design pack as the accepted cost of the v3 path; (3) record it as the standing argument for eventually turning v4 on (control-only capsules restore dense domain positions — this is precisely what design s5.2 was designed for).","kind":"task","labels":["docs","asterism"],"size":"s","title":"document: $registry records consume global positions — user-visible position sequence now has gaps"}	blake3:xYQdBhSvuuUVMmOCXMqrwDySYMQI5Im8n3MF8elc0Sk
1783981930620617	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjAc		item.comment	bn-agy	{"body":"STEP 1 POSTSCRIPT — three things from the fix worker's final report worth carrying forward:\n\n(1) GLOBAL POSITIONS ARE NO LONGER DENSE OVER USER EVENTS. $registry batches are real v3 event batches and consume global positions; the engine filters stream 0 out of read_global (both tiers) so users never see them, but user events' global_position values now have GAPS. This is the explicitly-anticipated cost of the v3 registry path (research/06 M4 option 1; design s21.7 'accept the extra global positions rather than weakening recovery'). It already broke four suites whose oracles assumed position==Nth-user-event. Filed for documentation. It is also the standing argument for eventually enabling v4 — control-only capsules do NOT consume domain positions by design (s5.2), which is exactly the problem they were invented to solve.\n\n(2) The fix worker closed a CROSS-UNIT hole the reviewer never named: another thread could see a minted id and push a batch behind a registration that a full store then rejected (small batch fits the tail, tiny registration doesn't). Closed with a pending-registration park — an appender that would reference an in-flight id waits for its outcome (one is_empty() check on the hot path; the minter never waits on itself). A pushed registration that does not land poisons the Book (registry_lost); reopen is the only exit.\n\n(3) Known limitation (inherent to spec D9, documented): mixing a live Registry<LogEngine> writer with engine-side mints staleness the writer's cached head; the engine surfaces it as a Conflict, which Registry::append turns into its D9 panic. Acceptable — the engine is the writer in production."}	blake3:muCDitDmXVmiX0P1DUGbMFJkrQB6fHerluREm4lWpTo
1783982378020393	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjQc		item.comment	bn-2su	{"body":"NOT STARTED — worker died on a usage limit before writing any code. Workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-2su exists and is EMPTY/clean (reuse or recreate; nothing lost). Bone left in 'doing' with claim staked by mess-dev; next lead should re-stake or reset. Full brief + hard constraints are in handoff.md section 2."}	blake3:NDfDGYgOnf8nFYGyKHMUhW2STkrl9R07gPG_O_7DgF8
1783982378044008	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjgc		item.comment	bn-agy	{"body":"LEAD HANDOFF 2026-07-13: handoff.md written at repo root for the next lead agent. State: Step 1 (bn-2di, log-derived registry) MERGED at 05af6861 — Fjall holds nothing authoritative. Step 2 (bn-2su, flat owner) NOT STARTED (worker died on usage limit; empty workspace). All 12 spikes complete and merged. handoff.md covers: the next action + hard constraints for Step 2, the mandatory adversarial-review rule and what it caught, Spike J's decision and implementation order, the design-pack amendments the spikes produced, the no-existing-stores scope change (migration is moot; v4 is newly worth reconsidering because $registry consumes global positions), all open bones triaged, and operational notes (workers die on usage limits — always inspect the workspace before re-spawning; bench hygiene; .bones merge conflicts; three flaky tests to ignore)."}	blake3:LR8m-jhE7d8o10pcpcOioSbnwYJkG6pH9yu4J9yVN1M
