1783992716982367	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmwc		item.move	bn-agy	{"reason":"auto-completed: all children of bn-agy are done","state":"done"}	blake3:W-winjG2q_s5lHj85s1mpMR5Wi0vwok9R6BasECxICE
1784031389513239	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnAc		item.create	bn-o5b	{"description":"# Context & Background\\nThe original implementation goal bn-agy completed the log-derived  (bn-2di) and flat owner (bn-2su), then auto-closed because those were its only children. Its own accepted roadmap still has substantial work: finish the owner hot path, install dense resident state and exact dedupe, integrate active microblocks, land SegmentEffect/checkpoints, finish and default SealPack, remove Fjall, and make a deliberate v4 decision. The current engine has evolved beyond parts of the original design pack, so this successor goal starts with a code-to-roadmap authority audit rather than blindly implementing obsolete migration or dormant-MetaStore assumptions.\\n\\n# Outcome\\nMess has one log-authoritative storage engine whose resident and sealed accelerators are rebuildable, bounded, and measured end to end. No production authority or required runtime path depends on Fjall. Every admitted Asterism component is integrated through LogEngine, recovery, sealing, observability, and fault tests—not left as a spike.\\n\\n# Constraints\\n- Preserve one canonical writer/order/durability spine and publish only after the mode's accepted write/barrier point.\\n- Keep B1 barrier pipelining, log striping, and stride-8 global pointers rejected unless wholly new evidence overturns their negative spikes.\\n- Exactness is mandatory: no probabilistic dedupe answers, hidden metadata authority, or checkpoint-dependent correctness.\\n- There are no existing stores to migrate; research/06 M0-M9 is historical analysis, not implementation scope.\\n- v4 is a gated product decision, not assumed. If declined, the v3 system-record design must still make remaining state log-derived.\\n- Use matched, batch-stratified composed benchmarks and committed raw evidence; isolated microbench wins do not waive end-to-end gates.\\n\\n# Success Metrics\\n- Flat-owner append refinements retain Fjall-era wins and improve the measured residual API/direct-path costs.\\n- Dense heads, microblocks, effects, checkpoints, and SealPack pass their spike-derived correctness, memory, startup, and throughput gates after production integration.\\n- Snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe authority is represented canonically in the log or explicitly removed as dormant API.\\n- The fjall dependency and obsolete MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend production surface are deleted after replacements land.\\n- A final composed fresh-store matrix, recovery/RSS corpus, crash/fault suite, and adversarial review either admit the completed architecture or invoke predeclared kill/narrow criteria.\\n\\n# Primary Evidence\\n- notes/mess-asterism/design.md §§6-18, 23\\n- notes/mess-asterism/research/01-10 plus the code review/response in research/11-12\\n- spikes/composed_decision/REPORT.md §11\\n- spikes/state_kernel_dense_heads, active_microblocks, segment_effect, epoch_dedupe, directory_tournament, and seal_pack reports\\n- spikes/baseline_matrix/BN-2SU-REPORT.md and BN-2SU-FINAL.csv\\n\\n# Stop Criteria\\nStop or narrow any custom structure that misses its composed gate, increases correctness surface without an end-to-end win, or causes the formal/crash suites to be bypassed.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"size":"xl","title":"Complete the post-flat-owner Asterism storage-engine roadmap"}	blake3:VnpmUr1mdOiJZkAD49-I57lmcJ1SHxidRoE9HzB3Sjw
1784031419265547	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnQc		item.update	bn-o5b	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\\nThe original implementation goal bn-agy completed the log-derived registry (bn-2di) and flat owner (bn-2su), then auto-closed because those were its only children. Its own accepted roadmap still has substantial work: finish the owner hot path, install dense resident state and exact dedupe, integrate active microblocks, land SegmentEffect/checkpoints, finish and default SealPack, remove Fjall, and make a deliberate v4 decision. The current engine has evolved beyond parts of the original design pack, so this successor goal starts with a code-to-roadmap authority audit rather than blindly implementing obsolete migration or dormant-MetaStore assumptions.\\n\\n# Outcome\\nMess has one log-authoritative storage engine whose resident and sealed accelerators are rebuildable, bounded, and measured end to end. No production authority or required runtime path depends on Fjall. Every admitted Asterism component is integrated through LogEngine, recovery, sealing, observability, and fault tests—not left as a spike.\\n\\n# Constraints\\n- Preserve one canonical writer/order/durability spine and publish only after the mode's accepted write/barrier point.\\n- Keep B1 barrier pipelining, log striping, and stride-8 global pointers rejected unless wholly new evidence overturns their negative spikes.\\n- Exactness is mandatory: no probabilistic dedupe answers, hidden metadata authority, or checkpoint-dependent correctness.\\n- There are no existing stores to migrate; research/06 M0-M9 is historical analysis, not implementation scope.\\n- v4 is a gated product decision, not assumed. If declined, the v3 system-record design must still make remaining state log-derived.\\n- Use matched, batch-stratified composed benchmarks and committed raw evidence; isolated microbench wins do not waive end-to-end gates.\\n\\n# Success Metrics\\n- Flat-owner append refinements retain Fjall-era wins and improve the measured residual API/direct-path costs.\\n- Dense heads, microblocks, effects, checkpoints, and SealPack pass their spike-derived correctness, memory, startup, and throughput gates after production integration.\\n- Snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe authority is represented canonically in the log or explicitly removed as dormant API.\\n- The fjall dependency and obsolete MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend production surface are deleted after replacements land.\\n- A final composed fresh-store matrix, recovery/RSS corpus, crash/fault suite, and adversarial review either admit the completed architecture or invoke predeclared kill/narrow criteria.\\n\\n# Primary Evidence\\n- notes/mess-asterism/design.md sections 6-18 and 23\\n- notes/mess-asterism/research/01-10 plus the code review/response in research/11-12\\n- spikes/composed_decision/REPORT.md section 11\\n- spikes/state_kernel_dense_heads, active_microblocks, segment_effect, epoch_dedupe, directory_tournament, and seal_pack reports\\n- spikes/baseline_matrix/BN-2SU-REPORT.md and BN-2SU-FINAL.csv\\n\\n# Stop Criteria\\nStop or narrow any custom structure that misses its composed gate, increases correctness surface without an end-to-end win, or causes the formal/crash suites to be bypassed."}	blake3:jwdWXwCLs5DgT2-KNzjehoJ9q6jemlb7vxVzM6VgKAc
1784031512630285	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExngc		item.create	bn-ogn	{"description":"# Context\\nThe post-spike design pack contains known stale claims: production dedupe is dormant, LogEngine reads no longer carry all-history payloads in Book, migration is moot, and the current Fjall dependency is concentrated in MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend rather than the flat append path. Before more code lands, future workers need one exact map of canonical authority, derived accelerators, public compatibility obligations, and the chosen v3/v4 control-record boundary.\\n\\n# Goal\\nFreeze the remaining implementation contract against the current code and accepted spike evidence. Resolve documentation contradictions, record the no-migration scope, and decide how snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and optional dedupe keys become log-derived without silently making v4 mandatory.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Current code ownership and every Fjall keyspace/API are classified as authoritative, derived, dormant, or removable.\\n- A written decision selects v3 system records or accelerated v4 controls for each remaining canonical transition and rewires later bones if needed.\\n- State-frontier, fairness/cancellation, exact dedupe, retention, and batch-stratified performance contracts are explicit.\\n- Design/research manifests identify amendments and historical documents unambiguously.\\n- Stale resolved bones are closed and relevant existing bones are filed under this roadmap.\\n\\n# Why\\nThis prevents implementing the old migration narrative or synthetic MetaStore assumptions after the engine has already changed.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"l","title":"Phase 3: reconcile Asterism authority, contracts, and evidence"}	blake3:1YR4fVO9LpyK4p-TGMqT0PpfWAFf9KVA_sVkujSdlpc
1784031512836062	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnwc		item.create	bn-3ef	{"description":"# Context\\nbn-2su put the production append path under one owner and moved large pure frame preparation to producers, beating the Fjall-era engine. Profiling and the Asterism risk register still identify avoidable direct-path acknowledgements/allocations, borrowed-record copies for small batches, singleton retirement/publication, byte-ring fairness gaps, and one pwrite per batch.\\n\\n# Goal\\nComplete the reversible v3 flat-owner subset: owned/prepared submission, allocation-light direct commits, group-wide state publication, and evidence-gated coalesced writes, without B1 barrier overlap or any change to canonical ordering/durability semantics.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Public/facade owned append path avoids the defensive record clone while preserving the borrowed compatibility adapter.\\n- DirectCommitter has an ack-free result path with reusable bounded scratch.\\n- One committed group applies index/head effects and publishes one watermark before completing waiters.\\n- Backpressure fairness and dropped-waiter behavior are specified and model-tested.\\n- Coalesced pwritev is admitted only on matched production workloads and handles partial writes/roll/faults exactly.\\n- Full matrix retains all bn-2su gates and commits raw results.\\n\\n# Constraints\\nNo B1, no striped canonical log, no weakened CRC/barrier/publication ordering.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"xl","title":"Phase 4: finish the flat-owner execution kernel"}	blake3:PNFSLPXCBXYWM3iLu6DzbYBLJ6jEPnfLdMmSRe6cQ1g
1784031513042753	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoAc		item.create	bn-1n7j	{"description":"# Context\\nSpike A decisively admitted chunked dense head pages with packed AtomicU128 cells and a page-seqlock fallback. Spike G admitted exact epoch dedupe as a new feature, but research/11 correctly notes production dedupe is dormant today. Current Book still protects registry projections and HashMap heads under one mutex; MetaStore exposes Fjall tables whose runtime role must not be overstated.\\n\\n# Goal\\nMove live heads and latest-value slots into bounded direct pages owned by the flat owner, then add exact epoch dedupe only behind an explicit public semantic contract. Retire Book.heads after differential shadowing while keeping RegistryState canonical and strict.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Dense pages select lock-free packed cells by capability and use the verified seqlock fallback otherwise.\\n- Head reads/publish/recovery shadow-compare against the old representation before cutover.\\n- Book is reduced to registry/name concerns or replaced by explicit owner/read projections; no global head mutex remains.\\n- Dedupe semantics, scope, retention boundary, retry outcomes, collision exactness, and batch-level API are normative.\\n- Active/frozen epoch implementation passes forced-collision and recovery/checkpoint loss corpora.\\n- Composed memory/latency gates are measured; synthetic component wins alone do not admit integration.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"xl","title":"Phase 5: install dense resident state and exact dedupe"}	blake3:jRdn9Tv6urCm1nu6vyZ2IZVSZvsSCSfIxdcZQu8UDZk
1784031513249234	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoQc		item.create	bn-34as	{"description":"# Context\\nSpike F measured 2.2–5.3x apply throughput and admitted active pointer microblocks, while Spike J refuted stride-8 global pointers because each header pread made seeks 10–17x slower. Production should therefore replace only the stream-side ActiveIndex representation and retain a dense stride-1 global path.\\n\\n# Goal\\nIntegrate append-only stream microblocks with safe reader publication and reclamation across live append, recovery rebuild, segment roll, and sealed/hot union reads.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Production microblock arena uses bounded blocks, exact lookup, and deterministic fallback.\\n- Reader publication/reclamation is covered by Loom and real-thread roll/reclaim tests.\\n- Stream reads and head/tail resolution use microblocks; global active reads remain stride-1 and are rebenchmarked.\\n- Recovery uses the same apply path as live publication.\\n- End-to-end apply, read p99, memory, and contention gates meet the admitted Spike F thresholds; otherwise retain the simpler ActiveIndex.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"l","title":"Phase 6: replace active stream indexes with microblocks"}	blake3:1_PWJyAsw1hTvkz582uNegtV6Q6xT9a-dKjKSot96eA
1784031513451865	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExogc		item.create	bn-35f2	{"description":"# Context\\nSpike D proved compact SegmentEffect construction, parallel effect build, sequential apply, and content-addressed incremental checkpoints. Its review amendments require explicit bottom/existence tracking, allocator first/last boundaries, one canonical Effect definition, and discardable corruption fallback. Fjall can disappear only after snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe authority is canonical in the log and their resident heads are rebuildable.\\n\\n# Goal\\nIntegrate per-segment algebraic effects and Merkle-page kernel checkpoints into sealing/recovery, make remaining metadata transitions log-derived, replace FjallSnapshotBackend/MetaStore runtime roles, and remove the fjall dependency.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Canonical effects cover stream transitions, registry, snapshot/checkpoint slots, optional dedupe, and all named frontiers with proven composition laws.\\n- Seal builds effects in parallel where measured useful, validates them, and records them as rebuildable SealPack sections.\\n- Content-addressed checkpoint pages/manifests/current pointers install atomically; interrupted GC and every corruption class fall back to canonical log recovery.\\n- Recovery selects checkpoint plus suffix, validates anchors/digests, and meets the large-corpus startup gate without payload decode.\\n- Snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe control transitions follow the Phase 3 authority decision.\\n- MetaStore and FjallSnapshotBackend are replaced or explicitly demoted to non-production compatibility; fjall is removed from the workspace dependency graph.\\n- Formal, randomized differential, and fault-filesystem suites show identical canonical state.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"xl","title":"Phase 7: land SegmentEffect checkpoints and retire Fjall"}	blake3:jXdUIBGRFZstcCygZLdur3JVTuv2C_yQr73U3OXmw1w
1784031513656199	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExowc		item.create	bn-35fy	{"description":"# Context\\nSealPack and the density-adaptive bitrank directory are already integrated behind EngineOptions::seal_pack, default off. Default-on is blocked by measured reopen RSS growth and missing footer-to-pack identity binding. The HashMap fallback also has a proven foldhash win, and sidecar candidate lifecycle has an open re-seal bug.\\n\\n# Goal\\nResolve the known SealPack correctness/RSS blockers, validate the production directory chooser and bounded read path at scale, enable SealPack by default, and simplify legacy sidecars only after fallback/repair obligations are clear.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Large optional payload sections remain pread/cache-backed rather than resident; 2M/10M reopen RSS meets the bounded-memory gate.\\n- Segment footer binds exact pack identity and load/recovery verify the trust chain.\\n- Dense bitrank and sparse fallback selection is observable, deterministic, and exact on real segment distributions.\\n- Refuted/missing packs are re-queued safely for re-seal.\\n- Default-on matrix covers reopen, cold/warm reads, corruption, partial install, parity, and seal time.\\n- Legacy sidecar read/write paths are retained only where an explicit compatibility or repair requirement remains.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"l","title":"Phase 8: finish the sealed tier and default SealPack"}	blake3:WGHcr9U8_zdmSlPhrd7_qfpceeVoWwHl7xLbyQsqdKE
1784031513862235	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpAc		item.create	bn-5hcg	{"description":"# Context\\nv4 control-prelude capsules passed their spike and are merged flag-off. The old migration risk is moot because there are no existing stores, while v3 registry events now create API-visible gaps in domain global positions. Conversely, v4 adds format and crash-model surface and is not required for the reversible state-kernel subset.\\n\\n# Goal\\nMake an evidence-backed v4 adoption decision after the state kernel is real. If admitted, integrate allocation-free validation, control/event atomicity, control-only capsules, mixed-version recovery, and downgrade refusal. If declined, freeze the v3 system-record control contract and close conditional implementation bones without ambiguity.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Decision compares position semantics, control needs, scan overhead, maintenance surface, and composed benefit—not migration concerns.\\n- Normative format wording includes the safety-preserving A5 amendment, contiguous batch-id recovery rule, frontiers, and per-capsule idempotency semantics.\\n- Admitted implementation preserves no-control scan overhead below the spike gate and passes exhaustive/random torn-write models.\\n- Registration/snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe controls reuse canonical state machines and never split from same-capsule events.\\n- v3 readers remain supported as decided; older writers refuse unsafe read-write open after v4 bytes.\\n- SIGKILL, validate-vs-decode differential fuzz, marker flags, and downgrade cases are complete before default-on.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"xl","title":"Phase 9: decide v4 and complete the control plane"}	blake3:0F9qwI6glOmTE_KAFmClZVM-dije51nSJtXEFldRgqk
1784031514067423	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpQc		item.create	bn-3dos	{"description":"# Context\\nAsterism admission is based on composed outcomes, not the sum of spike headlines. Once the main structures are integrated, the project needs one final reproducible evidence bundle and only then should consider device-specific O_DSYNC, FDP/ZNS, io_uring, or other media specialization.\\n\\n# Goal\\nRun the final correctness/performance/startup/RSS/durability program, close the roadmap against predeclared kill criteria, and make explicit adopt/decline decisions for optional media paths.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Fresh-store matched AB/BA matrix is batch/payload/writer stratified with raw rows, p50/p99, barrier counts, CPU/load provenance, and Fjall-era/bare comparisons.\\n- Large-corpus checkpoint open, recovery fallback, resident RSS, disk/write amplification, and seal backlog are measured.\\n- Crash/torn/SIGKILL/formal/differential suites produce a release evidence bundle.\\n- Observability reports owner saturation, backpressure, checkpoint/effect fallback, seal backlog, and cache behavior.\\n- Optional O_DSYNC/FDP/ZNS/io_uring work proceeds only after device capability probes and independent gates; negative results are recorded and code is not retained.\\n- The top-level roadmap is closed only when every admitted child is integrated or explicitly declined with evidence.","kind":"goal","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine"],"parent":"bn-o5b","size":"l","title":"Phase 10: prove the composed engine and evaluate media specialization"}	blake3:8A9Bwm1d95REfJwkZiPR6nanEeC9Tmf9ne7qiYjIIU0
1784031605395768	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpgc		item.create	bn-187h	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe original Asterism plan described six remaining Fjall keyspaces and an all-history Book, but the current code has moved: LogEngine's Book is O(streams+types), production dedupe is dormant, and Fjall is now concentrated in mess-index::meta plus FjallSnapshotBackend. Future implementation must start from current call graphs, not old design prose.\n\n# Goal\nTrace every metadata read/write/recovery/seal path and classify each state item as canonical log authority, rebuildable accelerator, dormant public component, compatibility surface, or dead code. Produce an explicit deletion/replacement map for Book fields, MetaStore tables, FjallSnapshotBackend, Cargo dependencies, CLI/doctor paths, and tests.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nInspect LogEngine open/append/read/recovery/seal/drop, mess-index meta, snapshot save/load, projection checkpoints, dedupe APIs, and all fjall call sites. Record who writes each field, what durable bytes rebuild it, what happens on corruption, and which public API commits compatibility. Reconcile findings with research/11-12.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] One checked-in authority table covers every Book field and Fjall keyspace/API.\n- [ ] Every claimed replacement names canonical source bytes, rebuild path, publication frontier, and corruption fallback.\n- [ ] Dormant/synthetic components are not described as production behavior.\n- [ ] Later roadmap bones are amended if the audit disproves their assumptions.\n- [ ] No code is deleted in this audit bone.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents accidental loss of the last authoritative copy and avoids building replacements for components the production engine never uses.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","architecture"],"parent":"bn-ogn","size":"m","title":"Audit current storage authority and produce the Fjall deletion map"}	blake3:mlIRaDNUtwnbWpdsrKQWpY_0O0aE0q3lV5ryp8MnDtM
1784031605511739	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpwc		item.create	bn-k8qd	{"description":"# Context & Background\nFjall cannot be removed until snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and any admitted dedupe keys are canonical in the log. v3 can encode explicit system-stream events, as the registry already does, but they consume global positions. v4 control preludes avoid domain-position gaps and passed their spike, but add format/crash-model surface. There are no stores to migrate.\n\n# Goal\nWrite and approve an ADR choosing the canonical representation for each remaining control transition. The decision may keep v3 system records through Fjall retirement or accelerate v4; it must not leave later phases assuming contradictory paths.\n\n# Decision Inputs\nCompare atomicity, global-position semantics, expected-version behavior, batch/capsule-level idempotency, recovery scan cost, allocation-free validation, compatibility, downgrade safety, implementation sequencing, and maintenance burden. Reuse RegistryState-style strict folds; never make accelerators authoritative.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR covers snapshot install, projection checkpoint, dedupe key, registry, and control-only transition shapes.\n- [ ] Retry outcomes distinguish registration idempotence from event idempotence.\n- [ ] v3 path states the accepted position-gap contract; v4 path states the A5 amendment and batch-id rule.\n- [ ] Phase 7 and Phase 9 dependencies/descriptions are rewired to the chosen sequence.\n- [ ] Adversarial review answers crash-before/after-write/barrier and duplicate/retry cases.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes Fjall deletion achievable without smuggling in an unreviewed wire-format decision.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","architecture","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-ogn","size":"m","title":"Choose canonical v3 or v4 controls for snapshot, checkpoint, and dedupe transitions"}	blake3:RbPda9qlhpa6iVh0nCTNsL7u3NWjq32rupp8Ax0dQyc
1784031605631796	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqAc		item.create	bn-2hkp	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe pack predates several discoveries: migration is moot; B1 and stride-8 are rejected; owner must be committer; effect build parallelizes but apply is sequential; frontier creation needs explicit bottom/existence; allocator effects need first/last boundaries; v4 scanning needs validate/materialize separation; spike lettering and current-vs-target write descriptions drifted. research/11-12 records accepted corrections but the main documents and manifest are not yet coherent.\n\n# Goal\nUpdate the design/research set into one self-consistent implementation reference while retaining superseded analysis as clearly historical evidence.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Incorporate accepted V1-V6, C2, D3, D6, S1-S8 and the response qualifications.\n- [ ] Apply all handoff section 6 spike amendments and distinguish current engine from target architecture.\n- [ ] Mark research/06 as historical/moot without deleting its reasoning.\n- [ ] Document state frontiers for Process and durable modes, fairness/cancellation, and exact dedupe retention.\n- [ ] Use batch-stratified gates and link committed raw matrices.\n- [ ] Update MANIFEST.sha256 or explicitly define its coverage.\n\n# Project Value\nGives future implementers and reviewers a trustworthy specification instead of forcing them to reconstruct amendments from bone comments.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","docs","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-ogn","size":"m","title":"Reconcile the Asterism design pack with admitted spikes and current code"}	blake3:mmgqNyMsE0x6UuulengWXAT41ET2QLS0Tqi-UXwDZS4
1784031687756333	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqQc		item.create	bn-2yye	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEventStore currently constructs owned RecordToAppend values, then the borrowed Backend contract forces LogEngine to copy them again. bn-2su avoids that deep copy only for large prepared batches; small/common appends still pay it. Spike J measured an owned/interned submission topology as a 30–45 percentage-point opportunity versus bare.\n\n# Goal\nAdd an owned/prepared append seam from the facade to LogEngine while retaining the existing borrowed Backend method as a compatibility adapter. Carry owned payloads, distinct type names or validated type tokens, and exact byte/frame totals without giving callers authority over positions or registry ids.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] EventStore's ordinary command/append path transfers owned records without a deep clone.\n- [ ] Borrowed Backend callers preserve source/API compatibility and exact error ordering.\n- [ ] New/aliased type names are owner-resolved and revalidated; no stale token can bypass RegistryState.\n- [ ] Small batches use inline/reused storage where measurement justifies it.\n- [ ] Allocation counts and matched throughput are recorded for batch 1/10/100/1000.\n- [ ] Cancellation after ownership transfer retains the committed-capsule-always-publishes rule.\n\n# Project Value\nRemoves a measured producer-side ceiling without weakening the sole-owner correctness boundary.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine","perf"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Add an owned, interned append submission API"}	blake3:yzBlCCZwZIabNLSaEB8Zq2rfjCmxrhsQoiLlDNNa9AM
1784031687874042	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqgc		item.create	bn-21ew	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe synchronous flat owner still routes through structures designed for asynchronous committers: Arc<Mutex<AckState>> per batch, nested vectors, shape reconstruction, and fresh subframe/outcome allocation. Asterism calls for bounded arenas and no warm-path allocation for small appends.\n\n# Goal\nCreate a genuinely direct commit core returning index-aligned outcomes without ack locks, and retain bounded reusable scratch for requests, subframes, effects, and results.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] No Ack/Mutex/Arc allocation occurs in DirectCommitter's owner-only path.\n- [ ] Ordered-unit partial-success and poison behavior are byte/outcome identical to the current implementation under ENOSPC/EIO/roll faults.\n- [ ] Scratch has explicit byte/count caps and cannot retain attacker-sized allocations indefinitely.\n- [ ] Os remains sync-per-batch; Group and Process preserve their existing barrier rules.\n- [ ] SimRuntime and real-fs tests cover every failure boundary.\n- [ ] Profiles show the removed machinery no longer appears materially in owner CPU.\n\n# Project Value\nLowers fixed append cost and creates the clean effect/result seam needed by group publication.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","mess-log","perf","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Replace DirectCommitter acknowledgements with direct outcomes and reusable scratch"}	blake3:mDuPjnNs_KfW6j0W9bdeuDPwdMEETUNYFnX5soLWXIU
1784031687988394	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExqwc		item.create	bn-3vj4	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDirectCommitter makes one ordered durability decision for a gathered group, but retirement currently applies ActiveIndex/head/cache/watermark work plan by plan. This repeats locks and wakeups and obscures the design invariant: compute effects, durably accept the group, apply all landed effects, publish one frontier, then complete waiters.\n\n# Goal\nBuild one landed-group effect/result structure, apply all index and head updates in order, advance one read watermark, and only then resolve completions. Preserve ordered-unit filtering when registry or domain members fail.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ActiveIndex/microblock apply receives the full landed group once.\n- [ ] Head and registry folds occur in canonical file/global-position order.\n- [ ] Cache insertion cannot make data visible before the group publication frontier.\n- [ ] Cancellation, same-stream Exact races, partial ordered units, poison, and roll are deterministic in model and real-thread tests.\n- [ ] Readers observe either pre-group or published-prefix state, never committed-but-unpublished holes.\n- [ ] Metrics count groups/batches/events without per-plan percentile scans.\n\n# Project Value\nAligns the production engine with Asterism's one-authoritative-transition model and reduces fixed publication overhead.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine","index","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Publish each committed owner group as one state transition"}	blake3:_UMS5qe791M7INyLlOMXHs1zs1JMVaAxWLfxJRS5QhA
1784031688105229	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrAc		item.create	bn-3eg0	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe owner ring is bounded by bytes, but its wake/admission policy for large versus small waiters and the behavior of a dropped space waiter are not normative. Strict FIFO prevents starvation but can create head-of-line blocking; opportunistic admission can starve large intents. research/11 D6 requires a deliberate policy and a cancellation model.\n\n# Goal\nSpecify the admission policy, implement it with bounded memory, expose starvation/queue metrics, and model dropped waiters before and after reservation/enqueue.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Policy states FIFO/opportunistic rules, maximum bypass, oversized-intent handling, and shutdown behavior.\n- [ ] Dropping before reservation leaks no permits; dropping after enqueue cannot suppress a committed publication.\n- [ ] Loom or deterministic scheduler explores wake, cancel, close, and permit-release interleavings.\n- [ ] Stress tests mix tiny and near-limit batches and prove bounded progress for both.\n- [ ] Queue depth/bytes/wait time and starvation/bypass counters are queryable.\n\n# Project Value\nConverts an implicit liveness assumption into a testable owner contract.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","concurrency","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Define and verify byte-ring fairness and cancellation"}	blake3:oinNIGhVNvtQbN8BYepryVJNUhxVrRK2k-uZLEYgpGc
1784031688226003	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrQc		item.create	bn-1zv6	{"description":"# Context & Background\nProduction writes one positioned buffer per batch and one group barrier. Asterism targets one gathered/coalesced write where size permits, using pwritev or bounded chunks. Earlier spikes found large syscall reductions at high concurrency, but ordinary payloads and the newly prepared production path require a fresh matched decision.\n\n# Goal\nAdd an isolated production-shaped experiment using the real PreparedBatch/SegmentWriter layout. Compare current k-pwrite groups, pwritev, contiguous copy, and bounded iovec/chunk policies under Process/Os/Group, roll boundaries, and partial-write injection.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Same bytes, receipts, CRCs, positions, chain heads, and barrier counts for all variants.\n- [ ] Matrix spans batch/payload/writer/group sizes and records syscall counts, CPU, throughput, p99, and copy bytes.\n- [ ] Partial iovec writes, EINTR, ENOSPC, EIO, IOV_MAX, segment roll, and oversized groups have explicit behavior.\n- [ ] Admission requires a composed win in an important region without >5% regressions elsewhere; otherwise record DECLINED.\n- [ ] Raw results and host/device provenance are committed.\n\n# Project Value\nTests the remaining syscall/coalescing prize without assuming a microbenchmark transfers to the real owner.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","perf","io"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Measure coalesced pwritev on production owner groups"}	blake3:uqNPV7P6nVafhcKOuuPYbFUpurUUdMpJRoV9Bd-uq8U
1784031688345663	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrgc		item.create	bn-1l4j	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThis is conditional implementation following the production pwritev decision. It must not exist if the measurement bone declines the variant.\n\n# Goal\nRoute eligible committed groups through a bounded ordered vectored-write path while retaining the simple single-batch/current fallback and exactly the same durability/publication semantics.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Decision bone explicitly says ADMIT; otherwise close this bone as declined with no production code.\n- [ ] Robust writev loop handles partial vectors, EINTR, IOV_MAX, roll boundaries, and zero-progress errors.\n- [ ] Final CRC bytes are complete before I/O and buffers remain alive until the write finishes.\n- [ ] Os does not coalesce barriers across batches; Group/Process retain one accepted cut.\n- [ ] Fault, crash, chain, registry ordered-unit, and recovery tests are byte-identical to the fallback.\n- [ ] Metrics expose path choice, vectors/chunks, bytes, partial-write retries, and fallback reason.\n\n# Project Value\nCaptures measured syscall headroom without making the core dependent on a fragile fast path.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","mess-log","io","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Integrate bounded pwritev group writes if admitted"}	blake3:leV1XsqrclouaOwUh589U7XJNUNd2yE-Q_dJUWbb6u8
1784031688472557	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrwc		item.create	bn-2l3n	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase 4 changes multiple fixed-cost components. Acceptance must use the same fresh-store, matched, batch-stratified discipline that caught and then reversed bn-2su's large-batch regressions.\n\n# Goal\nRun the full production LogEngine/bare/Fjall-era matrix plus focused allocation, owner-CPU, queue-fairness, and syscall profiles. Commit every repetition and a decision report.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AB/BA ordering, at least median-of-3, quiet/load provenance, fresh stores, identical workload shape.\n- [ ] Process and Group cells report ev/s, p50/p99, barriers, allocations/copy bytes, owner CPU, and path selection.\n- [ ] No Process cell regresses from BN-2SU-FINAL without explicit product approval; Group non-regression and barrier parity hold.\n- [ ] Correctness suites include cancellation, ordered registry units, same-stream races, roll, chain, recovery, and faults.\n- [ ] Raw CSV and report identify admitted/declined optimizations and update later phase budgets.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents local hot-path cleverness from eroding the already-won end-to-end engine.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","benchmark","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-3ef","size":"m","title":"Rebaseline the completed flat-owner kernel against Fjall-era and bare engines"}	blake3:0TGzgRM8Hv0AdsE80gwRAQTZXyBaVpuimGOOyopL1qs
1784031767545786	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsAc		item.create	bn-34v8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike A admitted grow-only chunked pages at 16.02 bytes/stream, 33–43M applies/s, and orders-of-magnitude better concurrent reads than Fjall/RwLock HashMap. Packed AtomicU128 is preferred only where lock-free; page seqlock is the portable fallback. The production type must preserve dense-id bounds and safe directory growth.\n\n# Goal\nPort the admitted dense head table into mess-index as a production component with capability-selected cells, batch-by-page apply, bounded reader retry/escalation, and explicit memory accounting.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] API covers grow, apply committed group, point head read, snapshot/export, and recovery rebuild.\n- [ ] AtomicU128 path is used only when lock-free; seqlock fallback shares identical semantics.\n- [ ] Sparse/huge untrusted ids cannot allocate unbounded directories; RegistryState density is validated at the boundary.\n- [ ] Loom models real read/write/grow functions; threaded tests hammer same-page and directory growth.\n- [ ] Memory and apply/read gates reproduce within documented tolerance on production types.\n- [ ] No engine call site changes yet; integration is a separate bone.\n\n# Project Value\nProvides the verified resident primitive needed to remove Book.heads without coupling implementation and cutover risk.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","mess-index","state-kernel","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-1n7j","size":"m","title":"Add production dense head pages with atomic and seqlock cells"}	blake3:IW_Bj-QuDa1J6tURYn2QrMSLXYmEj4MzxuLYjCaGmxQ
1784031767665489	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsQc		item.create	bn-2wtl	{"description":"# Context & Background\nBook currently combines strict RegistryState/name projections with HashMap heads under one mutex. Dense heads should replace only the head component; registry semantics must remain canonical and aliases must still update read projections correctly.\n\n# Goal\nFeed dense heads from recovery and committed-group publication, compare every result against Book heads in tests/development, switch head/read planning to the dense table, then delete Book.heads and narrow the registry lock.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Recovery and live publication update both representations in canonical order during shadow stage.\n- [ ] Differential corpus covers new streams, aliases, registry stream 0, same-stream races, roll/reopen, cancellation, and partial ordered units.\n- [ ] Reader snapshots are coherent with the published watermark and never observe a torn version/global pair.\n- [ ] Book.heads and its lock traffic are removed after shadow evidence is green.\n- [ ] RegistryState remains the one fold; name Arc projections cannot diverge.\n- [ ] Metrics/profile demonstrate mutex removal and bounded resident cost.\n\n# Project Value\nRemoves the last global live-head mutex while preserving the registry correctness earned in bn-2di.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine","state-kernel","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-1n7j","size":"m","title":"Shadow dense heads against Book and cut production reads over"}	blake3:Gg-ClXQgctnsxLHYzjyv7qFUOnBGwGvJuQjKRlFbqIg
1784031767786519	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsgc		item.create	bn-2ctq	{"description":"# Context & Background\nProduction LogEngine does not currently use MetaStore dedupe and the public append API carries no idempotency key. Asterism dedupe is therefore a new feature, not a migration. v4's one DedupeKey per capsule implies batch-level idempotency; retention must preserve canonical full keys through the live window and retry outcomes must combine dedupe, registration, and expected-version rules precisely.\n\n# Goal\nFreeze the public and internal dedupe contract before implementing storage: key scope/length, one-key-per-batch semantics, returned original commit, window definition, retention, expiry, collision exactness, and Process-mode recovery outcomes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] API/ADR states stream/global scope, maximum key size, batch-level granularity, and per-event alternative.\n- [ ] Retry order is resolve registration, exact dedupe, expected version; matching live key returns original commit.\n- [ ] No/expired key conflicts normally and never silently duplicates an event.\n- [ ] Segment deletion rule preserves full keys until segment.end_position <= window_start.\n- [ ] Approximate structures are negative accelerators only; all positive candidates verify full canonical keys.\n- [ ] Security/resource review covers adversarial keys, collision floods, namespace abuse, and memory/disk caps.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents a fast component spike from accidentally defining unsafe product semantics.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","api","dedupe","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-1n7j","size":"m","title":"Specify exact batch-idempotency and dedupe retention semantics"}	blake3:6wH0ZDAh1L3nVnImoKjDimKDVFFBCmmllqP1vcwZUuY
1784031767906487	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExswc		item.create	bn-3dp1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike G proved an exact design: canonical full keys live once in log records, active/frozen epochs route fingerprint candidates, and forced collisions fall back to full-key comparison. Whole epochs expire without per-key LSM deletes. The production component must remain correct without any checkpoint and under malicious key distributions.\n\n# Goal\nImplement the active table, frozen epoch format/router, exact overflow candidates, epoch rotation/reclamation, and rebuild interface as a standalone mess-index component.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Differential equality with a simple reference over arbitrary 0–4KiB keys and window boundaries.\n- [ ] Forced fingerprint collisions, same-prefix floods, duplicate keys, and corrupt/missing epochs never produce false absorbs.\n- [ ] Rotation/reclamation performs no per-key tombstone writes and respects canonical-key retention.\n- [ ] Frozen formats are checksummed/versioned with deterministic rebuild fallback.\n- [ ] Active miss/hit, whole-window miss, resident/disk, and write-amplification gates are recorded on production code.\n- [ ] Component has no authority to acknowledge appends; owner integration is separate.\n\n# Project Value\nReplaces mutable KV churn with an exact, lifecycle-shaped accelerator while keeping the log authoritative.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","mess-index","dedupe","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-1n7j","size":"m","title":"Port exact active and frozen dedupe epochs into mess-index"}	blake3:mhzpYsjXl4sjNr0Q6kapGOqPwtsKIKXk6FwUif9GKdM
1784031768020691	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtAc		item.create	bn-1sh8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nComponent correctness is insufficient: the owner must decide duplicate absorption before expected-version admission, the canonical key/control must land atomically with the batch, and recovered-but-unacknowledged commits must return the original result. This work depends on the canonical-control ADR and the public dedupe contract.\n\n# Goal\nWire optional idempotency keys into owned append intents, owner planning, canonical log bytes, group effects, recovery rebuild, retention, and metrics.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Matching live key returns original version/global position without writing another capsule.\n- [ ] Registration+event crash retry with matching key absorbs; without key it conflicts and writes nothing.\n- [ ] Canonical control/key and event cannot split under any write/barrier/torn outcome.\n- [ ] Expired keys follow documented expected-version behavior.\n- [ ] Recovery/checkpoint loss rebuilds identical dedupe answers from canonical bytes.\n- [ ] Metrics expose live window, active/frozen epochs, candidates/full-key reads, absorbs, expiries, and collision overflow.\n- [ ] Fault, property, and real-process tests cover Process/Os/Group modes.\n\n# Project Value\nTurns the admitted exact structure into trustworthy user-visible idempotency rather than a disconnected metadata table.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine","dedupe","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-1n7j","size":"m","title":"Integrate exact dedupe through append, recovery, and observability"}	blake3:xRq7q3tmHXBh_YiDzyiKxju36S0ig9qEr-2Hpp7ukqo
1784031768136742	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtQc		item.create	bn-1lbf	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSnapshot heads and projection checkpoints are latest-value components keyed by dense stream/projector ids. Their resident representation should share the dense-page publication discipline, but key creation must be distinguishable from an unchanged bottom value so incremental checkpoints cannot reuse stale pages.\n\n# Goal\nAdd reusable dense latest-value slot tables with explicit presence/bottom semantics, coherent publication, snapshot/export, and deterministic fold interfaces. Do not make them authoritative; canonical control integration belongs to Phase 7.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Cell format represents absent versus present-at-zero distinctly.\n- [ ] Batch/group apply and read snapshots are coherent with publication frontiers.\n- [ ] Dirty tracking marks key creation even when the logical numeric value equals bottom.\n- [ ] Snapshot references/hashes and projection identifiers have bounded validated encodings.\n- [ ] Loom/property tests cover torn pairs, growth, concurrent read, and creation/update races.\n- [ ] Memory/apply metrics meet the resident-state budget.\n\n# Project Value\nSupplies the resident half of Fjall snapshot/checkpoint replacement while preserving log authority.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","mess-index","state-kernel","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-1n7j","size":"m","title":"Add dense latest-value slots for snapshot and projection heads"}	blake3:Ezmq3kenb8mhwDAKAwysaOGUYtIBCa85W5YulhED368
1784031817120573	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtgc		item.create	bn-1fsu	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike F admitted append-only stream microblocks with 2.2–5.3x apply throughput, lower memory, and faster head/tail resolution than the sharded map-of-vectors. Production needs a reusable arena, exact lookup/iteration, bounded blocks, and a safe ownership model rather than copying spike code wholesale.\n\n# Goal\nImplement the stream-side microblock arena in mess-index with one-writer append, immutable published blocks, exact version lookup/range iteration, memory accounting, and deterministic fallback for edge shapes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Block layout/versioning and maximum counts/bytes are documented and validated.\n- [ ] Append preserves per-stream version continuity and global pointer identity.\n- [ ] Head, point, range, and ascending iteration match ActiveIndex reference results.\n- [ ] Hot/sparse/Zipf and one-event/100-event production-shaped benchmarks reproduce admitted gates.\n- [ ] No global stride sampling is introduced.\n- [ ] Engine integration and reclamation remain separate bones.\n\n# Project Value\nMoves the measured stream-tail representation into a reviewable production component.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","mess-index","microblocks","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-34as","size":"m","title":"Port the admitted active stream microblock arena"}	blake3:5GimBlaxmqzQvt4nPJeumP3eFgdn7rTvmw0XFIbVk0o
1784031817234687	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtwc		item.create	bn-dxv0	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe production ActiveIndex serves both stream and global active reads. Spike J proved stride-8 global pointers are disastrous because each skipped header becomes a pread, so only the stream side should move to microblocks; global positions remain dense stride-1 entries.\n\n# Goal\nFeed microblocks from committed-group publication and recovery, route active stream reads/heads/ranges through them, keep the current stride-1 global path, and preserve sealed+hot union semantics.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Live and recovery apply share one canonical microblock insertion path.\n- [ ] Stream reads across active/sealed boundaries are byte/order/version identical to the old index.\n- [ ] Global point/page/subscription paths retain stride-1 behavior and measured seek latency.\n- [ ] Publication watermark clamps both representations coherently.\n- [ ] Differential corpus covers rolls, empty/tail segments, hot streams, aliases, and cache on/off.\n- [ ] Old stream map-of-vectors storage is removed only after shadow comparison.\n\n# Project Value\nCaptures Spike F's win without adopting Spike J's refuted global design.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","engine","microblocks","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-34as","size":"m","title":"Integrate microblocks for stream reads while retaining stride-1 globals"}	blake3:bNLg2vwhJtOXf_pajm4Kkym1sNsPPuMe-Gx-If0kd-g
1784031817348631	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuAc		item.create	bn-1yi7	{"description":"# Context & Background\nImmutable blocks still need safe publication and reclamation when segments roll while readers hold references. The design proposes epoch/lease-style reclamation; a use-after-free or missing tail would corrupt reads even though canonical log bytes remain intact.\n\n# Goal\nSpecify ownership lifetimes, implement roll/reclaim handoff, and exhaust publication/reclamation interleavings with Loom, deterministic scheduling, and real-thread stress.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Reader leases/epochs make block lifetime and retirement conditions explicit.\n- [ ] Loom models append, block publish, reader acquire/release, roll, and reclaim using production primitives.\n- [ ] Forced slow readers across repeated rolls never observe freed/torn/missing blocks.\n- [ ] Shutdown and recovery rebuild release every allocation without blocking indefinitely.\n- [ ] Reclamation backlog and bytes are observable and bounded.\n- [ ] Sanitizer/Miri coverage is added where the implementation permits.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes the lock-free read win safe enough for the storage engine rather than merely fast in a spike.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","concurrency","microblocks","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-34as","size":"m","title":"Prove microblock publication, roll, and reclamation safety"}	blake3:zVsuQpzTvahkSad1aRVCI-DdX91oe8E921Y9ItEP83k
1784031817465240	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuQc		item.create	bn-1cwy	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase 6 admission requires real engine evidence, including sealed/hot reads and global-stride non-regression, not only Spike F component numbers.\n\n# Goal\nRun a matched ActiveIndex-versus-microblock engine matrix and either admit the cutover or retain the simpler representation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Measure apply throughput, stream head/point/range p50/p99, global seek, RSS, allocations, and reclamation backlog.\n- [ ] Cover hot stream, Zipf, uniform, 1/4/64 readers, one-event/100-event batches, roll/reopen, cache states.\n- [ ] Require >=1.25x apply, <=0.75x stream p99, <=0.7x memory, and no meaningful global/read correctness regression, or document a justified revised product gate.\n- [ ] Commit raw results and an ADMIT/DECLINE decision.\n- [ ] Decline removes experimental production code and closes dependent cutover work clearly.\n\n# Project Value\nEnforces Asterism's rule that sophisticated structures must win in the composed engine.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","benchmark","microblocks","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-34as","size":"s","title":"Gate production microblocks on composed memory and latency"}	blake3:LsE-moNYNazQe5kLz4mk-y-WdWFd0jk68Rled3IaCUU
1784031911936779	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExugc		item.create	bn-1mzi	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike D proved the approach but found specification corrections: latest-value creation needs explicit bottom/presence, allocator composition needs first/last boundaries, full ordered tree composition is slower than parallel build plus sequential apply, and Process mode cannot pretend a runtime-known crash-stable frontier exists.\n\n# Goal\nFreeze one canonical production Effect definition and laws for stream paths, registry assignments, latest snapshot/checkpoint slots, optional dedupe epochs, allocators, and named frontiers. This definition must be shared by live group effects, seal effects, checkpointing, and recovery.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Types encode incoming/outgoing stream versions, global boundaries, presence/bottom, allocator first/last, and stable control identities.\n- [ ] Frontiers distinguish speculative, written/accepted, crash-stable, and published with mode-specific inequalities.\n- [ ] Composition/apply identity, associativity, conflict, and idempotence boundaries are documented and property-tested.\n- [ ] Effect idempotence filters duplicate control identity before calling strict RegistryState; canonical duplicate registration remains an error.\n- [ ] One implementation is used by all consumers; no parallel registry/snapshot/dedupe fold drifts.\n- [ ] Formal/executable reference oracle is checked in before storage encoding.\n\n# Project Value\nEstablishes the mathematical/correctness core on which fast recovery and Fjall removal depend.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","state-kernel","formal","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Define the canonical SegmentEffect algebra and state frontiers"}	blake3:lKXfOhLH2h_ZTcT23Nh7i4aptXF8rmdVgMushN1g3HM
1784031912055238	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuwc		item.create	bn-2dbp	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSealPack reserves SEGMENT_EFFECT and REGISTRY_DELTA section kinds but does not emit them. Spike D showed effect build parallelizes while ordered composition should not materialize intermediate maps. Effects are accelerators: missing/corrupt/wrong-anchor data must fall back to scanning canonical headers/controls.\n\n# Goal\nBuild one effect per sealed segment from canonical accepted capsules, encode it in SealPack, verify it against the full fold before install, and expose exact decode/apply/fallback behavior.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Parallel map/build and sequential apply follow measured Spike D strategy.\n- [ ] Encoding is versioned, bounded, checksummed/content-bound, and reports bytes per touched stream/control.\n- [ ] Seal verification compares semantic state/digest, not compression-byte identity.\n- [ ] Missing, corrupt, stale, reordered, duplicate, or anchor-mismatched effect is ignored and raw canonical recovery succeeds.\n- [ ] Registry effects feed each canonical assignment exactly once into RegistryState.\n- [ ] Seal time, effect size, and apply throughput meet the production recovery budget.\n\n# Project Value\nTurns immutable segments into proof-carrying state accelerators without making them authoritative.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","seal","state-kernel","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Emit and validate SegmentEffect sections during sealing"}	blake3:ZyWELfKZBwhjZ4dYA4pnUVcnyvkWH3uA7Rd2vI44cXU
1784031912172630	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvAc		item.create	bn-18y1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike D admitted incremental content-addressed checkpoint pages with alternating current pointers. Checkpoints must be discardable, proportional to dirty pages, and safe under missing pages, stale manifests, hash mismatch, interruption, and GC races.\n\n# Goal\nImplement page encoding, content-addressed page store, manifest/anchor/digest, durable temporary-write and rename protocol, alternating current pointers, incremental dirty-page reuse, retention, and interruption-safe garbage collection.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Page/manifest formats are versioned, length-bounded, checksummed/hashed, and include canonical log anchor plus state digest.\n- [ ] Install ordering and directory fsync semantics are explicit and fault-injected at every operation.\n- [ ] Key creation dirties a page even when value equals logical bottom.\n- [ ] Missing/corrupt/stale page or pointer rejects the checkpoint and falls back without wrong state.\n- [ ] Incremental writes scale with dirty pages; GC never deletes a page reachable from either current generation/read lease.\n- [ ] Doctor/verify can validate, explain, and safely discard checkpoints.\n\n# Project Value\nChanges startup asymptotics while retaining the log as the only required recovery source.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","checkpoint","storage","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Add content-addressed Merkle-page kernel checkpoints"}	blake3:l3ppxEjznQL36s-xTdOj-UQgNJLPxH4eEQxc_6xJ6_g
1784031912295825	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvQc		item.create	bn-3ctl	{"description":"# Context & Background\nProduction open must choose the newest valid checkpoint, validate its anchor/digest, apply sealed effects and active suffix in canonical order, and fall back progressively. Parallelism belongs in effect construction/loading; final apply preserves order.\n\n# Goal\nIntegrate checkpoint discovery and validation into LogEngine recovery, apply effects/suffix without payload decode, expose recovery mode/digest metrics, and prove equality with full scan.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Startup modes are explicit: checkpoint+effects+suffix, effects+suffix, header/control scan, and loud corruption refusal where canonical bytes fail.\n- [ ] Ordered parallel load/build produces the same digest as sequential full fold over at least 100k randomized histories.\n- [ ] Wrong anchor, missing/corrupt page/effect, stale current pointer, and interrupted GC select the documented fallback.\n- [ ] 100M-event synthetic/realistic corpus measures wall time, syscalls, bytes read, payload decodes, CPU, and peak RSS.\n- [ ] Clean checkpoint open meets the declared budget and performs zero historical payload decode.\n- [ ] Recovery emits a reproducible digest/evidence record for doctor and tests.\n\n# Project Value\nDelivers the user-visible startup/RSS reason for the algebra rather than stopping at on-disk formats.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","recovery","checkpoint","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Recover from kernel checkpoint plus ordered suffix"}	blake3:jOqEqfbz4VCGET0jZUI7xvZByovR4Pkda_6fGFjmBa8
1784031912418574	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvgc		item.create	bn-ozi5	{"description":"# Context & Background\nFjallSnapshotBackend currently makes snapshot-head advancement authoritative in Fjall after writing blob storage. Asterism requires an out-of-place snapshot pack whose install transition is canonical in the log, with resident latest-value slots and effects/checkpoints as rebuildable projections. research/11 notes current state/event hashes may be absent; controls need explicit presence flags.\n\n# Goal\nAdd immutable snapshot packs, durable install protocol, canonical SnapshotInstalled transition selected by the Phase 3 ADR, recovery fold into dense slots/effects, and a replacement backend/API preserving save/load semantics.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Blob/pack is durable and hash-verified before the canonical install record can commit.\n- [ ] Install control carries explicit presence flags for optional state/event-prefix hashes.\n- [ ] Head never names a missing/unverified pack; orphan packs are GC-safe.\n- [ ] Recovery/checkpoint loss rebuilds identical latest snapshot heads from log controls.\n- [ ] Save/load, retention, corruption, interrupted install, concurrent save, and reopen tests match or improve FjallSnapshotBackend semantics.\n- [ ] File-count, disk amplification, save/load latency, and RSS are measured against current blob-per-version/Fjall heads.\n\n# Project Value\nRemoves the principal production Fjall consumer and avoids one-file-per-version scaling.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","snapshot","storage","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Replace Fjall snapshot heads with log-derived snapshot packs"}	blake3:FAMTJhWegcxTVxE6GYXu-yyOx2pNRhGWjSn0P6MWaro
1784031912536956	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvwc		item.create	bn-11mk	{"description":"# Context & Background\nMetaStore exposes projection checkpoint positions in Fjall, but Asterism models them as right-biased canonical control transitions projected into dense slots, effects, and kernel checkpoints. Position zero/key creation must be digest-visible, and a checkpoint cannot outrun the state it certifies.\n\n# Goal\nDefine and integrate canonical ProjectionCheckpoint transitions through the chosen v3/v4 control representation, public checkpoint API, owner commit path, recovery fold, dense slots, SegmentEffect, and kernel checkpoint.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Identifier, position/frontier, optional digest, monotonicity/rewind policy, and authorization boundaries are normative.\n- [ ] Control is committed on the same durability spine as events and cannot publish before its accepted write/barrier point.\n- [ ] Position-zero creation changes state/digest and incremental dirty tracking.\n- [ ] Recovery and checkpoint fallback reproduce exact latest values.\n- [ ] Concurrent writers/conflicts, stale digests, corruption, and Process-mode semantics are tested.\n- [ ] MetaStore checkpoint callers have a documented replacement path.\n\n# Project Value\nMoves the last checkpoint authority from a mutable side database into the event-log state model.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","projection","checkpoint","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Make projection checkpoints canonical log transitions"}	blake3:ZJ_xJb0SDEQilHKty0QpSpXxnKiVGV1b_DKoaGQ7EkY
1784031912658613	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwAc		item.create	bn-fj34	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDeletion is safe only after the authority audit and replacements for heads, snapshots, checkpoints, and any admitted dedupe are merged. There are no stores to migrate, but public APIs, CLI inspection, tests, Cargo features, docs, and golden assumptions may still depend on MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend.\n\n# Goal\nRemove the Fjall-backed metadata implementation and dependency from production/workspace code, migrate or deliberately remove callers, and prove a clean store exercises no Fjall files, symbols, or transitive dependency.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Authority audit checklist has a landed replacement or explicit API-removal decision for every table/caller.\n- [ ] mess-index::meta and FjallSnapshotBackend are deleted or moved to a clearly non-production external compatibility crate only if explicitly required.\n- [ ] fjall disappears from Cargo.lock/dependency trees and no meta directory is created.\n- [ ] CLI doctor/inspect/rebuild and docs describe log-derived state/checkpoints/packs.\n- [ ] Full tests, golden stores as applicable, recovery/fault suites, and fresh-store benchmarks are green.\n- [ ] Adversarial review traces every deleted authority to canonical bytes and rebuild proof.\n\n# Project Value\nCompletes the one-authority architecture and removes the second storage engine rather than merely bypassing it on append.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","cleanup","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Delete MetaStore, FjallSnapshotBackend, and the Fjall dependency"}	blake3:GLckBZNkApFC7Etdr-Qn0qSb5MBzfPb0KwLjBzNfLis
1784032030651352	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwQc		item.create	bn-we9x	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSealPack already contains the Spike H density chooser: bitvector+rank for dense stream-id universes and sorted fallback otherwise. The path needs production-scale validation and observability before default-on; the fallback should also receive the independent foldhash win where a HashMap remains in use.\n\n# Goal\nAudit actual chooser call sites and thresholds, replay real sealed-segment distributions, expose representation/open/build/lookup metrics, and verify exact deterministic fallback on corrupt/pathological inputs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Real 2M/10M/100M corpora report density, selected codec, bytes, build/open time, cold/warm hit/miss p99, and fallback count.\n- [ ] Dense chooser reproduces Spike H's >=20% important-region win and never selects bitrank above the approved density/size bounds.\n- [ ] Every present/absent lookup and ascending iteration is exact after serialize/open/corruption tests.\n- [ ] Representation selection is visible in engine/doctor metrics.\n- [ ] Sparse/empty/tail segments use a deterministic simple fallback.\n\n# Project Value\nEnsures the admitted succinct structure actually serves production data and remains explainable operationally.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","sealed","perf"],"parent":"bn-35fy","size":"s","title":"Validate and instrument the production sealed-directory chooser"}	blake3:yRW72daEle6jbZ3qHZrPJmPyQ5BslLoFZKpYKRoZM88
1784032030779828	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwgc		item.create	bn-ccx1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSealPack reduces sealed files and improves cold reads but is default-off until the measured RSS regression and footer identity gap are fixed. Defaulting changes recovery/install preference and must retain raw-segment fallback, parity, repair, and legacy sidecar reads.\n\n# Goal\nFlip EngineOptions::seal_pack only after all blockers and the default-on matrix are green. Define the transition policy for legacy sidecars, pack regeneration, repair, and any later removal of sidecar writes/readers.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] bn-dbz and bn-11g are done with adversarial reviews; re-seal lifecycle bug is closed.\n- [ ] Default-on fresh/open/roll/seal/reopen/verify/repair behavior is covered in CLI and library tests.\n- [ ] Missing/corrupt pack falls back to canonical raw bytes and can be re-queued without reopen loops.\n- [ ] Legacy sidecars remain readable as explicitly required; default writes create one pack installation state.\n- [ ] Config/docs/metrics expose pack state and fallback reason.\n- [ ] No flag flip occurs if RSS, seal time, open time, or correctness gates fail.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes the consolidated sealed representation the real engine default instead of a perpetually experimental branch.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","sealed","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-35fy","size":"m","title":"Enable SealPack by default after trust and RSS gates pass"}	blake3:FbFu-XhTOl4tOp3-cXVQG8HtAxPleQ5CpJQuM8brsEQ
1784032030896507	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwwc		item.create	bn-1yor	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike I missed reopen peak RSS, which Spike J later found regressed +18%/+36%. The acceptance run must therefore include memory and all installation/fallback states, not just file opens and cold p99.\n\n# Goal\nProduce the final default-on evidence bundle across 2M/10M/100M events and adversarial pack/segment states.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Measure reopen wall, peak/resident RSS, file opens, bytes read, cold/warm p50/p99, seal wall/p99, disk bytes, and directory representation.\n- [ ] Inject missing/truncated/header/directory/mandatory/optional corruption, wrong identity, torn temp, footer-without-pack, pack-without-footer, parity damage, and interrupted re-seal.\n- [ ] Semantic payload/pointer/type results match raw/legacy paths; optional corruption degrades locally.\n- [ ] AB/BA or equivalent matched order and raw per-run evidence are committed.\n- [ ] Report gives explicit ADMIT/DECLINE/default decision and sidecar cleanup constraints.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents another unmeasured dimension from escaping a local sealed-tier win.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","benchmark","sealed","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-35fy","size":"m","title":"Run the SealPack default-on scale and fault matrix"}	blake3:BvNxUzgcKWo13lIkw7Kd7LC1RbFxRq-dtOn8YbOBKo4
1784032108275045	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxAc		item.create	bn-1ojm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe v4 capsule prelude spike passed, but the roadmap deliberately deferred a production format change until the flat owner and log-derived state kernel existed. Phase 3 records the interim v3/v4 control contract; this gate revisits v4 with the actual post-Fjall engine and no migration burden because there are no stores/users.\n\n# Goal\nProduce a bounded ADMIT/DECLINE decision for v4. If admitted, freeze the writer/reader/crash-model work below. If declined, record why, freeze v3 control semantics, and close the conditional implementation bones without speculative production code.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Compare v3 system records and v4 control preludes on domain-position semantics, owner integration, validation cost, recovery, downgrade refusal, and long-term format burden.\n- [ ] Reconcile the Phase 3 ADR rather than leaving two competing contracts.\n- [ ] State the normative batch identity/idempotency rule and any A5 design amendment.\n- [ ] Include a wire-format/security review and exact stop criteria.\n- [ ] Emit an explicit ADMIT or DECLINE decision and update dependent bone state/descriptions accordingly.\n\n# Project Value\nKeeps v4 a deliberate capability decision rather than an accidental prerequisite or permanent half-feature.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","architecture","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"m","title":"Decide v4 admission at the post-Fjall control-plane gate"}	blake3:4dGSNREc47Rk2QU8i1focgvD_ZM-fbtppxsYophVE9Y
1784032108401044	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxQc		item.create	bn-ro0i	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpike E has a validated prelude prototype and model, but production adoption requires one normative byte contract shared by validation, decode, writer, recovery, and downgrade detection.\n\n# Goal\nTurn the admitted v4 design into the canonical format specification and checked implementation boundary, preserving allocation-free validate/materialize separation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Normative byte layout covers magic/version, flags, counts, lengths, CRC coverage, reserved-zero rules, control records, user records, and global-position effects.\n- [ ] Independent validate and materialize paths accept/reject the same corpus; fuzzing differentially pins them.\n- [ ] Checked arithmetic rejects count/length/offset overflow before allocation or slicing.\n- [ ] Formal/exhaustive model covers capsule atomicity, expected versions, batch identity, registry/control transitions, and recovery prefix rules.\n- [ ] v3/v4 compatibility and downgrade-refusal rules are executable tests, not prose only.\n\n# Project Value\nCreates one auditable contract before v4 bytes become durable production state.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","spec","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"m","title":"Make the admitted v4 capsule contract normative and mechanically checked"}	blake3:_z8IW5vhOMgaaMGu7oyuP1pVDmPLubnFnhzQGuLks1I
1784032108520632	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxgc		item.create	bn-5rc6	{"description":"# Context & Background\nv4 exists only as a prelude spike. The production writer must be owned by the same committer that assigns positions, validates expected versions, publishes state, and acknowledges durability.\n\n# Goal\nAdd bounded v4 capsule planning/encoding to the flat owner, including user-only, control-only, and mixed capsules, without adding a second authority or unbounded per-batch allocation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Owner validates the whole capsule before writing and assigns contiguous domain positions only to user records.\n- [ ] Registry, snapshot/checkpoint, and admitted dedupe controls use the Phase 3 ADR's transition forms.\n- [ ] iovec/scratch limits, cancellation, fairness, durability, and retry outcomes remain explicit and tested.\n- [ ] Writer produces deterministic bytes for a canonical corpus and never partially publishes a capsule.\n- [ ] v3 writing remains available only according to the approved compatibility/default policy.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes v4 an owner-native format rather than a parallel append mechanism.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","engine","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"m","title":"Integrate v4 capsule encoding into the single-owner append path"}	blake3:nol0k9A5t4BBLKcLxcpdl7uKmHD7zesEkc2-x9I_zRQ
1784032108640960	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxwc		item.create	bn-2wpj	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEven without user-store migration, test fixtures, rollback, and staged rollout require exact format detection and deterministic recovery behavior. A v3-only binary must never silently open durable v4 state.\n\n# Goal\nImplement production segment detection, mixed v3/v4 iteration and recovery, and explicit downgrade refusal while retaining one semantic event/control fold.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Open/recovery detect format before decoding and reject unsupported v4 directories with a typed actionable error.\n- [ ] Mixed v3/v4 segments replay to the same registry, heads, dedupe, snapshot, projection, and global-position state as a reference model.\n- [ ] Torn/truncated/corrupt capsules stop at the canonical prefix without accepting partial controls or user groups.\n- [ ] Repair/verify/inspect tooling identifies segment and capsule format and reports the first invalid boundary.\n- [ ] marker/reserved fields and unknown required features fail closed; optional forward-compatible behavior is specified.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes rollout and rollback behavior explicit at the durable-state boundary.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","recovery","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"m","title":"Integrate mixed v3/v4 read, recovery, and downgrade refusal"}	blake3:cVk5R5vFCskCpcyNQ-N6cR78sdlaklqGyjRE2zO5qpM
1784032108757400	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyAc		item.create	bn-3ew9	{"description":"# Context & Background\nLocal encode/decode success is insufficient for a storage format. The final gate must exercise real owner writes, SIGKILL/torn boundaries, mixed versions, semantic differential replay, malformed input, and operational downgrade behavior.\n\n# Goal\nProduce the evidence bundle required to enable v4 by default, or decline/default-off with a precise failure record.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] SIGKILL harness covers every write/barrier/publication boundary for user-only, control-only, and mixed capsules.\n- [ ] Differential fuzzing compares validate/decode/reference replay and includes overflow, reserved flags, corrupt lengths/counts/CRCs, and random truncation.\n- [ ] v3-only refusal, mixed v3/v4 recovery, verify/repair, and unsupported-feature errors pass end-to-end CLI tests.\n- [ ] Composed throughput, tail latency, bytes/event, recovery wall, and peak RSS are compared with the approved v3 engine using raw matched-order evidence.\n- [ ] Report records ADMIT/DECLINE and the actual default/compatibility policy.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents a wire-format improvement from shipping with an under-tested crash or downgrade model.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","benchmark","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"m","title":"Run the v4 crash, fuzz, compatibility, and default-on matrix"}	blake3:PWztkV7SkqnzIBXb8KfOaDk_nS6FMU3kPfp0K8bWGvg
1784032178646503	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyQc		item.create	bn-11ba	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEach Asterism component has local metrics, but operators need one coherent account of which state is canonical, which accelerators are installed, and whether background work or fallback paths are accumulating.\n\n# Goal\nAdd a stable engine/CLI observability surface for the owner, dense state, dedupe epochs, active indexes, effects/checkpoints, sealed packs/directories, format versions, and background queues.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Metrics report owner queue bytes/groups, group width/wait, write/barrier/ack latency, conflicts, cancellations, and durability mode.\n- [ ] State reports expose active/frozen generations, head/dedupe/index memory, checkpoint/frontier lag, SealPack representation/fallback, and canonical-log watermarks.\n- [ ] `mess doctor` or the approved inspection command explains authority and degraded/fallback states without treating an accelerator as authoritative.\n- [ ] Metric names/units/cardinality are documented and bounded; no stream/type/batch IDs become unbounded labels.\n- [ ] Fault-injection and recovery tests assert the visible state at important transitions.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes the redesigned engine diagnosable as one system rather than a collection of opaque fast paths.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","observability","cli"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Expose the composed engine's authority, backlog, and fallback state"}	blake3:pJUr2UBuiWqLdp4QG7uuUYoPuS5KNFGfyEyEUggBPCU
1784032178762215	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExygc		item.create	bn-hvp1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSeveral spikes proved local wins, while SealPack showed that a local latency win can hide a serious reopen RSS loss. The composed engine needs scale curves, not just a single steady-state throughput number.\n\n# Goal\nMeasure the complete engine at 2M/10M/100M events across cold open, replay, write, roll, seal, checkpoint, reopen, and backlog-drain phases.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Record wall/CPU, peak/resident RSS, allocation counts, file opens, read/write bytes, fsyncs, write amplification, recovery/checkpoint wall, and background backlog high-water/drain time.\n- [ ] Cover dense/sparse/Zipf streams, varied payloads, 1/4/16/64 writers, process/group/durable modes, and slow-seal/slow-device injection.\n- [ ] Compare bare log, Fjall-era baseline, flat-owner checkpoint, and current composed engine with matched semantics and workload topology.\n- [ ] Preserve raw per-run data, hardware/software metadata, order randomization or AB/BA, confidence intervals, and quiet-run diagnostics.\n- [ ] Attribute regressions to a component or mark the final gate failed; do not average them away.\n\n# Project Value\nDemonstrates that local Asterism wins compose without moving cost into startup, memory, write amplification, or maintenance debt.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","benchmark","scale","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Run large-scale Asterism startup, memory, write-amplification, and backlog benchmarks"}	blake3:WHhoaWUqG2KOB4zWsUhxzk8H41NDmhTOkCbjlY3GFjM
1784032178880660	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExywc		item.create	bn-2wso	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe roadmap succeeds only if the production engine preserves all durable semantics while materially beating the Fjall-era engine and staying close to the bare log on matched work. Component reports are inputs, not the release verdict.\n\n# Goal\nExecute one batch-stratified release matrix over the fully composed configuration and issue an evidence-backed ADMIT, NARROW, or DECLINE verdict for each feature and for the default engine as a whole.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Differential/state-model tests cover append conflicts, retry/idempotency, registry, heads, snapshots, projections, global positions, stream reads, roll/seal, reopen, verify, repair, and canonical-prefix recovery.\n- [ ] Crash/SIGKILL/torn/corruption tests cover every durable publication boundary and admitted v3/v4/SealPack combination.\n- [ ] Performance matrix reports throughput, p50/p99/p999, CPU, RSS, allocations, bytes/event, write amplification, recovery/open wall, and background interference by batch-size stratum.\n- [ ] Bare-log, Fjall-era, post-flat-owner, and final comparisons use matched semantics/topology; raw evidence and exact commands are retained.\n- [ ] Any missed gate names the failing component, reverts/default-disables it, or creates a bounded corrective bone before release.\n\n# Project Value\nSupplies the only verdict that matters: whether the integrated storage engine is faster, smaller, and still trustworthy.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","benchmark","release","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Run the final composed Asterism correctness and performance matrix"}	blake3:kFMebEPBMK4eHyxkhGLTJxkC2v9o_KNnTggB8bAXBFo
1784032178997359	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzAc		item.create	bn-7n39	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe research leaves media-aware placement and asynchronous I/O as optional endgame work. They must be tested only after the portable engine is clean, because neither may become a correctness dependency or obscure a regression in the core design.\n\n# Goal\nRun bounded production-shaped spikes for io_uring submission/completion and, where hardware/emulation permits, FDP/ZNS placement of active, sealed, checkpoint, and discardable accelerator writes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Baseline is the final portable engine on identical durability semantics and workload topology.\n- [ ] io_uring experiment measures syscall/wakeup/CPU savings, queue depth, cancellation, error propagation, tail latency, and fallback behavior.\n- [ ] FDP/ZNS experiment measures placement/write amplification, garbage collection, recovery, wear/space behavior, and clean fallback on ordinary filesystems/devices.\n- [ ] No device-specific metadata becomes canonical; the append log and control transitions remain sufficient for recovery.\n- [ ] Each experiment ends in ADMIT, NARROW, or DECLINE with raw evidence and explicit support/maintenance cost.\n\n# Project Value\nTests the last hardware-aware opportunities without contaminating the portable correctness model.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","spike","media"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Evaluate optional io_uring and FDP/ZNS media specialization"}	blake3:iVYg4cOnGeebanlIxU19xLU9qO37ZRuh18NB1couvac
1784032179112648	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzQc		item.create	bn-2m8n	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe completed roadmap will leave spike crates, temporary flags, compatibility paths, and older design claims whose status must be explicit. Cleanup must follow the final evidence rather than deleting escape hatches early.\n\n# Goal\nPublish the final architecture/adoption record, update operator and contributor documentation, and remove only the legacy code and flags made unreachable by admitted defaults.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Report lists every roadmap feature and its ADMIT/NARROW/DECLINE/default status with links to raw evidence and review.\n- [ ] Architecture docs identify the canonical log authority, owner/publish model, discardable accelerators, checkpoint/effect frontiers, sealed tier, and format policy.\n- [ ] Temporary dual-write/shadow/experimental flags and dead Fjall/sidecar/v3-or-v4 paths are inventoried and either removed or assigned an explicit compatibility lifetime.\n- [ ] Spike code/data retention policy keeps reproducible evidence without shipping unused dependencies or binaries.\n- [ ] Fresh install, reopen of supported fixtures, docs examples, full tests, formatting, and release checks pass after cleanup.\n\n# Project Value\nConverts a research program into a maintainable production engine with no ambiguous authority or forgotten escape path.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","docs","cleanup","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Publish the Asterism adoption report and retire superseded paths"}	blake3:C3VtZfn4Qf10WLWapOKMLqqw48B0Q6uZIz_ngpLzbI4
