1784032273610074	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsQg		item.comment	bn-3pz	{"body":"Resolved by the production flat-owner integration in bn-2su (commit 69b95604). BN-2SU-REPORT.md records stable four-writer log/bare barrier parity at approximately 803/801, 504/501, 303/301, and 103/101 for batches 1/10/100/1000; no Group cell regressed by 10%. This matches the prior Spike J conclusion that D7 owner grouping fixes the pre-existing convoy split, so no separate implementation remains."}	blake3:rbHVAHskvOzfJ-K4eM8RPaDDkITiulow31ZY5jD8c-A
1784032273642025	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsgg		item.move	bn-3pz	{"reason":"Resolved by flat-owner integration bn-2su","state":"done"}	blake3:jY81yW2UaRK7LkKWBQPieUzJL8ZkaHSn3KK01t_y7nY
1784032327430301	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExswg		item.comment	bn-o5b	{"body":"Roadmap planning complete after review of handoff.md, design.md, research 01-12, the spike reports, and the composed-decision report. Added 8 sequential phase goals and 40 new self-contained task bones; reparented 11 existing follow-ups instead of duplicating them. The graph preserves the admitted constraints: log is authoritative; owner is committer; B1/log striping/stride-8 globals are rejected; dedupe is exact; effects/checkpoints are discardable; v4 and media specialization remain gated decisions. bn admin verify/diagnose are clean and every phase plan reports zero scheduling violations. First ready work is bn-187h alongside bn-25c and bn-3m3."}	blake3:HfEVf8IFM3R6NoROD_rv5XsXjuuWYLlCBWnV8vV-qPo
1784033220619340	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtAg		item.update	bn-o5b	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe original implementation goal bn-agy completed the log-derived registry (bn-2di) and flat owner (bn-2su), then auto-closed because those were its only children. Its own accepted roadmap still has substantial work: finish the owner hot path, install dense resident state and exact dedupe, integrate active microblocks, land SegmentEffect/checkpoints, finish and default SealPack, remove Fjall, and make a deliberate v4 decision. The current engine has evolved beyond parts of the original design pack, so this successor goal starts with a code-to-roadmap authority audit rather than blindly implementing obsolete migration or dormant-MetaStore assumptions.\n\n# Outcome\nMess has one log-authoritative storage engine whose resident and sealed accelerators are rebuildable, bounded, and measured end to end. No production authority or required runtime path depends on Fjall. Every admitted Asterism component is integrated through LogEngine, recovery, sealing, observability, and fault tests—not left as a spike.\n\n# Constraints\n- Preserve one canonical writer/order/durability spine and publish only after the mode's accepted write/barrier point.\n- Keep B1 barrier pipelining, log striping, and stride-8 global pointers rejected unless wholly new evidence overturns their negative spikes.\n- Exactness is mandatory: no probabilistic dedupe answers, hidden metadata authority, or checkpoint-dependent correctness.\n- There are no existing stores to migrate; research/06 M0-M9 is historical analysis, not implementation scope.\n- v4 is a gated product decision, not assumed. If declined, the v3 system-record design must still make remaining state log-derived.\n- Use matched, batch-stratified composed benchmarks and committed raw evidence; isolated microbench wins do not waive end-to-end gates.\n\n# Success Metrics\n- Flat-owner append refinements retain Fjall-era wins and improve the measured residual API/direct-path costs.\n- Dense heads, microblocks, effects, checkpoints, and SealPack pass their spike-derived correctness, memory, startup, and throughput gates after production integration.\n- Snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe authority is represented canonically in the log or explicitly removed as dormant API.\n- The fjall dependency and obsolete MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend production surface are deleted after replacements land.\n- A final composed fresh-store matrix, recovery/RSS corpus, crash/fault suite, and adversarial review either admit the completed architecture or invoke predeclared kill/narrow criteria.\n\n# Primary Evidence\n- notes/mess-asterism/design.md sections 6-18 and 23\n- notes/mess-asterism/research/01-10 plus the code review/response in research/11-12\n- spikes/composed_decision/REPORT.md section 11\n- spikes/state_kernel_dense_heads, active_microblocks, segment_effect, epoch_dedupe, directory_tournament, and seal_pack reports\n- spikes/baseline_matrix/BN-2SU-REPORT.md and BN-2SU-FINAL.csv\n\n# Stop Criteria\nStop or narrow any custom structure that misses its composed gate, increases correctness surface without an end-to-end win, or causes the formal/crash suites to be bypassed.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose this goal manually only after all eight phase goals are manually closed, the final adoption report records ADMIT/NARROW/DECLINE and default status for every component, release checks pass, and no unresolved `risk:high` roadmap bone lacks an explicit deferral or replacement."}	blake3:cIiEiGCakbwt4lUouEC26_sq44bfFeCtpJ5GcNP0bJA
1784033220650893	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtQg		item.update	bn-k8qd	{"field":"title","value":"Freeze the pre-v4 log-derived control contract for Fjall retirement"}	blake3:oAD2r-t2ls-vd_68AWtpsFSDKKohXazSl_uOUQ4GgUk
1784033220655231	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtgg		item.update	bn-k8qd	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nFjall cannot be removed until snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and any admitted dedupe keys are canonical in the log. v3 can encode explicit system-stream events, as the registry already does, but they consume global positions. v4 control preludes avoid domain-position gaps and passed their spike, but add format/crash-model surface. There are no stores to migrate. Phase 9 remains the deliberate product/default decision for v4.\n\n# Goal\nFreeze the control-transition contract needed by Phase 7 to retire Fjall without prematurely deciding the Phase 9 v4 rollout. The default bridge is explicit v3 system records; pulling v4 forward is allowed only through an explicit ADR that rewires and collapses the later v4 sequence rather than leaving duplicate decisions.\n\n# Decision Inputs\nCompare atomicity, global-position semantics, expected-version behavior, batch/capsule-level idempotency, recovery scan cost, allocation-free validation, compatibility, downgrade safety, implementation sequencing, and maintenance burden. Reuse RegistryState-style strict folds; never make accelerators authoritative.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR covers snapshot install, projection checkpoint, dedupe key, registry, and control-only transition shapes.\n- [ ] Retry outcomes distinguish registration idempotence from event idempotence.\n- [ ] The v3 bridge states the accepted position-gap contract and how Phase 9 may supersede it.\n- [ ] If v4 is pulled forward, the ADR states the A5 amendment and batch-id rule and rewires/closes redundant Phase 9 bones immediately.\n- [ ] Phase 7 has one implementable canonical path and Phase 9 has one non-contradictory product decision.\n- [ ] Adversarial review answers crash-before/after-write/barrier and duplicate/retry cases.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes Fjall deletion achievable without smuggling in an unreviewed wire-format rollout or maintaining two competing control contracts."}	blake3:01dma__cfD8ow_HZ_T94Hut9j9Y86LRLX6cuOejndd4
1784033220677950	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtwg		item.update	bn-3ew9	{"field":"title","value":"Certify v4 crash safety, parser robustness, and compatibility"}	blake3:vPO3vH3QTGVcO_UnhHUozjHvWRwQpLxXh01EuZ5D8rA
1784033220681447	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuAg		item.update	bn-3ew9	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nLocal encode/decode success is insufficient for a storage format. Before measuring or defaulting v4, the production reader/writer must survive real owner writes, SIGKILL/torn boundaries, mixed versions, semantic differential replay, malformed input, and operational downgrade behavior.\n\n# Goal\nCertify v4 correctness, crash safety, parser robustness, mixed-version recovery, and downgrade refusal. This bone produces a correctness verdict only; performance and default policy are separate downstream gates.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] SIGKILL harness covers every write/barrier/publication boundary for user-only, control-only, and mixed capsules.\n- [ ] Differential fuzzing compares validate/decode/reference replay and includes overflow, reserved flags, corrupt lengths/counts/CRCs, and random truncation.\n- [ ] v3-only refusal, mixed v3/v4 recovery, verify/repair, and unsupported-feature errors pass end-to-end CLI tests.\n- [ ] Exhaustive small-state and randomized models prove prefix stability, no control/event split, no duplicate batch acceptance, and no control-only domain-position advance.\n- [ ] Sanitizer/Miri lanes cover the allocation-free validation boundary and malformed corpus.\n- [ ] Adversarial review records PASS/FAIL and every residual compatibility assumption.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents a wire-format improvement from reaching performance/default evaluation with an under-tested crash or downgrade model."}	blake3:ZxhExtNOhtPX8ZY7Sdqvn7Q6HNhmzRHK3MdgCL7_8y8
1784033220704110	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuQg		item.update	bn-2wso	{"field":"title","value":"Certify final composed semantics, crash safety, and recovery"}	blake3:EboQhtLzhLLxnZqfXekn4mIN3ppMfS42v8HNqxyirhk
1784033220707572	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExugg		item.update	bn-2wso	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe roadmap succeeds only if the fully composed production engine preserves durable semantics across components. Component-specific suites are inputs, but they may miss interactions among owner publication, dense state, dedupe, microblocks, effects/checkpoints, SealPack, and the admitted format policy.\n\n# Goal\nRun the final composed semantic, recovery, crash, corruption, and repair certification. Performance and the final admission verdict are separate downstream bones.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Differential/state-model tests cover append conflicts, retry/idempotency, registry, heads, snapshots, projections, global positions, stream reads, roll/seal, reopen, verify, repair, and canonical-prefix recovery.\n- [ ] Crash/SIGKILL/torn/corruption tests cover every durable publication boundary and admitted v3/v4/SealPack combination.\n- [ ] Checkpoint/effect/pack loss or corruption always falls back to canonical log state and produces the same semantic digest.\n- [ ] Slow-device, ENOSPC/EIO, cancellation, interrupted roll/seal/checkpoint, and repeated recovery cases retain bounded, explainable state.\n- [ ] Full workspace, sanitizer/Miri, Loom/formal, fuzz, and CLI end-to-end suites required by the touched surfaces are green.\n- [ ] Adversarial review issues an explicit PASS/FAIL and files bounded blockers for every failure.\n\n# Project Value\nProvides one cross-component correctness certificate before the architecture is judged or hardware-specialized."}	blake3:rhot8ReYQlNZ_HkHClz4qKUFgLrrtwlGA_k3nGPGx90
1784033220739742	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuwg		item.update	bn-7n39	{"field":"title","value":"Evaluate an optional io_uring I/O path"}	blake3:FytWtmXbnv7ZB4VTXZJf1OUdeMMCoPmFHlwAVD_i2oE
1784033220743247	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvAg		item.update	bn-7n39	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe research leaves asynchronous I/O as optional endgame work. It must be tested only after the portable engine is admitted, because io_uring may not become a correctness dependency or obscure a regression in the core owner/committer design.\n\n# Goal\nRun a bounded production-shaped io_uring submission/completion spike for the canonical append and selected pread paths, retaining a clean synchronous fallback.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Baseline is the final portable engine on identical durability semantics, batching, and workload topology.\n- [ ] Measure syscalls, wakeups, CPU, queue depth, throughput, p50/p99/p999, cancellation, partial completion, and error propagation.\n- [ ] Crash/durability behavior matches the synchronous path for Process/Os/Group modes and segment roll.\n- [ ] Unsupported kernels/filesystems and runtime setup failure fall back cleanly without changing durable metadata.\n- [ ] Report ends in ADMIT, NARROW, or DECLINE with raw evidence and explicit support/maintenance cost; losing code is not retained.\n\n# Project Value\nTests asynchronous I/O on its own merits without coupling it to device-placement research or the portable correctness model."}	blake3:_PNIuVhJIBBANYtOWHcaRUQY21T09u1gUgIma9zhwoU
1784033220766995	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvQg		item.update	bn-o5b	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:Pe1HJDf5ZLo4sB2hTOLdUFQBfcya2JGqx3fnahMvREo
1784033220780954	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvgg		item.update	bn-ogn	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:Sgtafx4z46yB3JAdrHzuSOUQfkxLhQCbpLlI7lNNUlE
1784033220797540	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvwg		item.update	bn-3ef	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:ckqIrVuaYyucvV0qDkcC59toQbWBh-f97sY1a4JBvbs
1784033220810969	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwAg		item.update	bn-1n7j	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:VFfQ8ZJBYlSYZZH2MjAHuakTXJ9V-VTWB2Zw5nX7lR0
1784033220824446	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwQg		item.update	bn-34as	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:uG1P6u_Igk2bDUnESPKQWIB1lFHy9WFkDDcdzat6Ooc
1784033220837877	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwgg		item.update	bn-35f2	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:xRpCi6hvXU-NdbATeREdoZ0NCsJ_NEwGPRR_VG2QjRo
1784033220851407	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwwg		item.update	bn-35fy	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:hHGP8wGQPq0nW9LJD67HS8AKFHWUJ0kD4uotXWdTAUw
1784033220864571	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxAg		item.update	bn-5hcg	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:rVe8V58EA0H8s4Q0h9afh10d-P3o-DglUfM6QiPSCbc
1784033220877888	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxQg		item.update	bn-3dos	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"goal:manual"}}	blake3:VKmJCfP4T70s6sAyJ-A5Ks_ASZZda_atqy2yfKwQ4F4
1784033220922046	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxgg		item.update	bn-ogn	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nThe post-spike design pack contains known stale claims: production dedupe is dormant, LogEngine reads no longer carry all-history payloads in Book, migration is moot, and the current Fjall dependency is concentrated in MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend rather than the flat append path. Before more code lands, future workers need one exact map of canonical authority, derived accelerators, public compatibility obligations, and the chosen v3/v4 control-record boundary.\\n\\n# Goal\\nFreeze the remaining implementation contract against the current code and accepted spike evidence. Resolve documentation contradictions, record the no-migration scope, and decide how snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and optional dedupe keys become log-derived without silently making v4 mandatory.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Current code ownership and every Fjall keyspace/API are classified as authoritative, derived, dormant, or removable.\\n- A written decision selects v3 system records or accelerated v4 controls for each remaining canonical transition and rewires later bones if needed.\\n- State-frontier, fairness/cancellation, exact dedupe, retention, and batch-stratified performance contracts are explicit.\\n- Design/research manifests identify amendments and historical documents unambiguously.\\n- Stale resolved bones are closed and relevant existing bones are filed under this roadmap.\\n\\n# Why\\nThis prevents implementing the old migration narrative or synthetic MetaStore assumptions after the engine has already changed.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after all five children are done, the authority audit and bridge-control ADR have adversarial review, the design/research amendments cross-reference the chosen contracts, and any resulting dependency rewiring has been revalidated with zero scheduling violations."}	blake3:RrxkFKJQG5iZZygbXD1nzun0k0y_jAzD1xRC66167Xo
1784033220963973	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExxwg		item.update	bn-3ef	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nbn-2su put the production append path under one owner and moved large pure frame preparation to producers, beating the Fjall-era engine. Profiling and the Asterism risk register still identify avoidable direct-path acknowledgements/allocations, borrowed-record copies for small batches, singleton retirement/publication, byte-ring fairness gaps, and one pwrite per batch.\\n\\n# Goal\\nComplete the reversible v3 flat-owner subset: owned/prepared submission, allocation-light direct commits, group-wide state publication, and evidence-gated coalesced writes, without B1 barrier overlap or any change to canonical ordering/durability semantics.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Public/facade owned append path avoids the defensive record clone while preserving the borrowed compatibility adapter.\\n- DirectCommitter has an ack-free result path with reusable bounded scratch.\\n- One committed group applies index/head effects and publishes one watermark before completing waiters.\\n- Backpressure fairness and dropped-waiter behavior are specified and model-tested.\\n- Coalesced pwritev is admitted only on matched production workloads and handles partial writes/roll/faults exactly.\\n- Full matrix retains all bn-2su gates and commits raw results.\\n\\n# Constraints\\nNo B1, no striped canonical log, no weakened CRC/barrier/publication ordering.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after bn-2l3n issues an evidence-backed ADMIT/NARROW/REVERT verdict, all correctness and cancellation/fairness suites pass, raw matched results are retained, and every declined conditional optimization is closed without leaving a dormant production branch."}	blake3:5uBGp0NqISgQgMIYbyPMePIbRRghr00Ok9udvW-hIDE
1784033221006273	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyAg		item.update	bn-1n7j	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nSpike A decisively admitted chunked dense head pages with packed AtomicU128 cells and a page-seqlock fallback. Spike G admitted exact epoch dedupe as a new feature, but research/11 correctly notes production dedupe is dormant today. Current Book still protects registry projections and HashMap heads under one mutex; MetaStore exposes Fjall tables whose runtime role must not be overstated.\\n\\n# Goal\\nMove live heads and latest-value slots into bounded direct pages owned by the flat owner, then add exact epoch dedupe only behind an explicit public semantic contract. Retire Book.heads after differential shadowing while keeping RegistryState canonical and strict.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Dense pages select lock-free packed cells by capability and use the verified seqlock fallback otherwise.\\n- Head reads/publish/recovery shadow-compare against the old representation before cutover.\\n- Book is reduced to registry/name concerns or replaced by explicit owner/read projections; no global head mutex remains.\\n- Dedupe semantics, scope, retention boundary, retry outcomes, collision exactness, and batch-level API are normative.\\n- Active/frozen epoch implementation passes forced-collision and recovery/checkpoint loss corpora.\\n- Composed memory/latency gates are measured; synthetic component wins alone do not admit integration.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after dense-head shadowing reports zero semantic divergence, the old head mutex/path is removed or explicitly retained with cause, exact dedupe has a reviewed public contract or explicit decline, recovery/crash tests pass, and composed memory/latency gates are recorded."}	blake3:rRniPPM1ETVedDho5ONBu9tAcjdwCelkAwfwvtmsJ_U
1784033221048571	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyQg		item.update	bn-34as	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nSpike F measured 2.2–5.3x apply throughput and admitted active pointer microblocks, while Spike J refuted stride-8 global pointers because each header pread made seeks 10–17x slower. Production should therefore replace only the stream-side ActiveIndex representation and retain a dense stride-1 global path.\\n\\n# Goal\\nIntegrate append-only stream microblocks with safe reader publication and reclamation across live append, recovery rebuild, segment roll, and sealed/hot union reads.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Production microblock arena uses bounded blocks, exact lookup, and deterministic fallback.\\n- Reader publication/reclamation is covered by Loom and real-thread roll/reclaim tests.\\n- Stream reads and head/tail resolution use microblocks; global active reads remain stride-1 and are rebenchmarked.\\n- Recovery uses the same apply path as live publication.\\n- End-to-end apply, read p99, memory, and contention gates meet the admitted Spike F thresholds; otherwise retain the simpler ActiveIndex.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after bn-1cwy records ADMIT or DECLINE, Loom plus real-thread publication/roll/reclamation tests pass, stream/global reads match the reference corpus, stride-1 globals remain intact, and the losing representation is not left ambiguously selectable."}	blake3:MfHsJgCpo5GiSAmLvnDzXw3VALOx08lxizDmRhHtsgA
1784033221091681	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExygg		item.update	bn-35f2	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nSpike D proved compact SegmentEffect construction, parallel effect build, sequential apply, and content-addressed incremental checkpoints. Its review amendments require explicit bottom/existence tracking, allocator first/last boundaries, one canonical Effect definition, and discardable corruption fallback. Fjall can disappear only after snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe authority is canonical in the log and their resident heads are rebuildable.\\n\\n# Goal\\nIntegrate per-segment algebraic effects and Merkle-page kernel checkpoints into sealing/recovery, make remaining metadata transitions log-derived, replace FjallSnapshotBackend/MetaStore runtime roles, and remove the fjall dependency.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Canonical effects cover stream transitions, registry, snapshot/checkpoint slots, optional dedupe, and all named frontiers with proven composition laws.\\n- Seal builds effects in parallel where measured useful, validates them, and records them as rebuildable SealPack sections.\\n- Content-addressed checkpoint pages/manifests/current pointers install atomically; interrupted GC and every corruption class fall back to canonical log recovery.\\n- Recovery selects checkpoint plus suffix, validates anchors/digests, and meets the large-corpus startup gate without payload decode.\\n- Snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe control transitions follow the Phase 3 authority decision.\\n- MetaStore and FjallSnapshotBackend are replaced or explicitly demoted to non-production compatibility; fjall is removed from the workspace dependency graph.\\n- Formal, randomized differential, and fault-filesystem suites show identical canonical state.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after checkpoint/effect corruption falls back to identical canonical state, snapshot/projection/dedupe transitions follow the Phase 3 ADR, bn-fj34 removes Fjall from code and the dependency graph, startup/RSS gates pass, and adversarial review traces every former authority to log bytes."}	blake3:l-rAnkpccEjt6bPeObkLE6zCHAC72zyxLP6Q2vz9raU
1784033221134875	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExywg		item.update	bn-35fy	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nSealPack and the density-adaptive bitrank directory are already integrated behind EngineOptions::seal_pack, default off. Default-on is blocked by measured reopen RSS growth and missing footer-to-pack identity binding. The HashMap fallback also has a proven foldhash win, and sidecar candidate lifecycle has an open re-seal bug.\\n\\n# Goal\\nResolve the known SealPack correctness/RSS blockers, validate the production directory chooser and bounded read path at scale, enable SealPack by default, and simplify legacy sidecars only after fallback/repair obligations are clear.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Large optional payload sections remain pread/cache-backed rather than resident; 2M/10M reopen RSS meets the bounded-memory gate.\\n- Segment footer binds exact pack identity and load/recovery verify the trust chain.\\n- Dense bitrank and sparse fallback selection is observable, deterministic, and exact on real segment distributions.\\n- Refuted/missing packs are re-queued safely for re-seal.\\n- Default-on matrix covers reopen, cold/warm reads, corruption, partial install, parity, and seal time.\\n- Legacy sidecar read/write paths are retained only where an explicit compatibility or repair requirement remains.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after known RSS, identity, and re-seal blockers are fixed, bn-1yor records a green ADMIT/default-on verdict, SealPack fallback and legacy-sidecar lifetimes are explicit, raw scale/fault evidence is retained, and adversarial review approves the trust chain."}	blake3:nKS-1Q1LcFboQ2-vHWde_iVPR4OTWUWn-6IaPvXRkLk
1784033221177138	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzAg		item.update	bn-5hcg	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nv4 control-prelude capsules passed their spike and are merged flag-off. The old migration risk is moot because there are no existing stores, while v3 registry events now create API-visible gaps in domain global positions. Conversely, v4 adds format and crash-model surface and is not required for the reversible state-kernel subset.\\n\\n# Goal\\nMake an evidence-backed v4 adoption decision after the state kernel is real. If admitted, integrate allocation-free validation, control/event atomicity, control-only capsules, mixed-version recovery, and downgrade refusal. If declined, freeze the v3 system-record control contract and close conditional implementation bones without ambiguity.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Decision compares position semantics, control needs, scan overhead, maintenance surface, and composed benefit—not migration concerns.\\n- Normative format wording includes the safety-preserving A5 amendment, contiguous batch-id recovery rule, frontiers, and per-capsule idempotency semantics.\\n- Admitted implementation preserves no-control scan overhead below the spike gate and passes exhaustive/random torn-write models.\\n- Registration/snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe controls reuse canonical state machines and never split from same-capsule events.\\n- v3 readers remain supported as decided; older writers refuse unsafe read-write open after v4 bytes.\\n- SIGKILL, validate-vs-decode differential fuzz, marker flags, and downgrade cases are complete before default-on.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after bn-1ojm records the v4 ADMIT/DECLINE decision; declined conditional bones are closed, or an admitted v4 has a frozen spec, production writer/reader, correctness certification, performance report, and reviewed default/compatibility policy."}	blake3:um6Wga8ygn5M1y2rJDQ3Upfsh2jdRbStpyj2uXHN6Nc
1784033221219903	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzQg		item.update	bn-3dos	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\\nAsterism admission is based on composed outcomes, not the sum of spike headlines. Once the main structures are integrated, the project needs one final reproducible evidence bundle and only then should consider device-specific O_DSYNC, FDP/ZNS, io_uring, or other media specialization.\\n\\n# Goal\\nRun the final correctness/performance/startup/RSS/durability program, close the roadmap against predeclared kill criteria, and make explicit adopt/decline decisions for optional media paths.\\n\\n# Acceptance Criteria\\n- Fresh-store matched AB/BA matrix is batch/payload/writer stratified with raw rows, p50/p99, barrier counts, CPU/load provenance, and Fjall-era/bare comparisons.\\n- Large-corpus checkpoint open, recovery fallback, resident RSS, disk/write amplification, and seal backlog are measured.\\n- Crash/torn/SIGKILL/formal/differential suites produce a release evidence bundle.\\n- Observability reports owner saturation, backpressure, checkpoint/effect fallback, seal backlog, and cache behavior.\\n- Optional O_DSYNC/FDP/ZNS/io_uring work proceeds only after device capability probes and independent gates; negative results are recorded and code is not retained.\\n- The top-level roadmap is closed only when every admitted child is integrated or explicitly declined with evidence.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after independent semantic/crash, scale/RSS/write-amplification, and hot-path performance evidence supports the final admission verdict; optional O_DSYNC/io_uring/FDP-ZNS work is explicitly admitted or declined; the adoption report and cleanup pass release checks; and no high-risk blocker is unresolved."}	blake3:1Crd9xdMJsSSv3Lzq5wTH-85pKqH466adc185a72-D4
1784033232681883	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzgg		item.create	bn-31f8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCorrect v4 bytes are not automatically worthwhile. The prelude spike measured no-control parsing locally; the production decision needs the real owner, mixed controls, recovery, and resident-state costs against the approved v3 bridge.\n\n# Goal\nMeasure v4's isolated and composed cost with matched semantics and raw evidence after correctness certification passes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Compare v3 and v4 append throughput/tails for user-only, control-only, and mixed capsules across batch sizes and writer counts.\n- [ ] Measure no-control and mixed-control scan throughput, bytes/event, CPU, allocations, peak RSS, cold open, and suffix recovery.\n- [ ] Preserve the spike gate for no-control scan overhead or explicitly amend it with product-level evidence.\n- [ ] Use AB/BA or randomized matched order, quiet-host provenance, confidence intervals, exact commands, and committed raw rows.\n- [ ] Attribute costs to framing, validation, controls, recovery, or compatibility rather than reporting one blended number.\n\n# Project Value\nSeparates format safety from the evidence needed to justify its production cost.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","benchmark","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"m","title":"Benchmark v4 append, scan, recovery, and memory cost"}	blake3:Fgqv5x9M97l9RHAaUiN3WFNlZxaQKCXcQF15hHHJxLo
1784033232801677	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzwg		item.create	bn-1zm0	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase 9 begins with an admission decision and, if admitted, produces a normative implementation, correctness certificate, and performance report. A final policy decision must translate that evidence into actual writer defaults and support guarantees.\n\n# Goal\nRecord and implement the v4 default/read/write/downgrade policy, or keep it default-off/declined with conditional code removed or explicitly bounded.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Correctness certification and performance report are linked and their failed gates cannot be waived silently.\n- [ ] Policy states default writer version, supported reader versions, mixed-store behavior, downgrade refusal, feature negotiation, and fixture lifetime.\n- [ ] Config, CLI inspect/doctor/verify/repair, docs, and end-to-end tests agree with the policy.\n- [ ] If default-on, rollback and unsupported-binary errors are actionable; if declined/default-off, speculative production paths are removed or assigned an owner/lifetime.\n- [ ] Lead and adversarial review record the final ADMIT/NARROW/DECLINE verdict.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents a completed v4 implementation from lingering in an ambiguous flag state.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","v4","gate","architecture","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-5hcg","size":"s","title":"Set the v4 production default and compatibility policy"}	blake3:txuynsLFejcH516FWXKyBBD-SlLGJxuPpiMVEz43aAs
1784033232927217	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0Ag		item.create	bn-37jh	{"description":"# Context & Background\nLarge-scale startup/RSS testing and semantic certification do not replace a matched hot-path comparison. The final engine must demonstrate that component wins compose across the workloads where the Fjall-era and bare-log baselines differ most.\n\n# Goal\nRun the final portable-engine throughput, latency, CPU, allocation, and barrier comparison with batch-size results kept stratified.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Cover 1/4/16/64 writers, representative payloads, hot/Zipf/distinct streams, batch 1/10/100/1000, and Process/Os/Group/durable modes as supported.\n- [ ] Report throughput, p50/p99/p999, CPU, allocations, bytes/event, barriers, queue delay, and owner saturation separately by stratum.\n- [ ] Compare bare log, Fjall-era engine, post-flat-owner checkpoint, and final portable engine with matched semantics and topology.\n- [ ] Preserve raw rows, exact binaries/commits/config, quiet-host telemetry, randomized or AB/BA order, and uncertainty.\n- [ ] Any regression beyond the phase gates is attributed, reverted/default-disabled, or represented by a bounded blocker.\n\n# Project Value\nAnswers whether the complete portable engine actually crushes the Fjall-era engine without hiding weak cells in aggregates.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","benchmark","perf","release","risk:low"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Run the final batch-stratified Asterism throughput and tail-latency comparison"}	blake3:kBhV_-mJbz2I90gIaicRr5iq6Xru-5ojK8en2A8H1rM
1784033233046305	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0Qg		item.create	bn-2z2j	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCorrectness, scale, and hot-path reports deliberately produce separate evidence. The project needs one explicit synthesis before optional device specialization or cleanup can begin.\n\n# Goal\nDecide ADMIT/NARROW/DECLINE for the composed portable engine and freeze its supported default configuration from the independent evidence bundles.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Semantic/crash certification, scale/RSS/write-amplification report, and batch-stratified performance report are complete and linked.\n- [ ] Every component has an ADMIT/NARROW/DECLINE/default status and any interaction regression has an owner or is disabled/reverted.\n- [ ] Default feature/format/durability/fallback configuration is explicit and matches tests and CLI behavior.\n- [ ] Fjall-era and bare-log comparisons use matched semantics; missed gates are not averaged away.\n- [ ] Lead and adversarial review approve the verdict before media experiments or final cleanup.\n\n# Project Value\nCreates a single auditable release decision from independent correctness and performance evidence.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","gate","release","architecture","risk:high"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"s","title":"Issue the final Asterism admission and default-configuration verdict"}	blake3:gAQNn_fM80o1aZA8ESs_hmZYPB1D8Xz5f-3J6yryd8U
1784033233167831	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0gg		item.create	bn-32xr	{"description":"# Context & Background\nFlexible Data Placement and Zoned Namespaces may reduce write amplification, but their lifecycle, recovery, and hardware constraints are distinct from io_uring. They must remain optional physical placement, never canonical metadata authority.\n\n# Goal\nOn supported hardware or a faithful emulator, evaluate placement of active log, sealed segments, checkpoint pages, and discardable accelerator writes against the admitted portable engine.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Define placement/lifetime classes and a synchronous ordinary-filesystem fallback before benchmarking.\n- [ ] Measure device/host write amplification, garbage collection, space utilization, throughput/tails, recovery, reset behavior, and operational complexity.\n- [ ] Power-loss/error/reopen tests prove canonical log/control bytes remain sufficient and misplaced/lost accelerators rebuild.\n- [ ] Unsupported devices and partial capability expose a typed fallback with no durable-format dependency.\n- [ ] Report ends in ADMIT, NARROW, or DECLINE with raw evidence, hardware provenance, and support cost; losing code is not retained.\n\n# Project Value\nTests media placement independently without contaminating the portable storage model.\n","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","roadmap","spike","media"],"parent":"bn-3dos","size":"m","title":"Evaluate optional FDP/ZNS placement for discardable and canonical writes"}	blake3:8QOYJaGUT8i-qeClnA4oi5LJEB9sfoRtp613NEA0qik
1784033242382521	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx0wg		item.update	bn-ogn	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe post-spike design pack contains known stale claims: production dedupe is dormant, LogEngine reads no longer carry all-history payloads in Book, migration is moot, and the current Fjall dependency is concentrated in MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend rather than the flat append path. Before more code lands, future workers need one exact map of canonical authority, derived accelerators, public compatibility obligations, and the chosen v3/v4 control-record boundary.\n\n# Goal\nFreeze the remaining implementation contract against the current code and accepted spike evidence. Resolve documentation contradictions, record the no-migration scope, and decide how snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and optional dedupe keys become log-derived without silently making v4 mandatory.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Current code ownership and every Fjall keyspace/API are classified as authoritative, derived, dormant, or removable.\n- A written decision selects v3 system records or accelerated v4 controls for each remaining canonical transition and rewires later bones if needed.\n- State-frontier, fairness/cancellation, exact dedupe, retention, and batch-stratified performance contracts are explicit.\n- Design/research manifests identify amendments and historical documents unambiguously.\n- Stale resolved bones are closed and relevant existing bones are filed under this roadmap.\n\n# Why\nThis prevents implementing the old migration narrative or synthetic MetaStore assumptions after the engine has already changed.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after all five children are done, the authority audit and bridge-control ADR have adversarial review, the design/research amendments cross-reference the chosen contracts, and any resulting dependency rewiring has been revalidated with zero scheduling violations."}	blake3:sbXnoKciR0PXxYOXLE-NMotqLRwXjprZgmjibYQ2Uls
1784033242434721	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1Ag		item.update	bn-3ef	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nbn-2su put the production append path under one owner and moved large pure frame preparation to producers, beating the Fjall-era engine. Profiling and the Asterism risk register still identify avoidable direct-path acknowledgements/allocations, borrowed-record copies for small batches, singleton retirement/publication, byte-ring fairness gaps, and one pwrite per batch.\n\n# Goal\nComplete the reversible v3 flat-owner subset: owned/prepared submission, allocation-light direct commits, group-wide state publication, and evidence-gated coalesced writes, without B1 barrier overlap or any change to canonical ordering/durability semantics.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Public/facade owned append path avoids the defensive record clone while preserving the borrowed compatibility adapter.\n- DirectCommitter has an ack-free result path with reusable bounded scratch.\n- One committed group applies index/head effects and publishes one watermark before completing waiters.\n- Backpressure fairness and dropped-waiter behavior are specified and model-tested.\n- Coalesced pwritev is admitted only on matched production workloads and handles partial writes/roll/faults exactly.\n- Full matrix retains all bn-2su gates and commits raw results.\n\n# Constraints\nNo B1, no striped canonical log, no weakened CRC/barrier/publication ordering.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after bn-2l3n issues an evidence-backed ADMIT/NARROW/REVERT verdict, all correctness and cancellation/fairness suites pass, raw matched results are retained, and every declined conditional optimization is closed without leaving a dormant production branch."}	blake3:FggVRi37WAteavB96SbeJQPt3BJ2qdZ2p-PZpkZzvHI
1784033242479004	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1Qg		item.update	bn-1n7j	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSpike A decisively admitted chunked dense head pages with packed AtomicU128 cells and a page-seqlock fallback. Spike G admitted exact epoch dedupe as a new feature, but research/11 correctly notes production dedupe is dormant today. Current Book still protects registry projections and HashMap heads under one mutex; MetaStore exposes Fjall tables whose runtime role must not be overstated.\n\n# Goal\nMove live heads and latest-value slots into bounded direct pages owned by the flat owner, then add exact epoch dedupe only behind an explicit public semantic contract. Retire Book.heads after differential shadowing while keeping RegistryState canonical and strict.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Dense pages select lock-free packed cells by capability and use the verified seqlock fallback otherwise.\n- Head reads/publish/recovery shadow-compare against the old representation before cutover.\n- Book is reduced to registry/name concerns or replaced by explicit owner/read projections; no global head mutex remains.\n- Dedupe semantics, scope, retention boundary, retry outcomes, collision exactness, and batch-level API are normative.\n- Active/frozen epoch implementation passes forced-collision and recovery/checkpoint loss corpora.\n- Composed memory/latency gates are measured; synthetic component wins alone do not admit integration.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after dense-head shadowing reports zero semantic divergence, the old head mutex/path is removed or explicitly retained with cause, exact dedupe has a reviewed public contract or explicit decline, recovery/crash tests pass, and composed memory/latency gates are recorded."}	blake3:duMDatKdwl1xPegcM_O06cy72hczGADqwkeqz3JW_iA
1784033242523153	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1gg		item.update	bn-34as	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSpike F measured 2.2–5.3x apply throughput and admitted active pointer microblocks, while Spike J refuted stride-8 global pointers because each header pread made seeks 10–17x slower. Production should therefore replace only the stream-side ActiveIndex representation and retain a dense stride-1 global path.\n\n# Goal\nIntegrate append-only stream microblocks with safe reader publication and reclamation across live append, recovery rebuild, segment roll, and sealed/hot union reads.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Production microblock arena uses bounded blocks, exact lookup, and deterministic fallback.\n- Reader publication/reclamation is covered by Loom and real-thread roll/reclaim tests.\n- Stream reads and head/tail resolution use microblocks; global active reads remain stride-1 and are rebenchmarked.\n- Recovery uses the same apply path as live publication.\n- End-to-end apply, read p99, memory, and contention gates meet the admitted Spike F thresholds; otherwise retain the simpler ActiveIndex.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after bn-1cwy records ADMIT or DECLINE, Loom plus real-thread publication/roll/reclamation tests pass, stream/global reads match the reference corpus, stride-1 globals remain intact, and the losing representation is not left ambiguously selectable."}	blake3:bb6CX146m3FhM-t2OwxTohB92nCcCc9JEmISu_4KZdM
1784033242565329	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1wg		item.update	bn-35f2	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSpike D proved compact SegmentEffect construction, parallel effect build, sequential apply, and content-addressed incremental checkpoints. Its review amendments require explicit bottom/existence tracking, allocator first/last boundaries, one canonical Effect definition, and discardable corruption fallback. Fjall can disappear only after snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe authority is canonical in the log and their resident heads are rebuildable.\n\n# Goal\nIntegrate per-segment algebraic effects and Merkle-page kernel checkpoints into sealing/recovery, make remaining metadata transitions log-derived, replace FjallSnapshotBackend/MetaStore runtime roles, and remove the fjall dependency.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Canonical effects cover stream transitions, registry, snapshot/checkpoint slots, optional dedupe, and all named frontiers with proven composition laws.\n- Seal builds effects in parallel where measured useful, validates them, and records them as rebuildable SealPack sections.\n- Content-addressed checkpoint pages/manifests/current pointers install atomically; interrupted GC and every corruption class fall back to canonical log recovery.\n- Recovery selects checkpoint plus suffix, validates anchors/digests, and meets the large-corpus startup gate without payload decode.\n- Snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe control transitions follow the Phase 3 authority decision.\n- MetaStore and FjallSnapshotBackend are replaced or explicitly demoted to non-production compatibility; fjall is removed from the workspace dependency graph.\n- Formal, randomized differential, and fault-filesystem suites show identical canonical state.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after checkpoint/effect corruption falls back to identical canonical state, snapshot/projection/dedupe transitions follow the Phase 3 ADR, bn-fj34 removes Fjall from code and the dependency graph, startup/RSS gates pass, and adversarial review traces every former authority to log bytes."}	blake3:tTynPyFAmydBdtTvTInsWSJv0yM1bMig4iMIebh9AC4
1784033242608166	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2Ag		item.update	bn-35fy	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSealPack and the density-adaptive bitrank directory are already integrated behind EngineOptions::seal_pack, default off. Default-on is blocked by measured reopen RSS growth and missing footer-to-pack identity binding. The HashMap fallback also has a proven foldhash win, and sidecar candidate lifecycle has an open re-seal bug.\n\n# Goal\nResolve the known SealPack correctness/RSS blockers, validate the production directory chooser and bounded read path at scale, enable SealPack by default, and simplify legacy sidecars only after fallback/repair obligations are clear.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Large optional payload sections remain pread/cache-backed rather than resident; 2M/10M reopen RSS meets the bounded-memory gate.\n- Segment footer binds exact pack identity and load/recovery verify the trust chain.\n- Dense bitrank and sparse fallback selection is observable, deterministic, and exact on real segment distributions.\n- Refuted/missing packs are re-queued safely for re-seal.\n- Default-on matrix covers reopen, cold/warm reads, corruption, partial install, parity, and seal time.\n- Legacy sidecar read/write paths are retained only where an explicit compatibility or repair requirement remains.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after known RSS, identity, and re-seal blockers are fixed, bn-1yor records a green ADMIT/default-on verdict, SealPack fallback and legacy-sidecar lifetimes are explicit, raw scale/fault evidence is retained, and adversarial review approves the trust chain."}	blake3:icJjm5f9x5oJWGiJcbj0RaxZG28hY4qJ1HWn6J5fIwg
1784033242650615	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2Qg		item.update	bn-5hcg	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nv4 control-prelude capsules passed their spike and are merged flag-off. The old migration risk is moot because there are no existing stores, while v3 registry events now create API-visible gaps in domain global positions. Conversely, v4 adds format and crash-model surface and is not required for the reversible state-kernel subset.\n\n# Goal\nMake an evidence-backed v4 adoption decision after the state kernel is real. If admitted, integrate allocation-free validation, control/event atomicity, control-only capsules, mixed-version recovery, and downgrade refusal. If declined, freeze the v3 system-record control contract and close conditional implementation bones without ambiguity.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Decision compares position semantics, control needs, scan overhead, maintenance surface, and composed benefit—not migration concerns.\n- Normative format wording includes the safety-preserving A5 amendment, contiguous batch-id recovery rule, frontiers, and per-capsule idempotency semantics.\n- Admitted implementation preserves no-control scan overhead below the spike gate and passes exhaustive/random torn-write models.\n- Registration/snapshot/checkpoint/dedupe controls reuse canonical state machines and never split from same-capsule events.\n- v3 readers remain supported as decided; older writers refuse unsafe read-write open after v4 bytes.\n- SIGKILL, validate-vs-decode differential fuzz, marker flags, and downgrade cases are complete before default-on.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after bn-1ojm records the v4 ADMIT/DECLINE decision; declined conditional bones are closed, or an admitted v4 has a frozen spec, production writer/reader, correctness certification, performance report, and reviewed default/compatibility policy."}	blake3:SbnFvTYQkfW0GncGHhNR32WLG1sC400qLmHqjm5YF70
1784033242693379	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2gg		item.update	bn-3dos	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nAsterism admission is based on composed outcomes, not the sum of spike headlines. Once the main structures are integrated, the project needs one final reproducible evidence bundle and only then should consider device-specific O_DSYNC, FDP/ZNS, io_uring, or other media specialization.\n\n# Goal\nRun the final correctness/performance/startup/RSS/durability program, close the roadmap against predeclared kill criteria, and make explicit adopt/decline decisions for optional media paths.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Fresh-store matched AB/BA matrix is batch/payload/writer stratified with raw rows, p50/p99, barrier counts, CPU/load provenance, and Fjall-era/bare comparisons.\n- Large-corpus checkpoint open, recovery fallback, resident RSS, disk/write amplification, and seal backlog are measured.\n- Crash/torn/SIGKILL/formal/differential suites produce a release evidence bundle.\n- Observability reports owner saturation, backpressure, checkpoint/effect fallback, seal backlog, and cache behavior.\n- Optional O_DSYNC/FDP/ZNS/io_uring work proceeds only after device capability probes and independent gates; negative results are recorded and code is not retained.\n- The top-level roadmap is closed only when every admitted child is integrated or explicitly declined with evidence.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after independent semantic/crash, scale/RSS/write-amplification, and hot-path performance evidence supports the final admission verdict; optional O_DSYNC/io_uring/FDP-ZNS work is explicitly admitted or declined; the adoption report and cleanup pass release checks; and no high-risk blocker is unresolved."}	blake3:f3MyeOjlVqjypJ57e2TQEpgYXHHjKEpCiNi6d5U7Y9E
