1784033265110909	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx6wg		item.link	bn-2m8n	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-32xr"}	blake3:F55YuTkAiqNfaLQlEHLgOI_nbu173RPYlOjhxpwOSuQ
1784033360219642	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx7Ag		item.update	bn-ogn	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe post-spike design pack contains known stale claims: production dedupe is dormant, LogEngine reads no longer carry all-history payloads in Book, migration is moot, and the current Fjall dependency is concentrated in MetaStore/FjallSnapshotBackend rather than the flat append path. Before more code lands, future workers need one exact map of canonical authority, derived accelerators, public compatibility obligations, and the pre-v4 bridge needed to retire Fjall.\n\n# Goal\nFreeze the remaining implementation contract against the current code and accepted spike evidence. Resolve documentation contradictions, record the no-migration scope, and define how snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and optional dedupe keys become log-derived without silently making the later v4 product decision here.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Current code ownership and every Fjall keyspace/API are classified as authoritative, derived, dormant, or removable.\n- A written ADR freezes one Phase 7 bridge for remaining canonical transitions; v3 is the default, while any decision to pull v4 forward must rewire and collapse the later v4 sequence.\n- State-frontier, fairness/cancellation, exact dedupe, retention, and batch-stratified performance contracts are explicit.\n- Design/research manifests identify amendments and historical documents unambiguously.\n- Stale resolved bones are closed and relevant existing bones are filed under this roadmap.\n\n# Why\nThis prevents implementing the old migration narrative, synthetic MetaStore assumptions, or two contradictory v4 decisions after the engine has already changed.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after all five children are done, the authority audit and bridge-control ADR have adversarial review, the design/research amendments cross-reference the chosen contracts, and any resulting dependency rewiring has been revalidated with zero scheduling violations."}	blake3:OmkGpt8MSocwWfeqRgJr6-2w_nsHkBIUBKh8WdBfRy0
1784033360246344	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx7Qg		item.update	bn-2ctq	{"field":"title","value":"Decide and specify exact batch-idempotency semantics"}	blake3:4rU8mu8oPfqIoXGsx4V3o4uZGqp94-AFiC7Ly29jcf4
1784033360249748	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx7gg		item.update	bn-2ctq	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nProduction LogEngine does not currently use MetaStore dedupe and the public append API carries no idempotency key. Asterism dedupe is therefore a new product capability, not a migration. Spike G admitted an exact epoch structure mechanically, but it did not establish that the public semantics and ongoing resource cost are worth shipping. v4's one DedupeKey per capsule implies batch-level idempotency; a v3 bridge must provide equivalent canonical atomicity.\n\n# Goal\nMake an explicit ADMIT or DECLINE decision for batch idempotency and, if admitted, freeze the public/internal contract before storage implementation: scope, key limits, one-key-per-batch semantics, original-result return, window definition, retention, expiry, collision exactness, and recovery outcomes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Decision states the user/job value, supported retry scenario, resource envelope, and why engine-level idempotency is preferable to caller-level handling.\n- [ ] API/ADR states stream/global scope, maximum key size, batch-level granularity, and whether per-event keys are rejected or deferred.\n- [ ] Retry order is resolve registration, exact dedupe, expected version; a matching live key returns the original commit.\n- [ ] No/expired key conflicts normally and never silently duplicates an event.\n- [ ] Segment deletion preserves full keys until the defined retention frontier; approximate structures are negative accelerators only and positives verify full canonical keys.\n- [ ] v3/v4 canonical encoding, crash atomicity, adversarial-key/collision behavior, namespace abuse, and memory/disk caps receive security and durability review.\n- [ ] If DECLINE, downstream dedupe implementation bones are closed with the rationale and no dormant production API/table remains.\n\n# Project Value\nPrevents a fast component spike from defining an expensive or unsafe product feature by inertia."}	blake3:4VOE2nYq2pTkFBdK0JDJAYKKBoy4ylB34Xqk9iJ0bZE
1784033360272946	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx7wg		item.update	bn-2r8	{"field":"title","value":"Close the four production v4 review gaps from Spike E"}	blake3:noi_npB28cJ8ZB1SlEIQ8CE7eEQLlyCuqKrEMRnNIOI
1784033360276351	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8Ag		item.update	bn-2r8	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe bn-9mw adversarial review left four bounded production gaps that could not be completed before a real v4 committer/reader existed. They are documented in spikes/capsule_v4_prelude/REPORT.md and must be closed before the broader v4 certification runs.\n\n# Goal\nClose the four concrete review gaps: real-writer SIGKILL coverage, v3-only open-time refusal, reserved marker validation, and validate-vs-decode differential fuzzing.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] SIGKILL harness exercises every v4 committer write/barrier/publication frontier for user-only, control-only, and mixed capsules.\n- [ ] A v3-only/unsupported binary refuses a directory containing v4 segments at open with a typed actionable error before mutation.\n- [ ] Reader rejects unknown required marker flags and nonzero reserved fields according to the normative compatibility mask.\n- [ ] Differential fuzz target proves validate_capsule and decode/materialize accept exactly the same corpus and semantic boundaries.\n- [ ] Regression seeds are committed and the focused plus workspace verification required by the v4 certification are green.\n\n# Project Value\nTurns review notes into executable prerequisites rather than relying on the final matrix to rediscover them."}	blake3:KdZDt9AeQt-4WnlMHCY29LJ9TXBb7QjxzhXZ-RgXSyo
1784033360299133	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8Qg		item.update	bn-30u	{"field":"title","value":"Make refuted sealed candidates converge to safe re-seal"}	blake3:TXPk2v0p1k2D1-fwkylmcNVqH_fzvJ7h3KCoalxsbwU
1784033360302508	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8gg		item.update	bn-30u	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nA footerless or refuted sealed-index candidate currently remains on disk, is re-evaluated on every reopen, and is never re-queued for sealing. The segment serves correctly from raw scan, but the engine can loop forever through the same failed candidate and never regain the admitted sealed path.\n\n# Goal\nDefine and implement an idempotent candidate lifecycle that quarantines/removes a refuted sidecar, continues serving canonical raw bytes, and enqueues the rolled segment exactly as needed for a fresh seal.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Refuted candidates receive a durable, deterministic cleanup/quarantine action and are not reconsidered unchanged on every reopen.\n- [ ] Rolled raw segment is re-queued for sealing without duplicate unbounded jobs or reopen loops.\n- [ ] Crash between refutation, cleanup, enqueue, and new-pack install converges after repeated reopen.\n- [ ] Missing/corrupt candidate never blocks raw reads, verify, repair, or later successful sealing.\n- [ ] Tests cover footerless, identity mismatch, truncated/corrupt candidate, interrupted cleanup, repeated reopen, and successful re-seal.\n- [ ] Metrics/doctor state expose refutation reason, fallback, and pending re-seal.\n\n# Project Value\nRestores the sealed optimization automatically while keeping raw log bytes authoritative."}	blake3:LxAyY9CyeUv45BtJabXFF_l9CzjwEXDWkmmJ2GJLH_M
1784033360326241	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8wg		item.update	bn-11g	{"field":"title","value":"Bind exact SealPack identity into the segment footer"}	blake3:up52A7RSC2V2tCgjIfvMp4msw5gpIXvzUwN_s_Q-0us
1784033360333846	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9Ag		item.update	bn-11g	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe segment footer currently trusts a SealPack on coverage match alone; it does not name the pack identity required by research/04's trust chain. Coverage-only trust was accepted only while SealPack remained default-off. The identity is the stable header+directory hash because the directory commits to section CRCs/content-hash prefixes.\n\n# Goal\nSpecify, encode, and validate exact pack identity in the segment trailer extension without weakening install ordering, raw fallback, or the explicit legacy compatibility policy.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Normative spec defines identity bytes, hash domain/version, trailer encoding, and legacy-footer behavior.\n- [ ] Complete pack is durable and hash-verified before a footer naming it can become the accepted installation record.\n- [ ] Open/recovery reject a missing, mismatched, truncated, or corrupt named pack and fall back to canonical raw bytes without trusting another same-coverage pack.\n- [ ] Tests cover torn pack/footer ordering, stale replacement, copied wrong pack, identity-bit corruption, re-seal, legacy footer, verify, and repair.\n- [ ] CLI inspection reports expected/observed identity and the fallback reason.\n- [ ] Adversarial review approves the trust chain before SealPack default-on.\n\n# Project Value\nMakes the installed pack cryptographically specific rather than merely plausible for the segment range."}	blake3:L_PxQ7es9TTUdbyqIr19iHpILpoA5YUSd-3iHQgsrAI
1784033360356436	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9Qg		item.update	bn-dcr	{"field":"title","value":"Adopt foldhash for the sparse sealed-directory fallback if reproduced"}	blake3:zYBHHG0iPwWy3sCsTjZZEw3vj6eFa5YATSgdhulQXNo
1784033360359990	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9gg		item.update	bn-dcr	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nSpike H measured foldhash at +53% median batch lookup throughput for real sealed-segment directories. A HashMap remains the sparse-region fallback even after bitrank integration, so the opportunity is independent—but the dependency and hasher choice still need correctness, threat-model, and composed evidence.\n\n# Goal\nAdopt foldhash for the sealed-directory u64-ID fallback only if the real-corpus win reproduces without changing exact lookup/iteration behavior or exposing an unacceptable collision-abuse surface.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Present/absent lookup and deterministic ascending iteration match the incumbent on real and synthetic sparse corpora.\n- [ ] Serialization remains hasher-independent and reopen/corruption behavior is unchanged.\n- [ ] Real-corpus benchmark reproduces the predeclared important-region win (or records DECLINE) with raw matched-order data.\n- [ ] Review confirms keys are internal interned u64 IDs, documents collision/DoS assumptions, and does not reuse the hasher for untrusted byte/string keys.\n- [ ] Dependency/version/features are minimal and full sealed-index tests pass.\n\n# Project Value\nCaptures the sparse fallback win without treating a one-line code change as evidence-free."}	blake3:EfSiiIszPZ8jqC7okOkzHunO4TO0qUpbuG1wb9RuYEA
1784033360382873	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9wg		item.update	bn-dbz	{"field":"title","value":"Eliminate SealPack whole-pack reopen residency"}	blake3:e81Xt8qgVl29Gr0mhpNXZugiLzScGYW_s-ksnrGSuNs
1784033360386213	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-Ag		item.update	bn-dbz	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nSpike J found that enabling SealPack increased reopen peak RSS by +18% at 2M events and +36% at 10M, eroding the bounded-memory win from removing Book. The likely cause is whole-pack materialization at open while legacy payload columns remained pread/cache-backed.\n\n# Goal\nOpen only directory and genuinely small mandatory sections eagerly; keep payload columns and other large optional sections file-backed behind the bounded cache, eliminating whole-pack residency while preserving exact reads and corruption isolation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Open path no longer requires a Vec containing the whole pack or an equivalent duplicate resident copy.\n- [ ] 2M/10M/100M reopen runs eliminate the measured growth and meet the predeclared Phase 8 RSS gate with raw peak/resident evidence.\n- [ ] Cold/warm payload/type/pointer reads, file opens, bytes read, cache residency/eviction, and p99 are measured against legacy and current pack paths.\n- [ ] Truncation/corruption of file-backed optional sections degrades locally and never invalidates already verified canonical raw bytes.\n- [ ] Reopen, concurrent reads, cache pressure, missing pack, fallback, verify, repair, and sanitizer tests pass.\n- [ ] No default-on flip occurs if memory is fixed by an unacceptable latency, syscall, or correctness regression.\n\n# Project Value\nKeeps SealPack's file/open/read wins without paying for sealed payload history in resident memory."}	blake3:oiI9zElIIea95SacmhpijW2UI76Vtk6t1SSs1s7C1Cs
1784033389973286	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx-Qg		item.link	bn-ozi5	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1mzi"}	blake3:J__bUJWlUc7xRtuB08RB-8gu-OmXDqOtT4uCRmKzOXc
