1784041212107715	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhQk		item.move	bn-187h	{"state":"done"}	blake3:xHRframDdbgbRvJk4hHLYW6ihZLSWoEYFQ0tIQlL_n8
1784041318545251	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhgk		item.update	bn-ogn	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe post-spike design pack contained stale authority claims. The landed source-traced audit now shows that LogEngine is already independent of MetaStore, FjallSnapshotBackend is the only live production Fjall consumer, snapshot discovery is discardable acceleration state, and MetaStore projection checkpoints and dedupe are dormant. There are no stores or users to migrate.\n\n# Goal\nFreeze the remaining implementation contract against current code and accepted measurements. Resolve documentation contradictions, record the no-migration scope, define opaque global-cursor semantics, and decide snapshot, projection-checkpoint, and dedupe behavior independently without inventing canonical controls merely to justify Fjall deletion.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Current code ownership and every Fjall keyspace/API are classified as canonical authority, rebuildable/discardable acceleration, dormant compatibility surface, or removable.\n- A reviewed ADR chooses the snapshot-discovery replacement and independently admits or declines projection checkpoints and exact dedupe.\n- Any canonical log transition states its global-position, retry, durability, recovery, and downgrade consequences; v4 remains a separate explicit product decision unless the graph is deliberately rewired.\n- State frontiers, fairness/cancellation, retention, and batch-stratified performance contracts are explicit.\n- Design and research manifests identify amendments and historical documents unambiguously.\n\n# Why\nThis prevents building replacements for dormant APIs, making accelerators authoritative, or treating a component implementation as completed Fjall integration.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after all five children are done, the authority audit and capability ADR have adversarial review, the design/research amendments cross-reference the chosen contracts, and all dependency changes pass triage validation with zero scheduling violations."}	blake3:fniZinApybZvUfszKaSLTXdEU1Kvka_fcXnFB01SSOs
1784041318657838	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExhwk		item.update	bn-k8qd	{"field":"title","value":"Decide snapshot, projection-checkpoint, and dedupe semantics"}	blake3:MisL8xGhf-eO-3ps2YqbDGMTN-d_CXWG05qaFBvooHY
1784041318672328	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiAk		item.update	bn-k8qd	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe landed authority audit disproved the premise that snapshot installs, projection checkpoints, and dedupe keys must all become log controls before Fjall can be removed. Snapshot discovery is live but discardable: losing its Fjall head causes a correct full replay. Projection checkpoint and dedupe MetaStore APIs have no production callers. Canonical controls remain possible product choices, not deletion prerequisites.\n\n# Goal\nProduce one bounded ADR that decides three capabilities independently: the replacement semantics for snapshot discovery; whether public projection checkpoints are admitted; and whether exact append dedupe is admitted. For each admitted capability, choose the smallest authority model that satisfies its user contract and name its v3/v4 encoding consequences. Declined capabilities must have an explicit API-removal or demotion path.\n\n# Decision Inputs\nCompare authority, publication order, crash fallback, global-position semantics, retry behavior, recovery cost, allocation-free validation, retention, compatibility, downgrade refusal, and operational observability. Preserve discardable semantics where they satisfy the contract; never promote an accelerator to authority by default.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A capability table classifies snapshot discovery, projection checkpoints, exact dedupe, and registry separately as canonical, discardable/rebuildable, dormant, admitted, or declined.\n- [ ] Snapshot discovery chooses either a self-describing pack plus discardable discovery metadata or a canonical install transition, with crash-before/after-publication behavior and full-replay fallback specified.\n- [ ] Projection checkpoints and exact dedupe each receive an ADMIT or DECLINE decision; admitted forms define identity, retry, retention, authorization, and recovery semantics.\n- [ ] Every canonical v3 control states its effect on visible global-position gaps and opaque cursors.\n- [ ] Pulling v4 forward is allowed only by an explicit ADR amendment that rewires or closes redundant Phase 9 work.\n- [ ] Downstream bones and dependencies are updated to match the decisions; no dormant capability remains a Fjall-deletion blocker.\n- [ ] Adversarial review covers torn writes, barriers, corrupt discovery state, duplicates, downgrade refusal, and recovery from canonical bytes.\n\n# Project Value\nSeparates required storage integration from optional product features and gives every later bone one implementable authority contract."}	blake3:qSm7z-k7vunhp7XzxN5QJD4_2ZXCn4PnOx6nHkt0rRs
1784041318773564	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiQk		item.update	bn-ozi5	{"field":"title","value":"Replace Fjall snapshot discovery with immutable self-describing packs"}	blake3:QToR46S-gpD-QE1Zd8u1ggZfUoqc5Gnhs9sYtiTFfGU
1784041318789571	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExigk		item.update	bn-ozi5	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nFjallSnapshotBackend writes snapshot blobs and stores the latest per-stream head in Fjall. The head is not event authority: if it is lost, snapshot lookup safely misses and aggregate loading performs a correct full replay. The replacement must preserve or deliberately strengthen that contract without assuming a canonical SnapshotInstalled record is necessary.\n\n# Goal\nReplace Fjall-backed snapshot discovery with an immutable, self-describing snapshot pack and the publication mechanism chosen by the Phase 3 ADR. Implement pack creation, validation, discovery, retention primitives, and a replacement backend/API while keeping missing or corrupt discardable metadata recoverable by safe fallback.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Pack bytes are immutable, bounded, hash-verified, and durable before any discovery state can publish them.\n- [ ] Optional state and event-prefix hashes have explicit presence encodings.\n- [ ] The selected discovery design is implemented exactly: either discardable manifest/current metadata with deterministic safe fallback, or a canonical log install transition if the ADR explicitly admits it.\n- [ ] No published head names a missing or unverified pack; orphan packs are safe to collect.\n- [ ] Loss/corruption/interruption/concurrent-save/reopen tests preserve aggregate correctness and never silently load stale or mismatched state.\n- [ ] The replacement backend itself has no Fjall dependency and exposes the integration hooks needed by applications and offline tooling.\n- [ ] Interleaved A/B measurements compare save/load p50/p99, forced-fallback latency, reopen time, file count, bytes written, disk amplification, and peak RSS against FjallSnapshotBackend.\n\n# Project Value\nBuilds the only live Fjall runtime replacement while retaining correct discardable-acceleration semantics and avoiding one-file-per-version scaling."}	blake3:i2ZfXlME92p05GQrTjaxmeXkZT35K3CqK8jJCg7J5D4
1784041318893844	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiwk		item.update	bn-11mk	{"field":"title","value":"Execute the projection-checkpoint ADMIT or DECLINE decision"}	blake3:JNkB7ip6gYZlRrFT-Q8dCEh-9jOIz_dRT11qaGVP4S0
1784041318906855	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjAk		item.update	bn-11mk	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nMetaStore exposes projection checkpoint positions, but the landed authority audit found no production caller. Projection checkpoints are therefore an optional product capability, not authority that must be migrated and not a blocker for Fjall deletion.\n\n# Goal\nExecute the Phase 3 ADR decision for projection checkpoints. If ADMIT, define and integrate a canonical checkpoint transition through the public API, owner durability spine, recovery fold, resident slots, effects, and kernel checkpoints. If DECLINE, remove or explicitly demote the dormant API and document the application-owned discardable checkpoint pattern instead.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The bone records the ADR branch taken and does not ship speculative implementation for a declined capability.\n- [ ] ADMIT: identifier, frontier, optional digest, monotonicity/rewind policy, authorization, global-position behavior, and retention are normative.\n- [ ] ADMIT: commit publication follows the event durability spine; recovery and checkpoint fallback reproduce exact latest values; position-zero creation is digest-visible.\n- [ ] ADMIT: conflict, stale digest, corruption, and Process/Os/Group behavior are tested and measured.\n- [ ] DECLINE: dormant MetaStore checkpoint APIs/callers are removed or clearly non-production, and the social projection sidecar remains explicitly discardable.\n- [ ] CLI, docs, and observability match the selected branch.\n\n# Project Value\nTurns checkpointing into a deliberate user-facing capability decision instead of inventing a replacement for unused storage."}	blake3:7-av6g3YYJnlf2TyGoYl8tDXOB5iIxynWTh73KRQ5eY
1784041319009549	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjQk		item.update	bn-fj34	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe authority audit proves LogEngine is already Fjall-free. Deletion becomes safe after the snapshot replacement is adopted by every live application and offline-tool surface. Projection checkpoints and dedupe are independent admitted-or-declined features and do not block removal of the storage engine.\n\n# Goal\nDelete MetaStore, FjallSnapshotBackend, and the Fjall dependency after end-to-end snapshot adoption. Remove or deliberately demote dormant/stale metadata APIs, migrate all operational surfaces, and prove that clean and reopened stores exercise no Fjall files, symbols, errors, or transitive dependency.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The authority-map checklist has a landed replacement or explicit removal decision for every table, API, caller, and compatibility surface.\n- [ ] mess-index::meta and FjallSnapshotBackend are deleted; any external compatibility artifact requires an explicit reviewed exception.\n- [ ] fjall disappears from Cargo manifests, Cargo.lock, and cargo-tree inversion; no meta directory or Fjall file is created.\n- [ ] Dead dedupe capacity, EngineError::Meta, rebuild-index --meta behavior, public exports, feature flags, docs, tests, and golden assumptions are removed or renamed coherently.\n- [ ] Doctor, inspect, retention, backup/restore, and corruption diagnostics use the self-describing snapshot replacement and have tested concurrent-open behavior.\n- [ ] Full workspace tests, snapshot law/fault suites, backup round trips, golden classification, and fresh/reopen benchmarks pass against recorded baselines.\n- [ ] Adversarial review traces every deleted state item to canonical bytes or proves it was discardable/dormant.\n\n# Project Value\nCompletes the one-authority architecture and removes the second storage engine from the full product, not merely from append."}	blake3:7Sjma4uMmIlL1LnOptl_Qqd4MmN8-hOLlF2naJ4uhJU
1784041319130952	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjgk		item.update	bn-35f2	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSpike D proved compact SegmentEffect construction, parallel build with sequential apply, and content-addressed incremental checkpoints. The authority audit corrected the Fjall premise: snapshot discovery is the only live Fjall-backed runtime role and is discardable; projection checkpoints and dedupe are independent product choices.\n\n# Goal\nIntegrate per-segment algebraic effects and Merkle-page kernel checkpoints into sealing/recovery, implement only the metadata capabilities admitted by Phase 3, replace snapshot discovery, adopt that replacement across applications and offline tooling, and remove Fjall end to end.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- Canonical effects cover admitted state only, with explicit bottom/existence, allocator boundaries, one shared Effect definition, and named publication frontiers.\n- Seal builds effects in parallel only where measured useful; final apply is ordered and identical to the reference fold.\n- Checkpoint pages/manifests/current pointers install atomically; interruption and corruption fall back to canonical log recovery.\n- Recovery selects checkpoint plus ordered suffix, validates anchors/digests, and meets startup/RSS gates without historical payload decode.\n- Snapshot discovery follows the Phase 3 decision and is adopted by the social example, public facade, CLI/doctor/inspect/retention, backup/restore, tests, and goldens before deletion.\n- Projection checkpoint and dedupe work follows independent ADMIT/DECLINE decisions and does not falsely block Fjall removal.\n- MetaStore and FjallSnapshotBackend are deleted and fjall is absent from the workspace dependency graph.\n- Formal, randomized differential, fault-filesystem, and interleaved benchmark suites show identical canonical state and no material regression.\n\n# Exit Gate\nClose manually only after effect/checkpoint corruption falls back to identical canonical state, the snapshot replacement is integrated through every product and offline surface, optional capabilities match their ADR decisions, bn-fj34 removes Fjall from code and dependency graphs, startup/RSS/save/load gates pass, and adversarial review traces every former state item to canonical bytes or proves it discardable/dormant."}	blake3:eLvbA3FXHdaJhEj6aVJZfL-ecJxxjvGefEN6jDahUR0
1784041321055774	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjwk		item.create	bn-3l8n	{"description":"# Context & Background\nReplacing FjallSnapshotBackend internally is not sufficient: the social example selects it by default, public exports and errors name it, and CLI doctor/inspect/retention, backup/restore, tests, goldens, and benchmarks encode its current layout and behavior. Fjall cannot be deleted until these consumers exercise the replacement.\n\n# Goal\nAdopt the snapshot-pack replacement end to end across applications, public APIs, operational/offline tooling, backup policy, fixtures, and observability. Make the replacement the measured default before the deletion bone begins.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Social Store types, examples, and benchmarks use the replacement backend by default with no Fjall path.\n- [ ] Public exports, errors, configuration, and feature flags use storage-neutral snapshot terminology and preserve documented save/load behavior.\n- [ ] Doctor, inspect, and retention read self-describing packs, report corrupt/missing discovery state accurately, and define/test concurrent-open behavior.\n- [ ] Backup/restore policy explicitly includes or deliberately excludes discardable packs/discovery metadata; both branches have restoration/fallback tests.\n- [ ] Golden fixtures are regenerated or classified with a reviewed compatibility statement; no stale meta-directory assumption remains.\n- [ ] Snapshot law, corruption/fault injection, interrupted install, concurrent save, reopen, retention, and aggregate full-replay fallback tests pass through the real public application path.\n- [ ] An interleaved baseline report compares Fjall and replacement save/load p50/p99, forced-fallback latency, reopen time, file count, bytes written, disk amplification, and peak RSS on identical datasets.\n- [ ] Adversarial review verifies integration call graphs and demonstrates that no production or offline consumer still instantiates FjallSnapshotBackend.\n\n# Project Value\nCloses the implementation-to-integration gap so the final deletion is mechanical and backed by product-level evidence.","kind":"task","labels":["asterism","snapshot","integration","risk:high","roadmap"],"parent":"bn-35f2","size":"m","title":"Adopt snapshot replacement across applications and offline tooling"}	blake3:PKhNlwEgbO4i8HLUWWptJG0rp29gQy6B6owGbK8xX_8
1784041331039536	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkAk		item.unlink	bn-fj34	{"target":"bn-11mk"}	blake3:T4Lt_hu_GWRWoqgKXGH9M5iEwNuzgbLHFSVNaPsmFC8
